CC.provideCourse.bind(null,"en")({"lessons":{"en-01":{"html":"<p>Before you begin the English I lessons, take this quick diagnostic check. Do <strong>not</strong> use a dictionary or translator—your honest score tells you which units need extra practice.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-part-a-grammar-check-10-items\"><h2 id=\"part-a-grammar-check-10-items\">Part A: Grammar check (10 items)</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-part-b-vocabulary-collocation-check-5-items\"><h2 id=\"part-b-vocabulary-collocation-check-5-items\">Part B: Vocabulary &amp; collocation check (5 items)</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-part-c-short-reading-check\"><h2 id=\"part-c-short-reading-check\">Part C: Short reading check</h2>\n<p>Read the 120-word passage and answer the questions below:</p>\n<blockquote><p>When first-year programming students start university in English, they often worry about accent or perfect grammar. In reality, technical clarity matters far more than sounding like a native speaker. A programmer who writes short, direct sentences in a bug report helps the whole team solve problems faster. Similarly, reading English documentation every day for just fifteen minutes builds vocabulary naturally. Instead of memorizing long lists of rare words, focus on verbs and nouns you actually meet in lectures: <em>compile, query, route, format,</em> and <em>debug</em>. Over one semester, consistent daily reading turns English from a barrier into your strongest study tool.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-how-to-interpret-your-score\"><h2 id=\"how-to-interpret-your-score\">How to interpret your score</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>14–17 / 17</strong>: Solid B1+/B2 foundation! Move smoothly through Units 1–5 and aim straight for <code>en-ready</code>.</li><li><strong>9–13 / 17</strong>: Good intermediate base—pay close attention to <strong>verb tenses</strong> (<code>en-02</code>, <code>en-07</code>, <code>en-08</code>) and <strong>articles/modals</strong> (<code>en-05</code>, <code>en-06</code>).</li><li><strong>0–8 / 17</strong>: Take each lesson slowly, read every 300-word passage aloud, and complete all gap-fill items twice.</li></ul>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"According to the passage, what matters most for first-year programming students?","opts":[{"h":"Having a native-speaker accent","why":"the passage says not to worry about accent"},{"h":"Writing and speaking with technical clarity","ok":1,"why":"short, direct sentences help teams solve problems faster"},{"h":"Memorizing rare dictionary words","why":"the passage advises against memorizing rare word lists"},{"h":"Avoiding English documentation","why":"the passage recommends 15 minutes of daily reading"}],"why":"Technical clarity and consistent reading matter far more than accent."},{"q":"What study habit does the author recommend?","opts":[{"h":"Translating every lecture word by word","why":"not mentioned"},{"h":"Reading English documentation for 15 minutes every day","ok":1,"why":"builds vocabulary naturally"},{"h":"Writing long, complex paragraphs in bug reports","why":"recommends short, direct sentences"},{"h":"Skipping grammar lessons completely","why":"recommends focusing on useful lecture vocabulary"}],"why":"Fifteen minutes of daily English reading builds practical vocabulary."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["While I "," (walk) to the lab yesterday, I met my C instructor."],"a":[["was walking"]],"note":"past continuous for background action"},{"t":["By the time the server crashed, we "," (already / save) the database."],"a":[["had already saved"]],"note":"past perfect for earlier past action"},{"t":["Could you tell me where "," (the library / be)?"],"a":[["the library is"]],"note":"indirect question uses statement word order"},{"t":["She has lived in Istanbul "," 2023."],"a":[["since"]],"note":"point in time takes <code>since</code>"},{"t":["We have been debugging this loop "," two hours!"],"a":[["for"]],"note":"duration takes <code>for</code>"},{"t":["You "," (not / have to) print the report; PDF upload is enough."],"a":[["don't have to","do not have to"]],"note":"lack of necessity"},{"t":["If you "," (press) <code>Ctrl+S</code>, the editor saves your file."],"a":[["press"]],"note":"zero conditional (present simple)"},{"t":["If I "," (have) more time tomorrow, I will review the SQL joins."],"a":[["have"]],"note":"first conditional"},{"t":["English "," (speak) in most international software teams."],"a":[["is spoken"]],"note":"present simple passive"},{"t":["That is the classmate "," laptop runs Linux."],"a":[["whose"]],"note":"possessive relative pronoun"}],[{"t":["Please "," attention to the semicolon at the end of each C statement."],"a":[["pay"]],"note":"collocation: <code>pay attention</code>"},{"t":["Remember to "," a backup before running <code>DROP TABLE</code>."],"a":[["make","take"]],"note":"collocation: <code>make/take a backup</code>"},{"t":["The new monitor looks much "," (bright) than the old one."],"a":[["brighter"]],"note":"comparative adjective"},{"t":["Can you "," me a favor and check my slide contrast?"],"a":[["do"]],"note":"collocation: <code>do someone a favor</code>"},{"t":["Her explanation was very ",", so everyone understood pointers."],"a":[["clear"]],"note":"clear explanation"}]],"id":"en-01","title":"English I — Diagnostic level check","goal":"assess your starting grammar, vocabulary, and reading level before you start the course","week":"1","minutes":20},"en-02":{"html":"<p>When you describe what happened in a lab, a project, or a story, you need four <strong>narrative tenses</strong> to show <strong>which action finished</strong>, <strong>which was in progress</strong>, and <strong>which happened earlier</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-the-four-narrative-tenses\"><h2 id=\"the-four-narrative-tenses\">The four narrative tenses</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tense</th><th>Form</th><th>Use</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Past Simple</strong></td><td><code>V2</code> / <code>did + V1</code></td><td>Completed main events in order</td><td><em>I saved the file and closed the laptop.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Past Continuous</strong></td><td><code>was/were + V-ing</code></td><td>Action in progress at a past moment (background)</td><td><em>I was testing my code when the power went out.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Past Perfect</strong></td><td><code>had + V3</code></td><td>Action that happened <strong>before</strong> another past event</td><td><em>When I returned, the OS had already restarted.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Past Perfect Continuous</strong></td><td><code>had been + V-ing</code></td><td>A <strong>longer</strong> action that continued up to an earlier past moment (often with <em>for / since</em>)</td><td><em>She <strong>had been working</strong> on the machine <strong>for</strong> ten years when she finally finished it.</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Past perfect vs. past perfect continuous: <em>He <strong>had written</strong> three books</em> (finished result, a number) vs. <em>He <strong>had been writing</strong> all night, so he was tired</em> (duration, the activity itself).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-gap-fill-15-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-gap-fill-15-items\">Practice: Gap-fill (15 items)</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-the-midnight-bug\"><h2 id=\"reading-the-midnight-bug\">Reading: The Midnight Bug</h2>\n<p>Read this original passage (~280 words) and answer the questions:</p>\n<blockquote><p>Last Tuesday, Emir stayed up late to finish his first C programming assignment. By 11:00 p.m., he had written fifty lines of code and felt proud of his progress. The program was supposed to read five exam scores from the user and print their average. While he was compiling the file, a single red warning appeared on his screen, yet the executable still ran. Confident that everything was fine, Emir typed five numbers and pressed Enter. Instead of printing <code>78.50</code>, the console printed <code>0.00</code>.</p>\n<p>At first, Emir thought the compiler had broken. He checked his <code>for</code> loop three times, drank a cup of tea, and stared at the <code>printf</code> line while his roommate was sleeping nearby. Nothing looked wrong—until he remembered what the lecturer had warned about during Monday's class. In <code>scanf(\"%d\", score)</code>, he had forgotten the <code>&amp;</code> address-of operator! Because he had omitted <code>&amp;</code>, <code>scanf</code> was trying to write into the wrong place in memory.</p>\n<p>Emir added the single <code>&amp;</code> symbol, saved the file, and compiled again. This time, the warning disappeared, and the console printed <code>78.50</code> cleanly. Before going to bed at 12:30 a.m., he uploaded his <code>.c</code> file to the course portal and made a backup copy on his cloud drive. He had lost an hour to one tiny symbol, but he knew he would never forget <code>&amp;</code> in <code>scanf</code> again.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write <strong>120–150 words</strong> about an <strong>unusual person from the past</strong> — a famous person (an inventor, explorer, artist) or someone from your own family. Say what was unusual about them and tell <strong>one event</strong> from their life. Use <strong>all four narrative tenses</strong> at least once.</p>\n<p>Story structure: <strong>1. Set the scene</strong> (who, where, when — past continuous for background) → <strong>2. The main events</strong> (past simple, in order) → <strong>3. The background before</strong> (past perfect / past perfect continuous) → <strong>4. The ending</strong> (what happened later and why the person is remembered).</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>My great-grandmother, Fatma, was an unusual woman for her time. In the 1950s, while most women in her village were working in the fields, she was repairing radios in a small shop in Bursa. Nobody had taught her; she had been taking broken radios apart since she was twelve, just to see how they worked. One winter evening, the town's only doctor arrived at her shop in a panic. His radio had stopped working, and he needed to hear the weather news before a dangerous trip over the mountains. Fatma opened the radio, found a burnt wire and fixed it in ten minutes. The doctor heard that a storm was coming and waited until morning. Later, he told everyone the story, and people started bringing her their radios from all the nearby villages.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Story self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Introduces the person and says what was unusual about them</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses past simple for the main events in order</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses past continuous for background (<em>while most women were working…</em>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses past perfect <strong>and</strong> past perfect continuous for earlier events</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Has a clear ending; 120–150 words</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using Past Simple for both actions when one clearly happened <strong>before</strong> the other (<code>When I arrived, the class already started</code> → <code>had already started</code>).</li><li>Using Past Continuous with state verbs like <code>know</code>, <code>want</code>, or <code>understand</code> (<code>I was knowing</code> → <code>I knew</code>).</li><li>Forgetting irregular <code>V2</code>/<code>V3</code> forms (<code>write → wrote → written</code>, <code>forget → forgot → forgotten</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>past perfect continuous</strong></td><td>had been + V-ing: a longer action continuing up to an earlier past moment</td><td lang=\"tr\">geçmişte süregelmiş eylem (past perfect continuous)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>narrative tenses</strong></td><td>past tenses (Simple, Continuous, Perfect) used together to tell a story</td><td lang=\"tr\">hikaye zamanları</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Past Continuous</strong></td><td><code>was/were + -ing</code>, showing an ongoing background action in the past</td><td lang=\"tr\">şimdiki zamanın hikayesi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Past Perfect</strong></td><td><code>had + V3</code>, showing an action completed before another past time</td><td lang=\"tr\">miş'li geçmiş zaman</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Why did Emir's program print <code>0.00</code> instead of <code>78.50</code>?","opts":[{"h":"His computer ran out of battery","why":"not mentioned"},{"h":"His roommate deleted a line of code","why":"his roommate was sleeping"},{"h":"He forgot the <code>&amp;</code> operator inside <code>scanf</code>","ok":1,"why":"<code>scanf</code> could not store the input properly without <code>&amp;</code>"},{"h":"He used <code>float</code> instead of <code>int</code>","why":"the bug was the missing <code>&amp;</code>"}],"why":"Forgetting <code>&amp;</code> in <code>scanf</code> caused the bug."},{"q":"Which event happened <strong>first</strong> chronologically?","opts":[{"h":"Emir drank a cup of tea","why":"happened after seeing <code>0.00</code>"},{"h":"The lecturer warned the class about <code>&amp;</code> on Monday","ok":1,"why":"happened before Tuesday night (Past Perfect: <code>had warned</code>)"},{"h":"Emir uploaded his file to the portal","why":"happened last at 12:30 a.m."},{"h":"The warning disappeared","why":"happened after fixing <code>&amp;</code>"}],"why":"<code>had warned</code> marks the earliest event in the timeline."}],[{"q":"Choose the best completion: <em>\"While I ___ to music, my friend knocked on the door.\"</em>","opts":[{"h":"listened","why":"<code>while</code> with a background ongoing action takes Past Continuous"},{"h":"was listening","ok":1,"why":"ongoing background action interrupted by <code>knocked</code>"},{"h":"had listened","why":"listening did not finish before the knock"},{"h":"listen","why":"present tense doesn't fit <code>knocked</code>"}],"why":"Use Past Continuous (<code>was listening</code>) for the longer background action."},{"q":"Choose the best completion: <em>\"When we got to the cinema, the film ___.\"</em>","opts":[{"h":"already started","why":"use Past Perfect to show it started <em>before</em> we arrived"},{"h":"had already started","ok":1,"why":"earlier past event before <code>got</code>"},{"h":"was already starting","why":"<code>already</code> with completed start before arrival fits Past Perfect"},{"h":"has already started","why":"Present Perfect cannot pair with <code>When we got</code>"}],"why":"<code>had + V3</code> shows the film started before our arrival."},{"q":"Which sentence is grammatically <strong>correct</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"I was knowing the answer during the quiz.","why":"<code>know</code> is a stative verb, not normally used in <code>-ing</code>"},{"h":"By 10 p.m., she had finished all five SQL queries.","ok":1,"why":"correct Past Perfect with <code>by + past time</code>"},{"h":"While he typed, the phone was ringing twice.","why":"short <code>twice</code> event should be Past Simple (<code>rang</code>)"},{"h":"Did you went to the lab yesterday?","why":"after <code>did</code>, use base verb <code>go</code>"}],"why":"<code>By 10 p.m., she had finished...</code> uses Past Perfect accurately."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["By 1843, Ada Lovelace "," (study) mathematics for many years."],"a":[["had been studying"]],"note":"long action up to a past moment"},{"t":["The scientist was exhausted because she "," (work) since dawn."],"a":[["had been working"]],"note":"duration → past perfect continuous"},{"t":["They "," (wait) for two hours when the ship finally arrived."],"a":[["had been waiting"]],"note":"duration with <em>for</em>"},{"t":["When he died, he "," (write) more than 40 books."],"a":[["had written"]],"note":"a finished number → past perfect simple"}],[{"t":["Yesterday, Deniz "," (arrive) at the computer lab ten minutes early."],"a":[["arrived"]],"note":"completed past event"},{"t":["While he "," (wait) for the projector to warm up, he checked his notes."],"a":[["was waiting"]],"note":"background action in progress"},{"t":["He realized he "," (leave) his USB drive in the dormitory!"],"a":[["had left"]],"note":"happened earlier, before arriving at the lab"},{"t":["Fortunately, he "," (upload) a copy to Google Drive the night before."],"a":[["had uploaded"]],"note":"earlier past action"},{"t":["As he "," (download) the file, the lab instructor walked in."],"a":[["was downloading"]],"note":"interrupted action"},{"t":["The instructor "," (ask) everyone to open Cisco Packet Tracer."],"a":[["asked"]],"note":"main event"},{"t":["Deniz "," (not / finish) his coffee before class started."],"a":[["didn't finish","did not finish"]],"note":"Past Simple negative"},{"t":["When I "," (call) Ece last night, she was studying for DBMS."],"a":[["called"]],"note":"short interrupting event"},{"t":["While we "," (discuss) the ER diagram, the library lights flickered."],"a":[["were discussing"]],"note":"ongoing background action"},{"t":["By the time the bus arrived, the rain "," (stop)."],"a":[["had stopped"]],"note":"completed before the bus arrived"},{"t":["What "," (you / do) at 9 p.m. yesterday?"],"a":[["were you doing"]],"note":"action in progress at a specific past time"},{"t":["I "," (never / see) a C pointer diagram before last week's lecture."],"a":[["had never seen"]],"note":"experience before a past point"},{"t":["Mert "," (type) the <code>SELECT</code> query, pressed Enter, and saw 24 rows."],"a":[["typed"]],"note":"sequence of completed actions"},{"t":["She couldn't log in because she "," (forget) her campus password."],"a":[["had forgotten"]],"note":"cause happening earlier"},{"t":["While the students "," (write) their exams, the room stayed silent."],"a":[["were writing"]],"note":"continuous background"}]],"id":"en-02","title":"Narrative tenses — telling a story about an unusual person","goal":"combine past simple, past continuous, past perfect and past perfect continuous to tell a clear story about a person from the past","week":"2","minutes":22,"terms":[{"t":"past perfect continuous","m":"had been + V-ing: a longer action continuing up to an earlier past moment","tr":"geçmişte süregelmiş eylem (past perfect continuous)"},{"t":"narrative tenses","m":"past tenses (Simple, Continuous, Perfect) used together to tell a story","tr":"hikaye zamanları"},{"t":"Past Continuous","m":"`was/were + -ing`, showing an ongoing background action in the past","tr":"şimdiki zamanın hikayesi"},{"t":"Past Perfect","m":"`had + V3`, showing an action completed before another past time","tr":"miş'li geçmiş zaman"}]},"en-03":{"html":"<p>In seminars and group projects you will often give your view on someone else's idea. English speakers rarely say just \"Yes\" or \"No\". They use <strong>softening phrases</strong> so that the conversation stays friendly, even when they disagree.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-three-kinds-of-response\"><h2 id=\"three-kinds-of-response\">Three kinds of response</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>You want to…</th><th>Useful phrases</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Agree</strong></td><td>I completely agree. · That's exactly what I think. · You're absolutely right. · Good point. · I couldn't agree more.</td><td><em>\"Group work is useful.\" — \"I couldn't agree more.\"</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Partially agree</strong></td><td>I agree up to a point, but… · That's true, but… · I see what you mean, but… · You have a point, however… · To some extent, yes.</td><td><em>\"Online classes are better.\" — \"I agree up to a point, but labs need to be in person.\"</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Disagree (politely)</strong></td><td>I'm not sure about that. · I'm afraid I don't agree. · I see it differently. · I take your point, but I think… · Actually, I'd say…</td><td><em>\"Exams are useless.\" — \"I'm not sure about that. They make us revise.\"</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Making disagreement softer</h3>\n<ul><li>Start with something positive: <strong>\"That's an interesting idea, but…\"</strong></li><li>Use <em>I think / I feel / in my opinion</em> to show it is your view, not a fact.</li><li>Avoid direct words like <em>\"You're wrong\"</em> or <em>\"That's stupid\"</em>. They sound rude in English.</li><li><strong>\"I couldn't agree more\"</strong> means you agree <strong>100%</strong> (not that you disagree). <em>\"I couldn't care less\"</em> means you don't care at all.</li></ul>\n<h3>Asking for others' opinions</h3>\n<p><em>What do you think? · Do you agree? · How do you feel about…? · What's your view on…?</em></p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example\"><h2 id=\"worked-example\">Worked example</h2>\n<p>Read this short discussion between three students about phones in class.</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Deniz:</strong> I think phones should be banned in lectures. <strong>Sara:</strong> I agree up to a point. They are distracting, but we sometimes use them to look up words. <strong>Ali:</strong> I'm afraid I don't agree with a total ban. We're adults; we can decide ourselves. <strong>Deniz:</strong> That's a fair point, but some people text the whole lesson. <strong>Sara:</strong> Exactly. Maybe the answer is a rule: phones on silent, only for class work. <strong>Ali:</strong> I couldn't agree more. That's a good compromise.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Notice: nobody says \"No.\" Each speaker <strong>reacts</strong> (agree / partly agree / disagree), <strong>gives a reason</strong>, and the group reaches a <strong>compromise</strong>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-choose-the-phrase-16-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-choose-the-phrase-16-items\">Practice: choose the phrase (16 items)</h2>\n<p>Type the missing word.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n<p>Now decide: does the speaker <strong>agree</strong>, <strong>partly</strong> agree or <strong>disagree</strong>? Type <em>agree</em>, <em>partly</em> or <em>disagree</em>.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-the-four-day-week-debate\"><h2 id=\"reading-the-four-day-week-debate\">Reading: The four-day week debate</h2>\n<blockquote><p>Last month our university's student council held an open debate. The question was simple: should students have classes on only four days a week? About sixty people came, and the discussion was lively but polite.</p>\n<p>The first speaker, a second-year engineering student, was strongly in favour. She argued that a free Friday would give students time for part-time jobs, sport and self-study. \"Many of us work in the evenings,\" she said. \"A fifth free day would reduce stress.\" Several people in the audience nodded.</p>\n<p>The second speaker agreed up to a point. He accepted that students are tired, but he worried about the timetable. \"If we put five days of lessons into four,\" he explained, \"some days will finish at eight in the evening. That is not less stress; it is just different stress.\" This comment made many people think again.</p>\n<p>A lecturer who was invited as a guest disagreed with the whole idea. She said politely that she understood the wish for more free time, but she believed that daily contact with teachers is important, especially in the first year. \"Learning a language or a programming skill needs small steps every day,\" she said.</p>\n<p>In the end, the council did not take a final decision. Instead, it suggested a test: one department would try the four-day week next term, and students would complete a survey at the end. Most people agreed that this was a sensible compromise, because it allowed the university to collect real evidence instead of only opinions.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write a short dialogue (6–8 lines) between two classmates about this topic: <strong>\"All university lectures should be recorded and put online.\"</strong> One person agrees, the other partly agrees, and they reach a compromise. Use at least <strong>four</strong> different phrases from the table.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p><strong>A:</strong> I think all lectures should be recorded. Then we can watch them again before exams. <strong>B:</strong> I agree up to a point. Recordings are useful, but some students might stop coming to class. <strong>A:</strong> That's a fair point. But people who are ill or working could still follow the course. <strong>B:</strong> True. I'm just not sure that a video can replace asking the teacher a question. <strong>A:</strong> I see what you mean. Maybe recordings should be available only after the lesson, as revision. <strong>B:</strong> I couldn't agree more. That's a good compromise.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Dialogue self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least one clear <strong>agree</strong> phrase and one <strong>partly agree</strong> phrase</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Every opinion has a <strong>reason</strong> (because…, then…, but…)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>No rude language (<em>You're wrong</em>, <em>That's stupid</em>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>The speakers reach a compromise at the end</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>\"I am agree.\"</strong> → <em>I agree.</em> (<em>agree</em> is a verb; no <em>am</em>.)</li><li><strong>\"I agree with this idea\" for a person</strong>: use <em>agree <strong>with</strong> someone/something</em>, but <em>agree <strong>to</strong> do</em> something (<em>He agreed to help</em>).</li><li>Thinking <strong>\"I couldn't agree more\"</strong> is negative. It means <em>I agree completely</em>.</li><li>Saying only <em>\"No\"</em> or <em>\"You are wrong\"</em>. In English this sounds rude; soften it.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>partial agreement</strong></td><td>agreeing with some of an idea but not all of it</td><td lang=\"tr\">kısmen katılma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>compromise</strong></td><td>a solution where both sides accept part of what they wanted</td><td lang=\"tr\">uzlaşma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>softener</strong></td><td>a phrase that makes disagreement more polite</td><td lang=\"tr\">yumuşatıcı ifade</td></tr><tr><td><strong>gist</strong></td><td>the general meaning of a text or conversation</td><td lang=\"tr\">ana fikir</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is the <strong>main idea</strong> (gist) of the text?","opts":[{"h":"Students want longer holidays","why":"holidays are not discussed"},{"h":"A polite debate about a four-day week ended with a plan to test the idea","ok":1,"why":"this covers the whole text"},{"h":"A lecturer cancelled Friday classes","why":"no classes were cancelled"},{"h":"Engineering students work too much","why":"only one detail, not the main idea"}],"why":"The gist is the general message of the whole text, not a single detail."},{"q":"How did the second speaker respond to the first speaker?","opts":[{"h":"He agreed completely","why":"he had a worry, so not completely"},{"h":"He partly agreed: students are tired, but long days would cause other stress","ok":1,"why":"\"agreed up to a point\""},{"h":"He refused to speak","why":"he gave a clear argument"},{"h":"He disagreed with everything","why":"he accepted that students are tired"}],"why":"\"Agreed up to a point\" means partial agreement."},{"q":"Why did the lecturer disagree?","opts":[{"h":"She wanted longer days","why":"not mentioned"},{"h":"She does not like students","why":"she was polite and understanding"},{"h":"She thinks daily contact and small daily steps help first-year learning","ok":1,"why":"stated in paragraph 4"},{"h":"She works on Fridays only","why":"not mentioned"}],"why":"Her reason: skills need \"small steps every day\"."},{"q":"What did the council decide?","opts":[{"h":"To start the four-day week for everyone","why":"no final decision was taken"},{"h":"To test the idea in one department and survey students","ok":1,"why":"stated in the last paragraph"},{"h":"To cancel the debate","why":"the debate happened"},{"h":"To ask the government","why":"not mentioned"}],"why":"A trial with a survey was the compromise."}],[{"q":"Which response shows <strong>partial</strong> agreement?","opts":[{"h":"I couldn't agree more.","why":"this is complete agreement"},{"h":"I agree up to a point, but it's too expensive.","ok":1,"why":"agrees with part, then adds \"but\""},{"h":"I'm afraid I don't agree.","why":"this is disagreement"},{"h":"Exactly!","why":"complete agreement"}],"why":"\"Up to a point\", \"to some extent\" and \"That's true, but…\" show partial agreement."},{"q":"Which sentence is grammatically correct?","opts":[{"h":"I am agree with you.","why":"<em>agree</em> is a verb; don't add <em>am</em>"},{"h":"I agreeing with you.","why":"missing a correct verb form"},{"h":"I agree with you.","ok":1,"why":"subject + verb + with + person"},{"h":"I am agree to you.","why":"two mistakes: <em>am</em> and <em>to</em>"}],"why":"Say <em>I agree</em> / <em>I don't agree</em> — never <em>I am agree</em>."},{"q":"What is the most <strong>polite</strong> way to disagree?","opts":[{"h":"You're wrong.","why":"too direct; sounds rude"},{"h":"That's a stupid idea.","why":"offensive"},{"h":"I see what you mean, but I see it differently.","ok":1,"why":"acknowledges the other view first"},{"h":"No.","why":"too short and blunt"}],"why":"Acknowledge the other person's view, then give yours."},{"q":"\"I couldn't agree more\" means:","opts":[{"h":"I disagree a little","why":"the opposite"},{"h":"I agree completely","ok":1,"why":"it is impossible to agree more"},{"h":"I can't decide","why":"not the meaning"},{"h":"I don't care","why":"that is \"I couldn't care less\""}],"why":"It is a strong way to agree."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["I completely "," with you. Group projects teach teamwork."],"a":[["agree"]],"note":""},{"t":["I couldn't agree ",". That's exactly my view."],"a":[["more"]],"note":""},{"t":["I agree up to a ",", but the deadline is too short."],"a":[["point"]],"note":""},{"t":["That's ",", but it isn't the whole story."],"a":[["true"]],"note":""},{"t":["I see what you ",", but I think the price is too high."],"a":[["mean"]],"note":""},{"t":["I'm "," I don't agree. Homework helps me practise."],"a":[["afraid"]],"note":""},{"t":["I'm not "," about that. Can you give an example?"],"a":[["sure"]],"note":""},{"t":["You have a ",". However, labs still need computers."],"a":[["point"]],"note":""},{"t":["To some ",", yes. But not for every subject."],"a":[["extent"]],"note":""},{"t":["I "," it differently. Online notes save paper."],"a":[["see"]],"note":""},{"t":["I take your ",", but I still think we need a break."],"a":[["point"]],"note":""},{"t":["What's your "," on studying at night?"],"a":[["view"]],"note":""},{"t":["How do you "," about group exams?"],"a":[["feel"]],"note":""},{"t":["You're absolutely ",". The Wi-Fi is too slow here."],"a":[["right"]],"note":""},{"t":["That's exactly what I ","."],"a":[["think"]],"note":""},{"t":["That's an "," idea, but I'm not sure it will work."],"a":[["interesting"]],"note":""}],[{"t":["\"I couldn't agree more.\" → ",""],"a":[["agree"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"That's true, but it's expensive.\" → ",""],"a":[["partly"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"I'm afraid I see it differently.\" → ",""],"a":[["disagree"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"Good point.\" → ",""],"a":[["agree"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-03","title":"Agreeing, disagreeing and partially agreeing","goal":"agree, disagree and partly agree politely in a discussion, using natural phrases","week":"3","minutes":18,"terms":[{"t":"partial agreement","m":"agreeing with some of an idea but not all of it","tr":"kısmen katılma"},{"t":"compromise","m":"a solution where both sides accept part of what they wanted","tr":"uzlaşma"},{"t":"softener","m":"a phrase that makes disagreement more polite","tr":"yumuşatıcı ifade"},{"t":"gist","m":"the general meaning of a text or conversation","tr":"ana fikir"}]},"en-04":{"html":"<p>In exams you often meet a long text and only a few minutes. Good readers do <strong>not</strong> read every word slowly the first time. They use two different kinds of reading (or listening):</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-gist-vs-detail\"><h2 id=\"gist-vs-detail\">Gist vs. detail</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><strong>Reading for gist</strong></th><th><strong>Reading for detail</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Question you ask</td><td><em>What is this text mainly about?</em></td><td><em>Exactly what / when / how many / why?</em></td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>fast, one quick read</td><td>slow, careful, only the part you need</td></tr><tr><td>What you look at</td><td>title, first and last sentences of each paragraph, repeated words</td><td>key words from the question, numbers, names</td></tr><tr><td>Typical exam task</td><td>\"Choose the best title\", \"What is the main idea?\"</td><td>\"According to the text, how many…?\", \"True / False / Not given\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>A simple method (works for listening too)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Before:</strong> read the questions first and underline key words.</li><li><strong>First read / listen — gist:</strong> do not stop for unknown words. Ask: <em>topic? writer's opinion?</em></li><li><strong>Second read / listen — detail:</strong> find the part of the text that matches each question. Look for <strong>synonyms</strong>: the question often uses different words from the text (<em>\"pets\"</em> in the question, <em>\"companion animals\"</em> in the text).</li><li><strong>Check:</strong> is the answer really stated, or are you guessing? If it is not in the text, it is <strong>Not given</strong>.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-vocabulary-talking-about-animals-and-attitudes\"><h2 id=\"vocabulary-talking-about-animals-and-attitudes\">Vocabulary: talking about animals and attitudes</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Word / phrase</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>wildlife</strong></td><td>animals living free in nature</td></tr><tr><td><strong>endangered</strong></td><td>in danger of disappearing completely</td></tr><tr><td><strong>habitat</strong></td><td>the natural home of an animal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>domestic / pet</strong></td><td>living with people</td></tr><tr><td><strong>stray</strong> (cat, dog)</td><td>without a home, living on the streets</td></tr><tr><td><strong>cruelty</strong></td><td>causing pain on purpose</td></tr><tr><td><strong>animal welfare</strong></td><td>the health and good treatment of animals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>to protect / to conserve</strong></td><td>to keep safe</td></tr><tr><td><strong>to be fond of</strong></td><td>to like a lot</td></tr><tr><td><strong>to treat (well / badly)</strong></td><td>to behave towards</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-vocabulary-gap-fill-15-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-vocabulary-gap-fill-15-items\">Practice: vocabulary gap-fill (15 items)</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-istanbul-s-street-cats-300-words\"><h2 id=\"reading-istanbul-s-street-cats-300-words\">Reading: Istanbul's street cats (≈300 words)</h2>\n<p>First read in <strong>60 seconds</strong> for the gist. Then answer the questions, reading again for detail.</p>\n<blockquote><p>Visitors to Istanbul are often surprised by the number of cats. They sleep on café chairs, sit in shop windows and wait politely outside butchers. Nobody knows the exact number, but some estimates suggest there are more than a hundred thousand street cats in the city.</p>\n<p>Unlike in many other countries, these animals are not usually seen as a problem. Most belong to nobody and to everybody at the same time. Shopkeepers leave out bowls of water, neighbours bring leftover food, and some local councils build small wooden houses to keep the cats warm in winter. In several districts, volunteers and vets work together to vaccinate and neuter the animals so that the population stays healthy and does not grow too fast.</p>\n<p>This attitude has a long history. Cats were welcome in Ottoman cities because they kept mice away from food stores and libraries. Many people also say that caring for animals is simply part of being a good neighbour. A popular documentary about the cats, released in 2016, made them famous around the world.</p>\n<p>However, not everyone is completely happy. Some residents complain about noise at night or about food left on the pavements. Bird lovers point out that cats hunt small birds. Animal welfare groups, on the other hand, worry most about cats that are injured by traffic or that do not get enough food during long, cold winters.</p>\n<p>Experts agree that the best approach is a balance: feeding stations in fixed places, regular vet programmes and education in schools. In this way, the city can keep one of its most loved traditions while reducing the problems.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-listening-style-practice-transcript\"><h2 id=\"listening-style-practice-transcript\">Listening-style practice (transcript)</h2>\n<p>In the exam you may hear a short talk. Here we give the <strong>transcript</strong>: cover it, ask a friend to read it aloud, or read it once quickly (gist) and then slowly (detail).</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>\"Good morning, everyone. Today I want to talk about zoos. Fifty years ago, most zoos kept animals in small cages, mainly so that visitors could look at them. Today the best zoos have three aims: conservation, education and research. For example, the snow leopard breeding programme in Europe now includes more than 200 animals. Critics still argue that large animals such as elephants should never live in zoos, because they need to walk many kilometres a day. So the question is not only 'Are zoos good or bad?' but 'Which animals can live well in a zoo?'\"</em></p></blockquote>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Check your answers</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Gist:</strong> how zoos have changed and the debate about which animals should live in them.</li><li><strong>Detail 1:</strong> the three aims of modern zoos are <em>conservation, education and research</em>.</li><li><strong>Detail 2:</strong> the snow leopard programme includes <em>more than 200</em> animals.</li><li><strong>Detail 3:</strong> critics say elephants should not live in zoos because they <em>need to walk many kilometres a day</em>.</li></ul></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write <strong>80–100 words</strong>: <em>\"How do people in your town treat animals? Give one good example and one problem.\"</em> Use at least <strong>four</strong> words from the vocabulary table.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>In my town, most people are fond of animals, especially the stray cats and dogs that live near the market. Shopkeepers often leave out water, and a local charity runs a small shelter where vets vaccinate homeless animals. This is a good example of animal welfare that comes from neighbours, not only from the government. However, there is also a problem. Some drivers go too fast in the narrow streets, and every month a few animals are injured. I believe the council should build more speed bumps to protect both animals and children.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Writing self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Clear topic sentence about attitudes in your town</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>One good example <strong>and</strong> one problem</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least four vocabulary items used correctly</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>80–100 words, short clear sentences</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Reading every word slowly the first time and running out of time.</li><li>Choosing an answer because it contains the <strong>same word</strong> as the text. Exam questions often use synonyms — check the meaning.</li><li>Mixing <strong>False</strong> (the text says the opposite) and <strong>Not given</strong> (the text says nothing about it).</li><li><em>\"The cats are very fond to people\"</em> → <em>fond <strong>of</strong></em>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>gist</strong></td><td>the general meaning of a text</td><td lang=\"tr\">ana fikir, genel anlam</td></tr><tr><td><strong>skimming</strong></td><td>reading quickly to get the gist</td><td lang=\"tr\">göz gezdirme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>scanning</strong></td><td>searching a text quickly for one specific piece of information</td><td lang=\"tr\">tarama (belirli bilgi arama)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>endangered</strong></td><td>in danger of disappearing completely</td><td lang=\"tr\">nesli tükenmekte olan</td></tr><tr><td><strong>animal welfare</strong></td><td>the good treatment and health of animals</td><td lang=\"tr\">hayvan refahı</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"<strong>Gist:</strong> Which is the best title for the text?","opts":[{"h":"How to Adopt a Cat in Turkey","why":"adoption is not discussed"},{"h":"Istanbul's Shared Street Cats: Tradition and Challenges","ok":1,"why":"covers care, history, problems and solutions"},{"h":"Why Cats Are Dangerous for Birds","why":"only one small detail"},{"h":"A Famous 2016 Documentary","why":"mentioned only once"}],"why":"A good title covers the whole text, not one paragraph."},{"q":"<strong>Detail:</strong> According to the text, why were cats welcome in Ottoman cities?","opts":[{"h":"They were expensive pets","why":"not stated"},{"h":"They kept mice away from food and libraries","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 3"},{"h":"They were used in films","why":"the film is from 2016"},{"h":"The government paid people to keep them","why":"not stated"}],"why":"Find the paragraph about history (paragraph 3)."},{"q":"<strong>Detail:</strong> What do volunteers and vets do together?","opts":[{"h":"Build roads","why":"not stated"},{"h":"Take cats to other countries","why":"not stated"},{"h":"Vaccinate and neuter the cats","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 2"},{"h":"Close cafés","why":"not stated"}],"why":"Look for the key words \"volunteers\" and \"vets\"."},{"q":"<strong>True, False or Not given:</strong> \"Most Istanbul residents want the cats removed from the streets.\"","opts":[{"h":"True","why":"the text says the cats are usually not seen as a problem"},{"h":"False","ok":1,"why":"the text says most people do not see them as a problem; only some residents complain"},{"h":"Not given","why":"the text does talk about people's attitude"}],"why":"\"Some residents complain\" is not the same as \"most residents\"."},{"q":"What does the writer think is the best solution?","opts":[{"h":"Removing all the cats","why":"not suggested"},{"h":"A balance of feeding stations, vet programmes and education","ok":1,"why":"last paragraph"},{"h":"Stopping all feeding","why":"not suggested"},{"h":"Only education in schools","why":"only one part of the solution"}],"why":"The writer's view is often in the last paragraph."}],[{"q":"You need to find <strong>the year</strong> a law was passed in a long article. You should:","opts":[{"h":"read every word from the beginning","why":"too slow"},{"h":"scan the text for numbers that look like years","ok":1,"why":"scanning finds one specific detail fast"},{"h":"read only the title","why":"the year is unlikely to be in the title"},{"h":"guess","why":"you can find it in the text"}],"why":"Scanning = looking quickly for a specific detail."},{"q":"The text says \"Some residents complain about noise.\" The statement is \"All residents love the cats.\" The answer is:","opts":[{"h":"True","why":"some residents complain"},{"h":"False","ok":1,"why":"the text contradicts \"all residents love\""},{"h":"Not given","why":"the text gives information about residents' feelings"},{"h":"Both true and false","why":"choose one"}],"why":"False = the text says the opposite."},{"q":"Which word means \"the natural home of an animal\"?","opts":[{"h":"shelter","why":"a place people build for homeless animals"},{"h":"habitat","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"welfare","why":"good treatment"},{"h":"wildlife","why":"the animals themselves"}],"why":"<em>Habitat</em> = natural home."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["Pandas are ","; there are fewer than 2,000 in the wild."],"a":[["endangered"]],"note":""},{"t":["Cutting down forests destroys the "," of many birds."],"a":[["habitat","habitats"]],"note":""},{"t":["Istanbul is famous for its "," cats that live in the streets."],"a":[["stray"]],"note":""},{"t":["My grandmother is very "," of her old dog."],"a":[["fond"]],"note":""},{"t":["Hitting an animal for fun is an act of ","."],"a":[["cruelty"]],"note":""},{"t":["The charity works for animal ",": food, shelter and vet care."],"a":[["welfare"]],"note":""},{"t":["National parks "," wildlife from hunters."],"a":[["protect","conserve"]],"note":""},{"t":["Cows and sheep are "," animals; wolves are wild."],"a":[["domestic","farm"]],"note":""},{"t":["We saw a lot of "," on our trip: deer, eagles and foxes."],"a":[["wildlife"]],"note":""},{"t":["Some people "," their pets like members of the family."],"a":[["treat"]],"note":""},{"t":["Reading quickly for the main idea is called reading for ","."],"a":[["gist"]],"note":""},{"t":["Reading carefully for numbers and names is reading for ","."],"a":[["detail","details"]],"note":""},{"t":["If the answer is not in the text, the answer is \"not ","\"."],"a":[["given"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"Companion animals\" is a "," for \"pets\"."],"a":[["synonym"]],"note":""},{"t":["Before you listen, read the "," first."],"a":[["questions","question"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-04","title":"Animals and us — reading and listening for gist and detail","goal":"read or listen to a text once for the main idea (gist) and again for specific details, and talk about human attitudes to animals","week":"4","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"gist","m":"the general meaning of a text","tr":"ana fikir, genel anlam"},{"t":"skimming","m":"reading quickly to get the gist","tr":"göz gezdirme"},{"t":"scanning","m":"searching a text quickly for one specific piece of information","tr":"tarama (belirli bilgi arama)"},{"t":"endangered","m":"in danger of disappearing completely","tr":"nesli tükenmekte olan"},{"t":"animal welfare","m":"the good treatment and health of animals","tr":"hayvan refahı"}]},"en-05":{"html":"<p>Two high-frequency grammar points in English I are <strong>sense verbs</strong> (<code>look</code>, <code>sound</code>, <code>feel</code>, <code>taste</code>, <code>smell</code>) and <strong>articles</strong> (<code>a</code>, <code>an</code>, <code>the</code>, or no article).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-verbs-of-the-senses\"><h2 id=\"verbs-of-the-senses\">Verbs of the senses</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>sense verb + adjective</code></td><td>Describes a quality</td><td><em>Your code <strong>looks clean</strong>.</em> / <em>That idea <strong>sounds great</strong>!</em></td></tr><tr><td><code>sense verb + like + noun</code></td><td>Compares to a noun</td><td><em>This fabric <strong>feels like</strong> silk.</em> / <em>It <strong>sounds like</strong> a fan problem.</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>(Remember: it is <strong>sound</strong> (by ear) vs. <strong>feel</strong> (by touch/emotion), and never put an adverb like <code>cleanly</code> after <code>look/smell/taste</code> when describing the subject!)</em></p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-articles-a-an-the\"><h2 id=\"articles-a-an-the\">Articles: <code>a / an / the / Ø</code></h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>When to use</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><code>a</code> / <code>an</code></strong></td><td>First mention of a singular countable noun (<code>a</code> before consonant <strong>sound</strong>, <code>an</code> before vowel <strong>sound</strong>)</td><td><strong><em>a</strong> university</em> (<code>/juː/</code> = consonant sound!), <strong><em>an</strong> hour</em> (silent <code>h</code>!), <strong><em>an</strong> SQL query</em> (<code>/ɛs/</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>the</code></strong></td><td>Specific/known item, second mention, unique things, superlatives</td><td><em>Open <strong>the</strong> file I emailed you.</em> / <strong><em>The</strong> sun</em> / <strong><em>the</strong> best</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Zero (<code>Ø</code>)</strong></td><td>General plural nouns, general uncountable nouns, most countries/languages/meals</td><td><strong><em>Computers</strong> use binary.</em> / <strong><em>Water</strong> is essential.</em> / <em>I study <strong>English</strong>.</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-gap-fill-15-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-gap-fill-15-items\">Practice: Gap-fill (15 items)</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-how-sensors-give-machines-senses\"><h2 id=\"reading-how-sensors-give-machines-senses\">Reading: How Sensors Give Machines \"Senses\"</h2>\n<p>Read this original passage (~260 words) and answer the questions:</p>\n<blockquote><p>Humans rely on five senses—sight, hearing, touch, taste, and smell—to understand the world. Today, engineers build electronic <strong>sensors</strong> that give computers and robots similar abilities. A smartphone camera acts like an eye, a microphone acts like an ear, and a touchscreen feels the tiny electrical charge of your finger.</p>\n<p>In modern factories and smart homes, sense-like sensors prevent costly accidents. Gas detectors can \"smell\" a dangerous leak long before a human nose notices anything unusual. When a machine's motor wears out, a vibration sensor detects that the motor no longer sounds smooth; instead, it sounds like metal scraping against metal, triggering an automatic maintenance alert. Even agricultural drones use special cameras to check whether crops look healthy or need water.</p>\n<p>Choosing the right sensor requires careful engineering. Just as a chef tastes soup to adjust the salt, a food-processing plant uses chemical sensors to test acidity and sweetness automatically. Without these devices, self-driving cars would be blind, and hospital monitors could not track a patient's heartbeat. Sensors turn physical feelings, sounds, and light into binary numbers that a C or Python program can read and act upon in milliseconds.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write a short description (80–100 words) of <strong>your favorite café, library, or study spot</strong>. Use <strong>three different sense verbs</strong> (at least one with <code>+ adjective</code> and one with <code>+ like + noun</code>) and check every <strong><code>a / an / the</code></strong> carefully.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>My favorite study spot is a quiet corner on the second floor of the university library. The room always smells like fresh paper and mild coffee from the hallway café. Soft afternoon light makes the wooden desks look warm and inviting, and the chairs feel comfortable even during a two-hour coding session. When the campus gets busy, the library still sounds peaceful—almost like a gentle rain outside. It is the best place to focus before an exam.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Writing self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses at least 3 sense verbs (<code>smells like</code>, <code>look warm</code>, <code>feel comfortable</code>, <code>sounds peaceful</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Includes both <code>sense + adjective</code> and <code>sense + like + noun</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Accurate use of <code>a / an / the / Ø</code></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>80–100 words</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing <code>an university</code> or <code>a hour</code>. Always choose <code>a</code> vs. <code>an</code> by <strong>sound</strong>, not spelling (<code>a university</code>, <code>an hour</code>, <code>an MP3</code>).</li><li>Saying <code>Your idea sounds like great</code> (<code>like</code> is ONLY for nouns; use <code>sounds great</code> for adjectives!).</li><li>Putting <code>the</code> before general plural or uncountable nouns (<code>The computers are useful</code> → <code>Computers are useful</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>sense verb</strong></td><td>verb describing perception (<code>look, sound, feel, taste, smell</code>)</td><td lang=\"tr\">duyu fiili</td></tr><tr><td><strong>definite article (<code>the</code>)</strong></td><td>article used for specific, known, or unique nouns</td><td lang=\"tr\">belirli tanımlık</td></tr><tr><td><strong>zero article (<code>Ø</code>)</strong></td><td>omitting <code>a/an/the</code> before general plurals or uncountable nouns</td><td lang=\"tr\">tanımlıksız kullanım</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"How does a vibration sensor help prevent machine damage in a factory?","opts":[{"h":"It smells gas leaks","why":"gas detectors smell leaks"},{"h":"It detects abnormal vibrations when a motor stops running smoothly and sends an alert","ok":1,"why":"catches wear before breakdown"},{"h":"It waters crops automatically","why":"drones check crop health"},{"h":"It replaces the factory's electricity","why":"unrelated"}],"why":"Vibration sensors detect unusual motion/noise early and trigger maintenance alerts."},{"q":"Why does the passage compare a camera to an eye and a microphone to an ear?","opts":[{"h":"Because cameras and microphones are made of biological cells","why":"they are electronic devices"},{"h":"Because electronic sensors convert light and sound into data, just as human senses gather information","ok":1,"why":"helps explain what sensors do"},{"h":"Because computers have emotions","why":"computers process binary numbers"},{"h":"Because humans cannot hear or see","why":"humans use both"}],"why":"Sensors perform the electronic equivalent of human sensory input."}],[{"q":"Choose the <strong>correct</strong> phrase:","opts":[{"h":"an university","why":"<code>university</code> starts with <code>/j/</code> consonant sound → <code>a university</code>"},{"h":"an honest answer","ok":1,"why":"<code>h</code> in <code>honest</code> is silent, so it starts with a vowel sound → <code>an</code>"},{"h":"a hour","why":"<code>h</code> in <code>hour</code> is silent → <code>an hour</code>"},{"h":"a MP3 file","why":"<code>M</code> is pronounced <code>/ɛm/</code> → <code>an MP3 file</code>"}],"why":"Choose <code>a</code> vs. <code>an</code> by the first <strong>sound</strong> of the word."},{"q":"Choose the <strong>correct</strong> sentence:","opts":[{"h":"That pizza smells like delicious!","why":"<code>delicious</code> is an adjective, so drop <code>like</code>"},{"h":"That cloud looks like a dragon!","ok":1,"why":"<code>a dragon</code> is a noun, so <code>looks like</code> is correct"},{"h":"He felt happily after passing the exam.","why":"use adjective <code>happy</code>, not adverb <code>happily</code>"},{"h":"This music sounds terribly.","why":"use adjective <code>terrible</code>"}],"why":"Use <code>sense verb + adjective</code>, or <code>sense verb + like + noun</code>."},{"q":"Choose the <strong>correct</strong> article: <em>\"___ coffee in this thermos is still hot, even though I normally don't drink ___ coffee.\"</em>","opts":[{"h":"Ø / the","why":"first is specific (<code>the coffee in this thermos</code>); second is general (<code>Ø</code>)"},{"h":"The / Ø","ok":1,"why":"specific thermos coffee (<code>The</code>); general uncountable coffee (<code>Ø</code>)"},{"h":"A / a","why":"<code>coffee</code> as a substance is uncountable here"},{"h":"The / the","why":"second mention is general habit, not specific"}],"why":"Specific known noun = <code>the</code>; uncountable noun in general = zero article (<code>Ø</code>)."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["That new keyboard "," (feel) really comfortable."],"a":[["feels"]],"note":"<code>feel + adjective</code>"},{"t":["Your laptop fan sounds "," a tiny airplane!"],"a":[["like"]],"note":"<code>sound like + noun</code>"},{"t":["This coffee tastes "," (bitter) without sugar."],"a":[["bitter"]],"note":"<code>taste + adjective</code>"},{"t":["She waited for "," hour outside the exam hall."],"a":[["an"]],"note":"<code>hour</code> starts with a vowel sound"},{"t":["Istanbul Gelisim is "," university in Avcilar."],"a":[["a"]],"note":"<code>university</code> starts with <code>/juː/</code> consonant sound"},{"t":["Please turn off "," projector when you leave Room 204."],"a":[["the"]],"note":"specific projector in Room 204"},{"t":[""," (Ø / The) programmers need patience when debugging."],"a":[["Ø"]],"note":"plural noun in general"},{"t":["I bought a mouse and a cable; "," mouse is wireless."],"a":[["the"]],"note":"second mention"},{"t":["Did you see "," moon last night?"],"a":[["the"]],"note":"unique object"},{"t":["He speaks "," (Ø / the) Turkish and English fluently."],"a":[["Ø"]],"note":"languages take zero article"},{"t":["Can you send me "," email with the PDF attached?"],"a":[["an"]],"note":"<code>email</code> starts with vowel sound"},{"t":["That song sounds "," (wonderful)!"],"a":[["wonderful"]],"note":"<code>sound + adjective</code>"},{"t":["My little brother looks "," our father."],"a":[["like"]],"note":"<code>look like + noun</code>"},{"t":[""," (Ø / The) honesty is important in teamwork."],"a":[["Ø"]],"note":"abstract uncountable noun in general"},{"t":["She is "," (a / the) fastest typist in our class."],"a":[["the"]],"note":"superlative takes <code>the</code>"}]],"id":"en-05","title":"Verbs of the senses and English articles (a, an, the, zero)","goal":"use sense verbs with adjectives/nouns correctly and choose between a, an, the, and zero article","week":"5","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"sense verb","m":"verb describing perception (`look, sound, feel, taste, smell`)","tr":"duyu fiili"},{"t":"definite article (`the`)","m":"article used for specific, known, or unique nouns","tr":"belirli tanımlık"},{"t":"zero article (`Ø`)","m":"omitting `a/an/the` before general plurals or uncountable nouns","tr":"tanımlıksız kullanım"}]},"en-06":{"html":"<p>Technical manuals, lab guides, and README files rely on <strong>imperatives</strong>, <strong>sequence linkers</strong>, and <strong>modals of obligation</strong> (<code>must</code>, <code>have to</code>, <code>should</code>).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-imperatives-sequence-linkers\"><h2 id=\"imperatives-sequence-linkers\">Imperatives &amp; sequence linkers</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Imperative</strong> = base verb (no subject): <em>Click Save.</em> / <em>Do not unplug the cable.</em></li><li><strong>Sequence</strong>: <em>First, ...</em> → <em>Next, ...</em> / <em>Then, ...</em> → <em>After that, ...</em> → <em>Finally, ...</em></li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-modals-of-obligation-prohibition-advice\"><h2 id=\"modals-of-obligation-prohibition-advice\">Modals of obligation, prohibition &amp; advice</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Modal</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><code>must</code> / <code>have to</code></strong></td><td>Obligation (required rule)</td><td><em>You <strong>must</strong> include a Primary Key.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>mustn't</code></strong></td><td><strong>Prohibition</strong> (forbidden—do NOT do it!)</td><td><em>You <strong>mustn't</strong> share your password.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>don't have to</code></strong></td><td><strong>No obligation</strong> (optional—you can if you want)</td><td><em>You <strong>don't have to</strong> print the code; PDF is fine.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>should</code> / <code>shouldn't</code></strong></td><td>Advice / recommendation</td><td><em>You <strong>should</strong> back up your project daily.</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-gap-fill-15-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-gap-fill-15-items\">Practice: Gap-fill (15 items)</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-setting-up-a-safe-home-wi-fi-router\"><h2 id=\"reading-setting-up-a-safe-home-wi-fi-router\">Reading: Setting Up a Safe Home Wi-Fi Router</h2>\n<p>Read this original passage (~255 words) and answer the questions:</p>\n<blockquote><p>Setting up a new home Wi-Fi router only takes ten minutes if you follow a clear order. First, connect the ISP modem cable to the router's <strong>WAN</strong> (or Internet) port and plug in the power adapter. Wait one minute until the power and internet lights stop blinking. Next, connect your laptop to the router with an Ethernet cable or select the default Wi-Fi name printed on the bottom sticker.</p>\n<p>Then, open a web browser and visit the router's admin address, usually <code>192.168.1.1</code> or <code>192.168.0.1</code>. You must change the default admin password immediately because hackers know factory passwords for every popular router brand. After that, open the Wireless Security menu and choose <strong>WPA2-AES</strong> or <strong>WPA3</strong>. You mustn't leave your network open or use old WEP encryption, which can be broken in minutes. Create a strong Wi-Fi password with at least twelve characters.</p>\n<p>You don't have to hide your Wi-Fi network name (SSID), because hiding it does not stop packet sniffers and only makes connecting your own phone harder. However, you should turn on automatic firmware updates so security patches install safely. Finally, click <strong>Save</strong> and wait for the router to reboot before testing a website.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-how-a-how-to-web-article-is-built\"><h2 id=\"how-a-how-to-web-article-is-built\">How a \"how-to\" web article is built</h2>\n<p>Websites are full of short articles such as <em>\"How to save money as a student\"</em>. They follow a clear pattern:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>A catchy title</strong> with <em>How to…</em> or a number: <em>7 Easy Ways to Spend Less on Food</em>.</li><li><strong>A short introduction</strong> (2–3 sentences) that shows the reader's problem: <em>Is your money gone by the 20th of every month? You're not alone.</em></li><li><strong>Numbered steps or tips</strong>, each with a <strong>bold mini-heading</strong> and 1–3 sentences. Use imperatives (<em>Make a list</em>), modals (<em>You should… / You don't have to…</em>) and a reason (<em>because…, so that…</em>).</li><li><strong>A friendly conclusion</strong> with one last piece of advice: <em>Start small — even 50 lira a week adds up.</em></li></ol>\n<p>Web articles speak directly to the reader (<em>you</em>), use short paragraphs and avoid long, complex sentences.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write a <strong>150–180 word</strong> web article: <strong>\"How to make your monthly student budget last.\"</strong> Include a catchy title, an introduction, at least <strong>four</strong> numbered tips with mini-headings, and a conclusion. Use <strong>imperatives</strong>, <strong>sequence linkers</strong> and at least <strong>three modals</strong> (<em>must, mustn't, should, don't have to, have to</em>).</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p><strong>5 Simple Ways to Make Your Student Budget Last All Month</strong></p>\n<p>Does your money disappear before the month ends? Don't worry — a few small habits can make a big difference.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Write a budget first.</strong> At the start of every month, write down your income and your fixed costs, such as rent and transport. You must know these numbers before you spend anything.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Cook at home.</strong> Eating out every day is expensive. You don't have to become a chef; simple meals like pasta or lentil soup are cheap and healthy.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Use student discounts.</strong> Always carry your student card. Many cinemas, museums and transport passes are cheaper for students.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Wait 24 hours before big purchases.</strong> You mustn't buy something expensive just because it is on sale. If you still want it the next day, then buy it.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Save something — even a little.</strong> Finally, put a small amount into savings as soon as you get your money. You should treat it like a bill.</p>\n<p>Start with one tip this week. Small changes add up!</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Web article self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Catchy title and a short introduction that names the reader's problem</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least four numbered tips with bold mini-headings</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Imperatives in each tip and at least three modals used correctly (<em>mustn't</em> = forbidden, <em>don't have to</em> = not necessary)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Speaks directly to the reader (<em>you</em>); short paragraphs</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Friendly conclusion; 150–180 words</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Confusing <strong><code>mustn't</code></strong> (strictly forbidden!) with <strong><code>don't have to</code></strong> (optional / not necessary!).</li><li>Using <code>must</code> for past obligation (<code>Yesterday I must study</code> → <code>Yesterday I had to study</code>).</li><li>Using <code>-ing</code> in instructions instead of base imperatives (<code>Clicking Save</code> → <code>Click Save</code>).</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>imperative</strong></td><td>base verb form used for commands and step-by-step instructions</td><td lang=\"tr\">emir kipi</td></tr><tr><td><strong>prohibition (<code>mustn't</code>)</strong></td><td>modal meaning something is forbidden</td><td lang=\"tr\">yasaklama</td></tr><tr><td><strong>lack of necessity (<code>don't have to</code>)</strong></td><td>modal meaning something is optional</td><td lang=\"tr\">gereksizlik</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Why <strong>must</strong> you change the router's default admin password immediately?","opts":[{"h":"Otherwise the router will not turn on","why":"it turns on with factory settings"},{"h":"Because factory default passwords for router brands are widely known to attackers","ok":1,"why":"easy target"},{"h":"Because <code>192.168.1.1</code> stops working","why":"the IP stays the same"},{"h":"Because WPA3 deletes passwords","why":"unrelated"}],"why":"Default admin passwords are public knowledge and must be changed right away."},{"q":"What does the author say about <strong>hiding your SSID</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"You mustn't show your SSID","why":"opposite"},{"h":"You don't have to hide it—it is optional and doesn't stop sniffers","ok":1,"why":"real security comes from WPA2/WPA3"},{"h":"You have to hide it to pass security checks","why":"not true"},{"h":"Hiding it speeds up Wi-Fi","why":"unrelated"}],"why":"Hiding the SSID is unnecessary (\"don't have to\") and ineffective as security."}],[{"q":"Choose the right modal: <em>\"Entry is free today, so you ___ buy a ticket.\"</em>","opts":[{"h":"mustn't","why":"<code>mustn't</code> means buying a ticket is forbidden!"},{"h":"don't have to","ok":1,"why":"buying a ticket is not necessary"},{"h":"shouldn't","why":"illogical"},{"h":"have to","why":"contradicts \"free\""}],"why":"Use <code>don't have to</code> when something is unnecessary."},{"q":"Choose the right modal: <em>\"In a C exam, you ___ use your phone; anyone caught using one fails.\"</em>","opts":[{"h":"don't have to","why":"<code>don't have to</code> means phones are optional!"},{"h":"mustn't","ok":1,"why":"strictly prohibited"},{"h":"shouldn't","why":"too weak for a strict exam ban"},{"h":"can","why":"opposite"}],"why":"Use <code>mustn't</code> for strict prohibition."},{"q":"Which sentence is a <strong>past</strong> obligation?","opts":[{"h":"Yesterday I must finish my lab.","why":"<code>must</code> has no past form"},{"h":"Yesterday I had to finish my lab.","ok":1,"why":"past of <code>must</code> / <code>have to</code> is <code>had to</code>"},{"h":"Yesterday I have to finish my lab.","why":"present form doesn't match <code>Yesterday</code>"},{"h":"Yesterday I musted finish my lab.","why":"<code>musted</code> does not exist"}],"why":"Always use <code>had to</code> for past obligation."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":[""," (First / Finally), plug the Ethernet cable into the router's WAN port."],"a":[["First"]],"note":"opening step"},{"t":[""," (Do / Don't) forget to add <code>return 0;</code> at the end of <code>main</code>."],"a":[["Don't","Do not"]],"note":"negative imperative"},{"t":["Students "," (must / don't have to) bring their ID card to enter the exam hall; it is mandatory."],"a":[["must"]],"note":"strict requirement"},{"t":["You "," (mustn't / don't have to) buy the textbook; free PDF notes are provided."],"a":[["don't have to"]],"note":"optional, not forbidden"},{"t":["You "," (mustn't / don't have to) copy someone else's code; plagiarism is strictly banned."],"a":[["mustn't"]],"note":"prohibition"},{"t":["In my opinion, you "," (should / mustn't) test every SQL query before submitting."],"a":[["should"]],"note":"good advice"},{"t":[""," (Then / First), open your browser and type <code>192.168.1.1</code>."],"a":[["Then"]],"note":"middle step"},{"t":[""," (Click / Clicking) \"Save Settings\" to apply the new Wi-Fi password."],"a":[["Click"]],"note":"imperative uses base verb"},{"t":["Yesterday, I "," (have to / had to) reset my router twice."],"a":[["had to"]],"note":"past obligation uses <code>had to</code> (not <code>must</code>)"},{"t":["You "," (shouldn't / don't have to) leave <code>123456</code> as your Wi-Fi password—it's unsafe!"],"a":[["shouldn't"]],"note":"strong advice against"},{"t":[""," (Make / Making) sure both switch ports turn green."],"a":[["Make"]],"note":"imperative"},{"t":["Does she "," (has to / have to) attend Friday's optional workshop?"],"a":[["have to"]],"note":"after <code>does</code>, use base <code>have to</code>"},{"t":[""," (Finally / Next), restart the device and test your connection."],"a":[["Finally"]],"note":"last step"},{"t":["Visitors "," (mustn't / shouldn't) enter the server room without permission."],"a":[["mustn't"]],"note":"strict rule/prohibition"},{"t":["We "," (had to / must) wait 30 minutes at the help desk yesterday."],"a":[["had to"]],"note":"past obligation"}]],"id":"en-06","title":"Money and spending — writing a how-to web article","goal":"write a short how-to web article about money with a catchy title, imperatives, sequence linkers and modals such as must, mustn't, don't have to and should","week":"6","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"imperative","m":"base verb form used for commands and step-by-step instructions","tr":"emir kipi"},{"t":"prohibition (`mustn't`)","m":"modal meaning something is forbidden","tr":"yasaklama"},{"t":"lack of necessity (`don't have to`)","m":"modal meaning something is optional","tr":"gereksizlik"}]},"en-07":{"html":"<p>How will we pay for things in ten years? Will cash disappear? Talking about <strong>trends</strong> means talking about the <strong>future</strong> and giving <strong>opinions</strong>. This lesson covers both.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-five-ways-to-talk-about-the-future\"><h2 id=\"five-ways-to-talk-about-the-future\">Five ways to talk about the future</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Structure</th><th>Use it for</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>will</strong></td><td>will + verb</td><td>predictions you <em>believe</em>; quick decisions; promises</td><td><em>I think cash <strong>will disappear</strong>.</em> · <em>I'<strong>ll pay</strong>, don't worry.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>going to</strong></td><td>am/is/are going to + verb</td><td>plans already decided; predictions from <strong>evidence you can see now</strong></td><td><em>I'<strong>m going to open</strong> a savings account.</em> · <em>Look at the queue — we'<strong>re going to wait</strong> a long time.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>present continuous</strong></td><td>am/is/are + verb-ing</td><td>fixed arrangements with a time/place (in the diary)</td><td><em>I'<strong>m meeting</strong> the bank adviser on Friday at 3.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>future continuous</strong></td><td>will be + verb-ing</td><td>an action <strong>in progress</strong> at a future moment</td><td><em>This time next year I'<strong>ll be working</strong> part-time.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>future perfect</strong></td><td>will have + past participle</td><td>an action <strong>finished before</strong> a future moment</td><td><em>By 2030, most shops <strong>will have stopped</strong> taking cash.</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Time markers help: <em>by</em> (+ future perfect), <em>this time next…</em> / <em>at 8 p.m. tomorrow</em> (+ future continuous), <em>on Friday at 3</em> (+ present continuous for arrangements).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-giving-and-responding-to-opinions\"><h2 id=\"giving-and-responding-to-opinions\">Giving and responding to opinions</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Giving</th><th>Responding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>In my opinion, …</td><td>I see your point, but…</td></tr><tr><td>I strongly believe that…</td><td>That's a good point.</td></tr><tr><td>As far as I'm concerned, …</td><td>I'm not convinced.</td></tr><tr><td>It seems to me that…</td><td>Do you really think so?</td></tr><tr><td>I'd say that…</td><td>I'd go along with that.</td></tr><tr><td>If you ask me, …</td><td>I'm in two minds about it. <em>(= not sure)</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example\"><h2 id=\"worked-example\">Worked example</h2>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Mert:</strong> If you ask me, in ten years nobody <strong>will be carrying</strong> cash. We <strong>'ll</strong> all <strong>be paying</strong> with phones. <strong>Elif:</strong> I'm not convinced. My grandparents <strong>are going to keep</strong> using cash — they don't trust apps. <strong>Mert:</strong> That's a good point. But by then, banks <strong>will have closed</strong> most of their branches. <strong>Elif:</strong> Maybe. Anyway, I'<strong>m opening</strong> a digital savings account on Monday — the appointment is at ten.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Notice each choice: <em>will be carrying</em> (in progress at a future time), <em>are going to keep</em> (intention), <em>will have closed</em> (finished before a future time), <em>I'm opening … on Monday</em> (fixed arrangement).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-choose-the-future-form-18-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-choose-the-future-form-18-items\">Practice: choose the future form (18 items)</h2>\n<p>Type the verb in the correct form (use contractions or full forms — both are accepted where shown).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-where-is-our-money-going-290-words\"><h2 id=\"reading-where-is-our-money-going-290-words\">Reading: Where is our money going? (≈290 words)</h2>\n<blockquote><p>Ten years ago, most people in Türkiye paid for their bread with coins. Today, many young people do not carry a wallet at all. A phone, a watch or a card is enough. This is one of the biggest money trends of our time: the move to <strong>contactless and mobile payment</strong>.</p>\n<p>Experts expect this trend to continue. Many believe that by the end of this decade, small shops <strong>will have replaced</strong> their old card machines with QR codes and phone payments. Some economists go further and predict that a few countries <strong>will be using</strong> digital versions of their national currency within ten years.</p>\n<p>A second trend is <strong>\"buy now, pay later\"</strong>. Online shops let customers split a payment into three or four parts without interest. Supporters say this helps students buy useful things, such as a laptop for their studies. Critics are not convinced. In their opinion, it makes spending feel too easy, and some young people <strong>are going to find</strong> themselves with several small debts that add up to a large one.</p>\n<p>A third trend is more positive: interest in <strong>saving and investing</strong> is growing among people in their twenties. Free apps show where your money goes each month, and short videos explain basic ideas like budgets and emergency funds.</p>\n<p>So what should a student do? Most financial advisers give similar advice. First, make a monthly budget and check it every week. Second, be careful with instalments: add up the total you will pay. Third, try to save a small amount regularly, even if it is only a few hundred lira. As one adviser put it, \"Your future self <strong>will be thanking</strong> you.\"</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write <strong>100–120 words</strong>: <em>\"Will cash disappear in Türkiye in the next 15 years?\"</em> Use at least <strong>three different future forms</strong> and <strong>two opinion phrases</strong>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>In my opinion, cash will not disappear completely, but it will become much less common. By 2040, most young people will have stopped carrying coins, because phones are faster and safer. This time next decade, we will probably be paying for buses and coffee with our watches. However, I'm not convinced that everyone will change. Many older people and small village shops are going to keep using cash, because they trust what they can hold. As far as I'm concerned, the government should make sure that people who don't use smartphones can still pay easily. So cash is going to become rare, but not extinct.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Writing self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Clear opinion in the first sentence</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least three future forms (e.g. <em>will</em>, <em>going to</em>, <em>will be -ing</em>, <em>will have + V3</em>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least two opinion phrases (<em>In my opinion</em>, <em>As far as I'm concerned</em>…)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>A reason or example for each idea; 100–120 words</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>\"I will meet my friend tomorrow at 5\"</strong> for a fixed plan is not wrong, but native speakers usually say <em>I'm meeting</em>.</li><li><strong>\"By 2030 I will finish university\"</strong> → <em>I <strong>will have finished</strong></em> (with <em>by</em> + future time).</li><li>Using <em>will</em> after <em>when/if</em> for the future: <em>When I <strong>will</strong> get a job…</em> → <em>When I <strong>get</strong> a job…</em></li><li><em>\"In my opinion, I think…\"</em> repeats the same idea. Use one of them.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>trend</strong></td><td>a general direction in which something is changing</td><td lang=\"tr\">eğilim, trend</td></tr><tr><td><strong>future perfect</strong></td><td>will have + past participle: finished before a future time</td><td lang=\"tr\">gelecek zamanda bitmiş eylem (future perfect)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>future continuous</strong></td><td>will be + -ing: in progress at a future time</td><td lang=\"tr\">gelecekte devam eden eylem</td></tr><tr><td><strong>budget</strong></td><td>a plan of how much money you will spend</td><td lang=\"tr\">bütçe</td></tr><tr><td><strong>instalment</strong></td><td>one of several payments you make to pay for something</td><td lang=\"tr\">taksit</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is the <strong>gist</strong> of the text?","opts":[{"h":"Cash is illegal in Türkiye","why":"not stated"},{"h":"Three current money trends and some advice for students","ok":1,"why":"covers the whole text"},{"h":"How to open a bank account","why":"not discussed"},{"h":"The history of coins","why":"only mentioned briefly"}],"why":"Look at the topic of each paragraph."},{"q":"What do critics think about \"buy now, pay later\"?","opts":[{"h":"It is the best way to save","why":"opposite"},{"h":"It makes spending too easy and can create several small debts","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 3"},{"h":"It is only for old people","why":"not stated"},{"h":"It has very high interest","why":"the text says \"without interest\""}],"why":"Critics' opinion is in paragraph 3."},{"q":"\"By the end of this decade, small shops <strong>will have replaced</strong> their old card machines.\" The future perfect shows that:","opts":[{"h":"the replacing will be in progress at the end of the decade","why":"that would be future continuous"},{"h":"the replacing will be finished before the end of the decade","ok":1,"why":"correct use of future perfect"},{"h":"it is a sudden decision","why":"that's <em>will</em> + verb"},{"h":"it is an arrangement with a time","why":"that's present continuous"}],"why":"Future perfect = completed before a future point."},{"q":"Which piece of advice is <strong>not</strong> in the text?","opts":[{"h":"Make a monthly budget","why":"it is in the text"},{"h":"Add up the total of instalments","why":"it is in the text"},{"h":"Never use a bank card","ok":1,"why":"not stated"},{"h":"Save a small amount regularly","why":"it is in the text"}],"why":"Read the last paragraph for detail."}],[{"q":"\"At 10 a.m. tomorrow I ___ in the library, so text me instead of calling.\"","opts":[{"h":"will study","why":"simple <em>will</em> doesn't show \"in progress at 10 a.m.\""},{"h":"will be studying","ok":1,"why":"action in progress at a future moment"},{"h":"will have studied","why":"that means finished before 10 a.m."},{"h":"study","why":"present simple doesn't fit here"}],"why":"Future continuous for an action in progress at a future time."},{"q":"\"I've already bought the tickets. We ___ to Antalya in July.\"","opts":[{"h":"will go","why":"a decision made before speaking takes <em>going to</em> or present continuous"},{"h":"are going","ok":1,"why":"a fixed arrangement (tickets bought)"},{"h":"will have gone","why":"not \"finished before\""},{"h":"go","why":"incomplete"}],"why":"Arrangements → present continuous (or <em>going to</em>)."},{"q":"Which is an <strong>opinion</strong> phrase?","opts":[{"h":"by the time","why":"a time expression"},{"h":"as far as I'm concerned","ok":1,"why":"introduces your view"},{"h":"this time next year","why":"a time expression"},{"h":"in two weeks","why":"a time expression"}],"why":"Opinion phrases introduce what <em>you</em> think."},{"q":"\"When I ___ my degree, I'm going to look for a job abroad.\"","opts":[{"h":"will finish","why":"no <em>will</em> after <em>when</em> for the future"},{"h":"finish","ok":1,"why":"present simple after <em>when</em> with a future meaning"},{"h":"will have finished","why":"no <em>will</em> after <em>when</em>"},{"h":"am going to finish","why":"not used after <em>when</em> here"}],"why":"Time clauses (<em>when, after, as soon as</em>) use the present for the future."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["Look at those dark clouds. It "," (rain)."],"a":[["is going to rain","'s going to rain"]],"note":""},{"t":["I "," (meet) my tutor at 2 p.m. tomorrow. It's in my calendar."],"a":[["am meeting","'m meeting"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"The phone is ringing.\" \"I "," (answer) it!\""],"a":[["will answer","'ll answer"]],"note":""},{"t":["This time next week, I "," (sit) my maths exam."],"a":[["will be sitting","'ll be sitting"]],"note":""},{"t":["By the end of the year, I "," (save) 5,000 lira."],"a":[["will have saved","'ll have saved"]],"note":""},{"t":["I think online shopping "," (grow) even more."],"a":[["will grow","'ll grow"]],"note":""},{"t":["We've decided. We "," (buy) a second-hand laptop."],"a":[["are going to buy","'re going to buy"]],"note":""},{"t":["By 2035, many banks "," (close) their branches."],"a":[["will have closed","'ll have closed"]],"note":""},{"t":["At 9 p.m. tonight I "," (watch) the match, so don't call."],"a":[["will be watching","'ll be watching"]],"note":""},{"t":["My sister "," (fly) to Izmir on Saturday; she has the ticket."],"a":[["is flying","'s flying"]],"note":""},{"t":["Don't worry, I promise I "," (pay) you back next week."],"a":[["will pay","'ll pay"]],"note":""},{"t":["He hasn't studied at all. He "," (fail) the test."],"a":[["is going to fail","'s going to fail"]],"note":""},{"t":["By the time you arrive, we "," (finish) dinner."],"a":[["will have finished","'ll have finished"]],"note":""},{"t":["In 2040, people "," probably "," (use) cash at all."],"a":[["won't"],["use"]],"note":""},{"t":["I "," (start) a part-time job next month — I signed the contract yesterday."],"a":[["am starting","'m starting","am going to start"]],"note":""},{"t":["In my ",", prices will keep rising."],"a":[["opinion"]],"note":""},{"t":["As far as I'm ",", saving is more important than spending."],"a":[["concerned"]],"note":""},{"t":["I'm in two "," about buying a new phone."],"a":[["minds"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-07","title":"Money trends — opinions and future forms","goal":"give and respond to opinions about money trends and choose between will, going to, present continuous, future continuous and future perfect","week":"8","minutes":22,"terms":[{"t":"trend","m":"a general direction in which something is changing","tr":"eğilim, trend"},{"t":"future perfect","m":"will have + past participle: finished before a future time","tr":"gelecek zamanda bitmiş eylem (future perfect)"},{"t":"future continuous","m":"will be + -ing: in progress at a future time","tr":"gelecekte devam eden eylem"},{"t":"budget","m":"a plan of how much money you will spend","tr":"bütçe"},{"t":"instalment","m":"one of several payments you make to pay for something","tr":"taksit"}]},"en-08":{"html":"<p>Film reviews pack a lot of information into each sentence: who the characters are, where the story happens and what the reviewer thinks. <strong>Relative clauses</strong> make this possible. This lesson revises them and adds a more formal pattern: a <strong>preposition + which/whom</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-review-relative-pronouns\"><h2 id=\"quick-review-relative-pronouns\">Quick review: relative pronouns</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>who</strong> — people · <strong>which</strong> — things · <strong>that</strong> — people or things (defining clauses only) · <strong>where</strong> — places · <strong>whose</strong> — possession · <strong>when</strong> — times</li><li><strong>Defining</strong> clauses (no commas) tell us <em>which one</em>: <em>The actor <strong>who plays the father</strong> is excellent.</em></li><li><strong>Non-defining</strong> clauses (with commas) add extra information: <em>The film, <strong>which lasts three hours</strong>, never feels long.</em> Never use <em>that</em> after a comma.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-relative-clauses-with-prepositions\"><h2 id=\"relative-clauses-with-prepositions\">Relative clauses with prepositions</h2>\n<p>Many verbs and adjectives go with a preposition: <em>live <strong>in</strong></em>, <em>talk <strong>about</strong></em>, <em>fall in love <strong>with</strong></em>, <em>be famous <strong>for</strong></em>, <em>depend <strong>on</strong></em>. When this verb is inside a relative clause, the preposition can go in <strong>two places</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Formal (written reviews, essays)</th><th>Informal (speaking)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The town <strong>in which</strong> the story takes place is tiny.</td><td>The town <strong>which/that</strong> the story takes place <strong>in</strong> is tiny.</td></tr><tr><td>The woman <strong>with whom</strong> he falls in love is a spy.</td><td>The woman <strong>who</strong> he falls in love <strong>with</strong> is a spy.</td></tr><tr><td>The prize <strong>for which</strong> the film was nominated…</td><td>The prize <strong>that</strong> the film was nominated <strong>for</strong>…</td></tr><tr><td>The director, <strong>about whom</strong> little is known, …</td><td><em>(non-defining: use the formal form in writing)</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rules:</p>\n<ol><li>After a preposition use <strong>which</strong> (things) or <strong>whom</strong> (people). <strong>Never</strong> <em>in that</em> or <em>with who</em>.</li><li><em>in which / at which</em> can often be replaced by <strong>where</strong> for places: <em>the town where the story takes place</em>.</li><li><em>in which / on which</em> can be replaced by <strong>when</strong> for times: <em>the year in which / when the war ended</em>.</li><li>Quantity words: <em>several characters, <strong>most of whom</strong> are teenagers</em> · <em>five songs, <strong>all of which</strong> are original</em>.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example-from-two-sentences-to-one\"><h2 id=\"worked-example-from-two-sentences-to-one\">Worked example — from two sentences to one</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Two sentences</th><th>One sentence (formal)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The film is set in a village. The village has no electricity.</td><td>The village <strong>in which</strong> the film is set has no electricity.</td></tr><tr><td>She works with a detective. The detective is new to the city.</td><td>The detective <strong>with whom</strong> she works is new to the city.</td></tr><tr><td>The film has eight characters. Most of them are strangers.</td><td>The film has eight characters, <strong>most of whom</strong> are strangers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-16-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-16-items\">Practice (16 items)</h2>\n<p>Complete with <strong>which</strong>, <strong>whom</strong>, <strong>where</strong>, <strong>when</strong> or a <strong>preposition</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-a-film-review-300-words\"><h2 id=\"reading-a-film-review-300-words\">Reading: a film review (≈300 words)</h2>\n<blockquote><p><strong>\"The Last Signal\" — ★★★★☆</strong></p>\n<p><em>The Last Signal</em>, which opened in cinemas across Türkiye last week, is a quiet but powerful science-mystery film. It tells the story of Leyla, a radio engineer <strong>with whom</strong> the audience immediately sympathises. She lives alone in a mountain station <strong>in which</strong> old equipment is used to listen for signals from space.</p>\n<p>One winter night, Leyla receives a short message in a language that nobody can identify. The scientists <strong>to whom</strong> she sends the recording first think it is a joke. Only her former teacher, Professor Aydın, <strong>whose</strong> career was destroyed by a similar discovery thirty years earlier, takes her seriously.</p>\n<p>The film's greatest strength is its atmosphere. The station, <strong>where</strong> most of the action happens, feels both cosy and frightening. The director uses long, silent shots <strong>during which</strong> we hear only the wind and the static of the radio. Some viewers may find these scenes slow, but they create real tension.</p>\n<p>The acting is excellent. Deniz Kaya, <strong>for whom</strong> this is a first leading role, gives Leyla a quiet strength. The supporting cast, <strong>most of whom</strong> appear in only one or two scenes, are also convincing.</p>\n<p>The film is not perfect. The final twenty minutes, <strong>in which</strong> the mystery is explained, rely too much on dialogue, and the ending will divide audiences. However, this is a thoughtful, beautifully filmed story <strong>that</strong> asks a simple question: if someone out there called us, would anyone listen?</p>\n<p><strong>Recommended for:</strong> viewers who enjoy slow, intelligent mysteries rather than action.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write a <strong>100–130 word</strong> review of a film or series you know. Include: what it is about, one strength, one weakness, and who you recommend it to. Use at least <strong>two relative clauses with a preposition</strong> (<em>in which</em>, <em>with whom</em>, <em>most of whom</em>…).</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p><em>Kış Uykusu</em> is a slow drama set in a small hotel in Cappadocia, in which a retired actor lives with his wife and sister. The film follows long conversations during which old family problems slowly come to the surface. Its biggest strength is the dialogue: the characters, none of whom are completely good or bad, feel very real. The landscape, which looks beautiful in the snow, also reflects the cold relationships. However, at more than three hours, the film is too long for many viewers, and some scenes repeat the same arguments. I would recommend it to people who enjoy thoughtful films and do not mind a slow pace.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Review self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Says what the film is about in 1–2 sentences (no big spoilers)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Gives one strength and one weakness, with a reason</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses at least two relative clauses with a preposition, correctly (<em>which/whom</em>, never <em>that/who</em> after a preposition)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Ends with a recommendation; 100–130 words</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>\"the city in that she lives\"</strong> → <em>the city <strong>in which</strong> she lives</em> (no <em>that</em> after a preposition).</li><li><strong>\"the man with who she works\"</strong> → <em>with <strong>whom</strong></em>.</li><li>Using the preposition twice: <em>the town <strong>in</strong> which she lives <strong>in</strong></em> → remove one.</li><li>Forgetting commas in non-defining clauses: <em>The director, who is only 30, …</em></li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>review</strong></td><td>an article that describes and judges a film, book, etc.</td><td lang=\"tr\">eleştiri, inceleme yazısı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>plot</strong></td><td>the story of a film or book</td><td lang=\"tr\">olay örgüsü</td></tr><tr><td><strong>relative clause</strong></td><td>a part of a sentence that describes a noun, starting with who/which/that…</td><td lang=\"tr\">sıfat cümleciği (ilgi cümleciği)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>supporting cast</strong></td><td>actors in the smaller roles</td><td lang=\"tr\">yardımcı oyuncular</td></tr><tr><td><strong>spoiler</strong></td><td>information that tells you the end of a story too early</td><td lang=\"tr\">sürprizbozan</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is the reviewer's <strong>overall</strong> opinion?","opts":[{"h":"Very negative","why":"four stars and mostly positive comments"},{"h":"Mostly positive, with a weaker ending","ok":1,"why":"praises atmosphere and acting; criticises the last 20 minutes"},{"h":"The film is only for children","why":"not stated"},{"h":"The reviewer did not finish the film","why":"the review describes the ending part"}],"why":"Stars + the last paragraph usually show the overall view."},{"q":"Who believes Leyla at first?","opts":[{"h":"All the scientists","why":"they think it's a joke"},{"h":"Professor Aydın, her former teacher","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 2"},{"h":"Deniz Kaya","why":"that's the actress"},{"h":"The audience","why":"not the point here"}],"why":"Detail question — scan for \"takes her seriously\"."},{"q":"In \"long, silent shots <strong>during which</strong> we hear only the wind\", <em>which</em> refers to:","opts":[{"h":"the wind","why":"no"},{"h":"the shots","ok":1,"why":"we hear the wind during the shots"},{"h":"the radio","why":"no"},{"h":"the station","why":"no"}],"why":"The relative pronoun refers to the noun just before the clause."},{"q":"What does the reviewer criticise?","opts":[{"h":"The acting","why":"praised"},{"h":"The atmosphere","why":"praised"},{"h":"The last twenty minutes, which rely too much on dialogue","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 5"},{"h":"The length of the title","why":"not mentioned"}],"why":"Criticism is signalled by \"not perfect\" and \"However\"."}],[{"q":"Choose the correct formal sentence.","opts":[{"h":"The hotel in that we stayed was quiet.","why":"<em>that</em> can't follow a preposition"},{"h":"The hotel in which we stayed was quiet.","ok":1,"why":"preposition + which"},{"h":"The hotel in which we stayed in was quiet.","why":"preposition used twice"},{"h":"The hotel in where we stayed was quiet.","why":"no preposition before <em>where</em>"}],"why":"Preposition + <em>which</em> (things) / <em>whom</em> (people)."},{"q":"\"The students, ___ of whom had seen the film, discussed it in class.\"","opts":[{"h":"that","why":"not a quantity word"},{"h":"most","ok":1,"why":"<em>most of whom</em> = most of the students"},{"h":"who","why":"<em>who of whom</em> is wrong"},{"h":"which","why":"wrong"}],"why":"Quantity word + <em>of whom/which</em>."},{"q":"Which word can replace <em>in which</em> in \"the village <strong>in which</strong> the film is set\"?","opts":[{"h":"when","why":"for times"},{"h":"whose","why":"for possession"},{"h":"where","ok":1,"why":"for places"},{"h":"that","why":"<em>that</em> would need <em>in</em> at the end"}],"why":"<em>in which</em> (place) = <em>where</em>."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["The house in "," they grew up is now a museum."],"a":[["which"]],"note":""},{"t":["The friend with "," she travels is a photographer."],"a":[["whom"]],"note":""},{"t":["The scene "," which the two brothers argue is the best part."],"a":[["in"]],"note":""},{"t":["The director, about "," many books have been written, was born in Bursa."],"a":[["whom"]],"note":""},{"t":["The year in "," the film is set is 1923."],"a":[["which"]],"note":""},{"t":["The city "," the story takes place is never named."],"a":[["where","in which"]],"note":""},{"t":["It is a story "," which everyone can relate."],"a":[["to"]],"note":""},{"t":["The film has five main characters, all of "," are students."],"a":[["whom"]],"note":""},{"t":["The soundtrack contains twelve songs, most of "," are original."],"a":[["which"]],"note":""},{"t":["The prize for "," the actor was nominated is the most important in Europe."],"a":[["which"]],"note":""},{"t":["The day "," the film opened, the cinema was full."],"a":[["when","on which"]],"note":""},{"t":["The character "," I felt most sorry for was the old teacher."],"a":[["who","whom","that"]],"note":""},{"t":["The problem "," which the hero must deal is his own fear."],"a":[["with"]],"note":""},{"t":["That is the actress "," brother directed the film."],"a":[["whose"]],"note":""},{"t":["The book on "," the film is based sold millions of copies."],"a":[["which"]],"note":""},{"t":["The island to "," they escape looks peaceful but hides a secret."],"a":[["which"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-08","title":"Film reviews — relative clauses with prepositions","goal":"understand a film review and write relative clauses that include prepositions, such as \"the city in which the story takes place\"","week":"9","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"review","m":"an article that describes and judges a film, book, etc.","tr":"eleştiri, inceleme yazısı"},{"t":"plot","m":"the story of a film or book","tr":"olay örgüsü"},{"t":"relative clause","m":"a part of a sentence that describes a noun, starting with who/which/that…","tr":"sıfat cümleciği (ilgi cümleciği)"},{"t":"supporting cast","m":"actors in the smaller roles","tr":"yardımcı oyuncular"},{"t":"spoiler","m":"information that tells you the end of a story too early","tr":"sürprizbozan"}]},"en-09":{"html":"<p>When we describe how something <strong>is made</strong>, what <strong>was invented</strong> or what <strong>has been done</strong> about a problem, the important thing is usually the <strong>action</strong>, not the person who did it. That is when English uses the <strong>passive voice</strong>. Our topic is plastic: its history and its uses.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-active-vs-passive\"><h2 id=\"active-vs-passive\">Active vs. passive</h2>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Active:</strong> Factories <strong>produce</strong> about 400 million tonnes of plastic every year. <strong>Passive:</strong> About 400 million tonnes of plastic <strong>are produced</strong> every year.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Passive = <strong>be</strong> (in the right tense) + <strong>past participle (V3)</strong>. Add <strong>by + agent</strong> only if the agent is important: <em>Bakelite <strong>was invented by</strong> Leo Baekeland.</em></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tense</th><th>Passive form</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Present simple</td><td>am/is/are + V3</td><td>Plastic bottles <strong>are made</strong> from PET.</td></tr><tr><td>Past simple</td><td>was/were + V3</td><td>The first fully synthetic plastic <strong>was created</strong> in 1907.</td></tr><tr><td>Present perfect</td><td>has/have been + V3</td><td>Plastic bags <strong>have been banned</strong> in many countries.</td></tr><tr><td>Present continuous</td><td>am/is/are being + V3</td><td>New materials <strong>are being tested</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>Future (will)</td><td>will be + V3</td><td>More plastic <strong>will be recycled</strong> in the future.</td></tr><tr><td>Modal</td><td>can/must/should be + V3</td><td>Bottles <strong>can be recycled</strong> many times.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>When do we use the passive?</h3>\n<ul><li>The agent is unknown or obvious: <em>My bike <strong>was stolen</strong>.</em></li><li>We describe a <strong>process</strong> (science, technology, factories): <em>The plastic <strong>is melted</strong> and <strong>poured</strong> into a mould.</em></li><li>Formal and academic writing.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-giving-a-short-talk-2-3-minutes\"><h2 id=\"giving-a-short-talk-2-3-minutes\">Giving a short talk (2–3 minutes)</h2>\n<p>A good short talk has a clear structure. Use <strong>signposting</strong> phrases so listeners know where you are:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Useful phrases</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Introduction</td><td><em>Today I'd like to talk about… · I've divided my talk into three parts.</em></td></tr><tr><td>Moving on</td><td><em>First of all, … · Let's move on to… · Turning now to…</em></td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td><em>For example, … · A good example of this is…</em></td></tr><tr><td>Conclusion</td><td><em>To sum up, … · So, in conclusion, … · Thank you for listening. Are there any questions?</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Speaking tips: look up from your notes, speak a little more slowly than normal, and pause after each main point.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-active-passive-16-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-active-passive-16-items\">Practice: active → passive (16 items)</h2>\n<p>Type the verb in the passive.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-a-short-talk-about-plastic-transcript-310-words\"><h2 id=\"reading-a-short-talk-about-plastic-transcript-310-words\">Reading: a short talk about plastic (transcript, ≈310 words)</h2>\n<blockquote><p>\"Good afternoon, everyone. Today I'd like to talk about a material <strong>that is used</strong> by almost every person on Earth every single day: plastic. I've divided my talk into three parts: its history, its uses, and its problems.</p>\n<p>First of all, history. Natural plastic-like materials, such as rubber, <strong>have been used</strong> for centuries. However, the first fully synthetic plastic, Bakelite, <strong>was invented</strong> in 1907 by a Belgian-American chemist. It <strong>was used</strong> for telephones and radios because it did not melt when it got hot. After the Second World War, mass production began, and cheap plastic products <strong>were sold</strong> all over the world.</p>\n<p>Let's move on to uses. Plastic is light, strong and cheap. Medical equipment such as syringes <strong>is made</strong> from plastic because it can <strong>be thrown</strong> away after one use, which keeps patients safe. Cars <strong>are made</strong> lighter with plastic parts, so they use less fuel. And of course, almost all of our phones and laptops <strong>are covered</strong> in plastic.</p>\n<p>Turning now to the problems. The biggest one is waste. Only a small part of all plastic <strong>has ever been recycled</strong>. The rest <strong>is burned</strong>, <strong>buried</strong> or ends up in nature. Very small pieces, called microplastics, <strong>have been found</strong> in seas, in fish and even in drinking water.</p>\n<p>So what <strong>is being done</strong>? Single-use bags <strong>have been banned</strong> or <strong>charged</strong> for in many countries, including Türkiye. New materials made from plants <strong>are being developed</strong>. But scientists say that the most effective action is the simplest one: use less, and reuse more.</p>\n<p>To sum up, plastic changed modern life, and in many ways it made life safer and easier. But if nothing <strong>is changed</strong>, the problems <strong>will be passed</strong> on to the next generation. Thank you for listening. Are there any questions?\"</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Choose a product (a smartphone, a glass bottle, a T-shirt, a pencil…). Prepare a short talk with an <strong>introduction, three parts, and a conclusion</strong>. Use <strong>at least five passive verbs</strong> and <strong>four signposting phrases</strong>. Record yourself on your phone and listen back.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model plan (glass bottle)</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ul><li><strong>Intro:</strong> Today I'd like to talk about glass bottles. I've divided my talk into three parts: how they are made, how they are used, and how they are recycled.</li><li><strong>Part 1:</strong> Glass <strong>is made</strong> from sand, soda and limestone. The mixture <strong>is heated</strong> to about 1,500 °C and <strong>is blown</strong> into moulds.</li><li><strong>Part 2:</strong> Moving on to uses — drinks, medicines and food <strong>are stored</strong> in glass because it doesn't change the taste.</li><li><strong>Part 3:</strong> Turning now to recycling — glass <strong>can be recycled</strong> endlessly. In many cities it <strong>is collected</strong> in green bins.</li><li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> To sum up, glass is old-fashioned but very sustainable. Thank you for listening.</li></ul></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Talk self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Clear introduction that lists the parts</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least four signposting phrases</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least five correct passive verbs (be + V3) in different tenses</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>A short conclusion and \"Thank you for listening\"</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Spoken slowly, looking up from notes, about 2 minutes</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting <strong>be</strong>: <em>The bottle made in Germany</em> → <em>The bottle <strong>was</strong> made…</em></li><li>Wrong participle: <em>was builded</em> → <em>was <strong>built</strong></em>; <em>was choosed</em> → <em>was <strong>chosen</strong></em>.</li><li>Using the passive with verbs that have no object: <em>The accident <strong>was happened</strong></em> → <em>The accident <strong>happened</strong></em>.</li><li>Adding <em>by people / by someone</em> — it adds nothing. Leave it out.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>passive voice</strong></td><td>a structure (be + V3) where the action is more important than who does it</td><td lang=\"tr\">edilgen çatı</td></tr><tr><td><strong>agent</strong></td><td>the person or thing that does the action (by …)</td><td lang=\"tr\">eylemi yapan (fail)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>signposting</strong></td><td>phrases that show the structure of a talk</td><td lang=\"tr\">yönlendirme ifadeleri</td></tr><tr><td><strong>recycle</strong></td><td>to process used material so it can be used again</td><td lang=\"tr\">geri dönüştürmek</td></tr><tr><td><strong>single-use</strong></td><td>made to be used once and thrown away</td><td lang=\"tr\">tek kullanımlık</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Why was Bakelite used for telephones and radios?","opts":[{"h":"It was very cheap","why":"not the reason given"},{"h":"It did not melt when it got hot","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 2"},{"h":"It came from plants","why":"not stated"},{"h":"It was invented after the war","why":"it was invented in 1907"}],"why":"Scan for \"Bakelite\"."},{"q":"Which signposting phrase introduces the <strong>third</strong> part of the talk?","opts":[{"h":"First of all","why":"introduces part one"},{"h":"Let's move on to","why":"introduces part two"},{"h":"Turning now to","ok":1,"why":"introduces \"the problems\""},{"h":"To sum up","why":"introduces the conclusion"}],"why":"Signposts tell listeners where they are in the talk."},{"q":"According to the talk, what is the <strong>most effective</strong> action against plastic waste?","opts":[{"h":"Banning all plastic","why":"not stated"},{"h":"Using less and reusing more","ok":1,"why":"\"the most effective action is the simplest one\""},{"h":"Burning it","why":"that is part of the problem"},{"h":"Making plastic from plants","why":"mentioned, but not called the most effective"}],"why":"Detail question: look for \"most effective\"."},{"q":"\"Microplastics <strong>have been found</strong> in fish.\" Which tense is this passive?","opts":[{"h":"past simple","why":"that would be <em>were found</em>"},{"h":"present perfect","ok":1,"why":"has/have been + V3"},{"h":"present continuous","why":"that would be <em>are being found</em>"},{"h":"future","why":"that would be <em>will be found</em>"}],"why":"Present perfect passive: <em>have/has been + V3</em>."}],[{"q":"Choose the passive form: \"They <strong>are building</strong> a new recycling plant.\"","opts":[{"h":"A new recycling plant is built.","why":"wrong tense (present simple)"},{"h":"A new recycling plant is being built.","ok":1,"why":"present continuous passive"},{"h":"A new recycling plant has built.","why":"not passive"},{"h":"A new recycling plant was being build.","why":"wrong tense and wrong participle"}],"why":"Present continuous passive: am/is/are <strong>being</strong> + V3."},{"q":"Which sentence is <strong>wrong</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"The bottles are collected on Monday.","why":"correct"},{"h":"The meeting was happened yesterday.","ok":1,"why":"<em>happen</em> has no object, so no passive"},{"h":"Paper can be recycled.","why":"correct"},{"h":"The law has been changed.","why":"correct"}],"why":"Only verbs with an object can be passive."},{"q":"When is the passive most useful?","opts":[{"h":"When the agent is the most important information","why":"then use active"},{"h":"When describing a process, or when the agent is unknown or obvious","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"Only in questions","why":"no"},{"h":"Only in the past","why":"all tenses have a passive"}],"why":"Passive focuses on the action or the thing affected."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["PET bottles "," (make) from oil."],"a":[["are made"]],"note":""},{"t":["Bakelite "," (invent) in 1907."],"a":[["was invented"]],"note":""},{"t":["Plastic bags "," (ban) in many countries since 2010."],"a":[["have been banned"]],"note":""},{"t":["The bottles "," (collect) every Tuesday."],"a":[["are collected"]],"note":""},{"t":["Millions of tonnes of plastic "," (find) in the oceans last year."],"a":[["were found"]],"note":""},{"t":["New recycling machines "," (test) at the moment."],"a":[["are being tested"]],"note":""},{"t":["In the future, more packaging "," (recycle)."],"a":[["will be recycled"]],"note":""},{"t":["Plastic waste "," (should / sort) before it goes in the bin."],"a":[["should be sorted"]],"note":""},{"t":["The first credit cards "," (make) of cardboard, not plastic."],"a":[["were made"]],"note":""},{"t":["Nylon "," (use) for clothes and ropes today."],"a":[["is used"]],"note":""},{"t":["The beach "," (clean) by volunteers last weekend."],"a":[["was cleaned"]],"note":""},{"t":["Since 2019, a fee "," (charge) for plastic bags in Türkiye."],"a":[["has been charged"]],"note":""},{"t":["The plastic "," (melt) and then shaped into new products."],"a":[["is melted"]],"note":""},{"t":["These chairs "," (can / recycle)."],"a":[["can be recycled"]],"note":""},{"t":["A new law "," (discuss) in parliament right now."],"a":[["is being discussed"]],"note":""},{"t":["Today I'd "," to talk about the history of plastic."],"a":[["like"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-09","title":"The story of plastic — passive voice and giving a short talk","goal":"use the passive voice in different tenses to describe processes and history, and give a short structured talk","week":"10","minutes":22,"terms":[{"t":"passive voice","m":"a structure (be + V3) where the action is more important than who does it","tr":"edilgen çatı"},{"t":"agent","m":"the person or thing that does the action (by …)","tr":"eylemi yapan (fail)"},{"t":"signposting","m":"phrases that show the structure of a talk","tr":"yönlendirme ifadeleri"},{"t":"recycle","m":"to process used material so it can be used again","tr":"geri dönüştürmek"},{"t":"single-use","m":"made to be used once and thrown away","tr":"tek kullanımlık"}]},"en-10":{"html":"<p>The vocabulary of the internet is full of <strong>compound nouns</strong>: <em>password, search engine, web page, data centre</em>. This lesson shows how English joins nouns together, when to use <strong>'s</strong> instead, and how to write a balanced paragraph about advantages and disadvantages.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-compound-nouns\"><h2 id=\"compound-nouns\">Compound nouns</h2>\n<p>A compound noun = <strong>noun + noun</strong> (sometimes adjective + noun). The <strong>first</strong> noun works like an adjective; the <strong>second</strong> noun is the main thing.</p>\n<ul><li>a <strong>search engine</strong> = an engine (tool) for searching</li><li>a <strong>phone charger</strong> = a charger for a phone (not a phone!)</li><li>a <strong>data centre</strong> = a centre that holds data</li></ul>\n<p>They are written in three ways — you must learn each one:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>One word</th><th>Two words</th><th>Hyphen</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>password, website, keyboard, smartphone, download</td><td>search engine, web page, home page, email address, data centre, credit card</td><td>e-book, log-in (noun), Wi-Fi, set-up</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Grammar rules</h3>\n<ol><li>The <strong>first noun is usually singular</strong>, even when the meaning is plural: <em>a <strong>shoe</strong> shop</em> (not <em>shoes shop</em>), <em>a <strong>ten-minute</strong> video</em>, <em>a <strong>five-year</strong> plan</em>.</li><li>The <strong>plural</strong> goes on the <strong>second</strong> noun: <em>two <strong>search engines</strong>, three <strong>web pages</strong></em>.</li><li>The <strong>stress</strong> is usually on the <strong>first</strong> part: <strong>PASS</strong>word, <strong>SEARCH</strong> engine.</li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-possessive-s-or-compound-noun\"><h2 id=\"possessive-s-or-compound-noun\">Possessive 's or compound noun?</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Use <strong>'s</strong> for…</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>people and animals (possession)</td><td><em>my <strong>sister's</strong> laptop</em>, <em>the <strong>cat's</strong> bowl</em></td></tr><tr><td>time expressions</td><td><strong><em>today's</strong> news</em>, <strong><em>a week's</strong> holiday</em></td></tr><tr><td>organisations / places (often)</td><td><em>the <strong>university's</strong> website</em>, <strong><em>Istanbul's</strong> bridges</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Use a <strong>compound noun</strong> for…</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>types / purpose of things</td><td><em>a <strong>computer</strong> screen</em>, <em>a <strong>coffee</strong> cup</em> (a cup for coffee)</td></tr><tr><td>things that belong to things</td><td><em>the <strong>door</strong> handle</em> (not <em>the door's handle</em>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Spelling: singular <strong>'s</strong> (<em>the student's bag</em> = one student); regular plural <strong>s'</strong> (<em>the students' bags</em> = many students); irregular plural <strong>'s</strong> (<em>the children's books</em>, <em>people's opinions</em>).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-writing-an-advantages-and-disadvantages-paragraph\"><h2 id=\"writing-an-advantages-and-disadvantages-paragraph\">Writing: an advantages-and-disadvantages paragraph</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Topic sentence</strong> — introduce the topic and say there are two sides.</li><li><strong>Advantages</strong> — 2 points, each with an example. <em>One advantage of… is that… · Another benefit is…</em></li><li><strong>Disadvantages</strong> — 2 points with examples. <em>On the other hand, … · A major drawback is… · However, …</em></li><li><strong>Concluding sentence</strong> — your balanced view. <em>Overall, … · On balance, …</em></li></ol>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-18-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-18-items\">Practice (18 items)</h2>\n<p>Write the compound noun or the possessive.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-learning-online-a-balanced-view-280-words\"><h2 id=\"reading-learning-online-a-balanced-view-280-words\">Reading: Learning online — a balanced view (≈280 words)</h2>\n<blockquote><p>Over the last ten years, online learning has grown enormously. Today, a student in a small Anatolian town can watch a top university's lectures, join a programming course or practise English with a teacher in another country. But is learning online always a good thing?</p>\n<p>One clear advantage is <strong>access</strong>. Course materials, video lectures and e-books are available twenty-four hours a day, so students can study at their own speed. This is especially helpful for people with part-time jobs or family responsibilities. Another benefit is <strong>cost</strong>. Many online courses are free or much cheaper than classroom courses, and students save money on transport.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, online learning has some serious drawbacks. The first is <strong>motivation</strong>. Without a timetable or a teacher's face in front of them, many learners stop after a few weeks. In fact, completion rates for some free online courses are very low. A second problem is <strong>the digital divide</strong>: not everyone has a fast internet connection or a quiet room. A student whose family shares one smartphone is at a real disadvantage.</p>\n<p>Experts suggest that the best model is often a <strong>mix</strong>. Students attend lessons on campus for discussion, labs and teamwork, and use online resources for revision and extra practice. This \"blended\" approach keeps the human contact of the classroom while using the flexibility of the internet.</p>\n<p>On balance, online learning is a powerful tool, but it works best for learners who plan their time carefully — and it does not yet reach everyone equally.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write a <strong>120–150 word</strong> paragraph: <em>\"The advantages and disadvantages of social media for university students.\"</em> Use the four-part structure, at least <strong>four compound nouns</strong> and <strong>two possessives</strong>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>Social media has become part of every student's daily routine, and it has both advantages and disadvantages. One advantage is that it makes communication easy. Class group chats help students share lecture notes, exam dates and project files in seconds. Another benefit is access to information: many professors' accounts and university pages post useful news and job adverts. On the other hand, social media can be a serious distraction. A quick look at a video app can easily become an hour, especially during exam weeks. A further drawback is privacy, because personal photos and phone numbers can be seen by strangers. On balance, social media is a useful study tool, but students should turn off notifications while they study and check their privacy settings.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Paragraph self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Topic sentence that mentions both sides</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Two advantages and two disadvantages, each with an example</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Linking phrases: <em>One advantage…, Another…, On the other hand…, A further drawback…, On balance…</em></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least four compound nouns and two possessives, spelt correctly</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>120–150 words</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>\"a shoes shop\", \"a ten-minutes video\"</strong> → the first noun stays singular: <em>a shoe shop, a ten-minute video</em>.</li><li><strong>\"the computer's screen\"</strong> for things → <em>the computer screen</em>.</li><li>Apostrophe in the wrong place: <em>the students's bags</em> → <em>the students' bags</em>.</li><li>Using <em>its</em> and <em>it's</em>: <strong>its</strong> = possessive (<em>its screen</em>), <strong>it's</strong> = it is.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>compound noun</strong></td><td>two or more words used together as one noun (search engine)</td><td lang=\"tr\">birleşik isim</td></tr><tr><td><strong>possessive 's</strong></td><td>an ending that shows something belongs to someone</td><td lang=\"tr\">iyelik eki ('s)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>advantage / drawback</strong></td><td>a good point / a bad point</td><td lang=\"tr\">avantaj / dezavantaj</td></tr><tr><td><strong>digital divide</strong></td><td>the gap between people with and without good internet access</td><td lang=\"tr\">dijital uçurum</td></tr><tr><td><strong>blended learning</strong></td><td>a mix of classroom and online learning</td><td lang=\"tr\">karma (harmanlanmış) öğrenme</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is the writer's <strong>overall</strong> view?","opts":[{"h":"Online learning should replace universities","why":"the writer suggests a mix"},{"h":"Online learning is useful but works best combined with classroom learning","ok":1,"why":"last two paragraphs"},{"h":"Online learning is useless","why":"advantages are clearly listed"},{"h":"Only rich students should learn online","why":"not stated"}],"why":"The conclusion (\"On balance…\") gives the overall view."},{"q":"Find the <strong>possessive</strong>: which phrase shows possession with 's?","opts":[{"h":"e-books","why":"a compound noun"},{"h":"a top university's lectures","ok":1,"why":"the lectures belong to the university"},{"h":"video lectures","why":"a compound noun"},{"h":"course materials","why":"a compound noun"}],"why":"'s shows possession; noun + noun is a compound."},{"q":"What is \"the digital divide\" in the text?","opts":[{"h":"A type of software","why":"no"},{"h":"The gap between people who have good internet access and those who don't","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 3"},{"h":"A university department","why":"no"},{"h":"A way to divide numbers","why":"no"}],"why":"Use the context: \"not everyone has a fast internet connection\"."},{"q":"Which phrase introduces the <strong>disadvantages</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"One clear advantage","why":"introduces advantages"},{"h":"Another benefit","why":"advantages"},{"h":"On the other hand","ok":1,"why":"signals the opposite side"},{"h":"Experts suggest","why":"introduces the solution"}],"why":"<em>On the other hand</em> contrasts two sides."}],[{"q":"Which is correct?","opts":[{"h":"a five-years plan","why":"the number + noun is singular before another noun"},{"h":"a five-year plan","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"a five-year's plan","why":"no apostrophe"},{"h":"a plan five years","why":"wrong word order"}],"why":"In compounds, the first part is singular."},{"q":"\"The ___ toys are in the garden.\" (toys belonging to several children)","opts":[{"h":"childrens'","why":"<em>children</em> is already plural; add 's"},{"h":"children's","ok":1,"why":"irregular plural + 's"},{"h":"childrens","why":"apostrophe missing"},{"h":"children","why":"possessive needed"}],"why":"Irregular plurals take 's: <em>children's, people's, women's</em>."},{"q":"In \"phone charger\", the main thing is:","opts":[{"h":"the charger","ok":1,"why":"the second noun is the main noun"},{"h":"the phone","why":"the first noun describes the second"},{"h":"both equally","why":"no"},{"h":"neither","why":"no"}],"why":"A phone charger is a kind of charger."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["a charger for a phone → ",""],"a":[["a phone charger","phone charger"]],"note":""},{"t":["the screen of a computer → ",""],"a":[["the computer screen","computer screen","a computer screen"]],"note":""},{"t":["the laptop that belongs to my brother → ",""],"a":[["my brother's laptop"]],"note":""},{"t":["the opinions of people → ",""],"a":[["people's opinions"]],"note":""},{"t":["the news of today → ",""],"a":[["today's news"]],"note":""},{"t":["a shop that sells shoes → ",""],"a":[["a shoe shop","shoe shop"]],"note":""},{"t":["a video that lasts ten minutes → ",""],"a":[["a ten-minute video","ten-minute video"]],"note":""},{"t":["the bags of the students (many students) → ",""],"a":[["the students' bags","students' bags"]],"note":""},{"t":["the toys of the children → ",""],"a":[["the children's toys","children's toys"]],"note":""},{"t":["the website of the university → ",""],"a":[["the university's website","university's website","the university website"]],"note":""},{"t":["an address for email → ",""],"a":[["an email address","email address"]],"note":""},{"t":["the handle of the door → ",""],"a":[["the door handle","door handle"]],"note":""},{"t":["Please type your "," (word you use to log in)."],"a":[["password"]],"note":""},{"t":["Google is a popular search ","."],"a":[["engine"]],"note":""},{"t":["I can't find the home "," of the site."],"a":[["page"]],"note":""},{"t":["One "," of online shopping is that it saves time."],"a":[["advantage","benefit"]],"note":""},{"t":["On the other ",", it is easy to spend too much."],"a":[["hand"]],"note":""},{"t":["On ",", the internet does more good than harm."],"a":[["balance"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-10","title":"The internet — compound nouns, possessive 's and advantages/disadvantages","goal":"form compound nouns and possessives correctly, and write a balanced advantages-and-disadvantages paragraph","week":"11","minutes":22,"terms":[{"t":"compound noun","m":"two or more words used together as one noun (search engine)","tr":"birleşik isim"},{"t":"possessive 's","m":"an ending that shows something belongs to someone","tr":"iyelik eki ('s)"},{"t":"advantage / drawback","m":"a good point / a bad point","tr":"avantaj / dezavantaj"},{"t":"digital divide","m":"the gap between people with and without good internet access","tr":"dijital uçurum"},{"t":"blended learning","m":"a mix of classroom and online learning","tr":"karma (harmanlanmış) öğrenme"}]},"en-11":{"html":"<p>In real conversations people change topic, correct themselves, go back to an earlier point and add surprising information. Small words called <strong>discourse markers</strong> do this job. They don't change the grammar, but they help the listener follow you.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-discourse-markers\"><h2 id=\"common-discourse-markers\">Common discourse markers</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Marker</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>by the way</strong></td><td>introduces a new, less important topic</td><td><em>We loved Büyükada. <strong>By the way</strong>, did you get my photos?</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>anyway</strong></td><td>returns to the main topic, or ends a topic</td><td><em>…the ferry was late, the café was closed… <strong>Anyway</strong>, we finally got there.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>actually</strong></td><td>corrects someone or adds surprising information</td><td><em>\"You stayed a week?\" \"<strong>Actually</strong>, only three days.\"</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>as I was saying</strong></td><td>returns to what you said before an interruption</td><td><em>Sorry about that. <strong>As I was saying</strong>, the beaches are quiet in May.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>speaking of…</strong></td><td>links to a new topic using a word just mentioned</td><td><em>\"We ate fresh fish.\" \"<strong>Speaking of</strong> food, have you tried Bozcaada's grapes?\"</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>mind you</strong></td><td>adds a point that makes the previous one less strong</td><td><em>It was beautiful. <strong>Mind you</strong>, it was very expensive.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>to be honest</strong></td><td>gives a true, sometimes negative opinion</td><td><strong><em>To be honest</strong>, I prefer the mountains.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>I mean</strong></td><td>explains or corrects what you just said</td><td><em>It was crowded — <strong>I mean</strong>, really packed.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>you know</strong></td><td>checks the listener is following (informal)</td><td><em>That small island, <strong>you know</strong>, the one with no cars.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>so</strong></td><td>starts a topic or a conclusion</td><td><strong><em>So</strong>, where shall we go next summer?</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>right / OK then</strong></td><td>closes a topic or a conversation</td><td><strong><em>Right</strong>, I have to go. See you!</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Careful:</strong> <em>actually</em> does <strong>not</strong> mean <em>şu anda</em> (now). It means <em>in fact / aslında</em>. \"Now\" is <em>currently</em> or <em>at the moment</em>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example\"><h2 id=\"worked-example\">Worked example</h2>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Ece:</strong> Did you have a good time on Gökçeada? <strong>Can:</strong> Brilliant! The water was so clear. <strong>Mind you</strong>, the wind was strong every afternoon. <strong>Ece:</strong> <strong>Speaking of</strong> wind, did you try windsurfing? <strong>Can:</strong> <strong>Actually</strong>, I did! I fell in about twenty times. <strong>Anyway</strong>, it was fun. <strong>Ece:</strong> Ha! <strong>By the way</strong>, how did you get there? <strong>Can:</strong> By ferry from Kabatepe. <strong>As I was saying</strong>, the best part was the villages — <strong>I mean</strong>, the old stone houses. <strong>Ece:</strong> <strong>Right</strong>, I think I'm going next year.</p></blockquote>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-16-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-16-items\">Practice (16 items)</h2>\n<p>Type the best discourse marker.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-a-conversation-about-a-trip-260-words\"><h2 id=\"reading-a-conversation-about-a-trip-260-words\">Reading: a conversation about a trip (≈260 words)</h2>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Selin:</strong> Welcome back! How was the island? <strong>Burak:</strong> Honestly? Amazing. Bozcaada is so small that you can cycle around it in a day. Mind you, the hills are steep, so \"a day\" means a very tired day. <strong>Selin:</strong> Ha! Did you stay in a hotel? <strong>Burak:</strong> Actually, no. We stayed with a family who rent rooms in their house. They made breakfast every morning — fresh tomatoes, cheese, local jam… <strong>Selin:</strong> Speaking of food, is it true that the island is famous for wine and grapes? <strong>Burak:</strong> Yes, grapes everywhere. Anyway, the best thing for me was the sunset at the lighthouse on the west side. Everyone goes there in the evening and just sits quietly. <strong>Selin:</strong> That sounds lovely. By the way, how much did the ferry cost? <strong>Burak:</strong> It's cheap for foot passengers, but if you take a car it's much more. To be honest, you don't need a car at all. <strong>Selin:</strong> Good to know. I mean, I don't have a car anyway! <strong>Burak:</strong> [phone rings] Sorry, one second… OK. As I was saying, go in June or September. In August it's really crowded — I mean, you can't find a table anywhere. <strong>Selin:</strong> Right, June it is. Thanks, Burak!</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write (or say with a partner) a <strong>10–12 line</strong> conversation about a holiday you had, real or imaginary. Use at least <strong>six</strong> different discourse markers. Include one interruption and <em>as I was saying</em>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p><strong>A:</strong> So, how was Kaş? <strong>B:</strong> Fantastic. The sea was incredibly clear. Mind you, it was 38 degrees every day. <strong>A:</strong> Wow. Did you go diving? <strong>B:</strong> Actually, I did my first diving course! I mean, just a beginner one. <strong>A:</strong> Speaking of beginners, my brother wants to learn too. <strong>B:</strong> He'd love it. Anyway, the best day was the boat trip to Kekova. <strong>A:</strong> By the way, where did you stay? <strong>B:</strong> In a small guesthouse near the harbour — oh, sorry, that's my mum calling… OK. As I was saying, the boat trip was the highlight. <strong>A:</strong> To be honest, I'm jealous. <strong>B:</strong> Right, I need to go. I'll send you photos!</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Conversation self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least six different discourse markers, each used for the right job</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><em>Actually</em> used for correction or surprise (not \"now\")</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>An interruption followed by <em>As I was saying</em></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Sounds natural: short turns, questions and reactions</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using <strong>actually</strong> to mean \"now\": <em>Actually I live in Avcılar</em> (meaning <em>currently</em>) → <em>I <strong>currently</strong> live in Avcılar</em>.</li><li><strong>\"By the way\"</strong> for the main topic. It introduces a <em>side</em> topic.</li><li>Overusing <em>you know</em> and <em>I mean</em> in formal speaking or writing.</li><li>Writing <em>any way</em> (two words) for the discourse marker → <em>anyway</em>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>discourse marker</strong></td><td>a word or phrase that organises speech (anyway, by the way)</td><td lang=\"tr\">söylem belirleyici</td></tr><tr><td><strong>topic shift</strong></td><td>changing the subject of a conversation</td><td lang=\"tr\">konu değiştirme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>interruption</strong></td><td>stopping someone while they are speaking</td><td lang=\"tr\">söz kesme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>actually</strong></td><td>in fact (used to correct or surprise)</td><td lang=\"tr\">aslında</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ferry</strong></td><td>a boat that carries people (and cars) on a regular route</td><td lang=\"tr\">feribot, vapur</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"Why does Burak say \"Mind you, the hills are steep\"?","opts":[{"h":"To change the topic","why":"it stays on the topic of cycling"},{"h":"To make his positive point about cycling a little less strong","ok":1,"why":"\"mind you\" adds a limiting point"},{"h":"To go back to an earlier point","why":"that's \"as I was saying\""},{"h":"To end the conversation","why":"no"}],"why":"<em>Mind you</em> adds a \"but\" to what was just said."},{"q":"\"<strong>Actually</strong>, no. We stayed with a family.\" Here <em>actually</em> is used to:","opts":[{"h":"talk about now","why":"<em>actually</em> doesn't mean \"now\""},{"h":"correct Selin's guess that he stayed in a hotel","ok":1,"why":"correcting / surprising information"},{"h":"agree with Selin","why":"he says \"no\""},{"h":"introduce a new topic","why":"same topic"}],"why":"<em>Actually</em> = in fact (correction)."},{"q":"Which marker does Burak use after the phone call to return to his topic?","opts":[{"h":"by the way","why":"introduces a new topic"},{"h":"speaking of","why":"links to a word just said"},{"h":"as I was saying","ok":1,"why":"returns to an interrupted point"},{"h":"right","why":"closes the topic"}],"why":"<em>As I was saying</em> = going back after an interruption."},{"q":"When does Burak recommend going?","opts":[{"h":"August","why":"he says it's crowded then"},{"h":"June or September","ok":1,"why":"detail after the phone call"},{"h":"Only in winter","why":"not stated"},{"h":"Any time","why":"he gives specific months"}],"why":"Detail question."}],[{"q":"Which marker introduces a <strong>new, less important</strong> topic?","opts":[{"h":"as I was saying","why":"goes back to an old point"},{"h":"by the way","ok":1,"why":"introduces a side topic"},{"h":"anyway","why":"returns to the main topic"},{"h":"mind you","why":"adds a limiting point"}],"why":"<em>By the way</em> = \"this is not the main topic, but…\""},{"q":"\"You're a third-year student, aren't you?\" \"___, I'm in my first year.\"","opts":[{"h":"Anyway","why":"doesn't correct"},{"h":"Actually","ok":1,"why":"corrects the wrong information"},{"h":"By the way","why":"new topic"},{"h":"Speaking of","why":"needs a linked word"}],"why":"<em>Actually</em> corrects."},{"q":"\"The trip was cheap. ___, the hotel was not very clean.\"","opts":[{"h":"By the way","why":"not a new topic"},{"h":"As I was saying","why":"no interruption"},{"h":"Mind you","ok":1,"why":"adds a point that limits \"cheap\""},{"h":"Speaking of","why":"no link word"}],"why":"<em>Mind you</em> = but remember that…"}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["\"Did you fly there?\" \"",", we went by ferry.\""],"a":[["actually"]],"note":""},{"t":["We missed the bus and it started to rain. ",", we got there in the end."],"a":[["anyway"]],"note":""},{"t":["I love the island. ",", have you seen my sunglasses?"],"a":[["by the way"]],"note":""},{"t":["Sorry, my phone rang. As I was ",", the hotel was great."],"a":[["saying"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"The food was amazing.\" \"Speaking "," food, what did you eat?\""],"a":[["of"]],"note":""},{"t":["The hotel was lovely. "," you, the rooms were small."],"a":[["mind"]],"note":""},{"t":["To be ",", I didn't enjoy the boat trip. I felt sick."],"a":[["honest"]],"note":""},{"t":["It was hot — I ",", 40 degrees!"],"a":[["mean"]],"note":""},{"t":["",", I must go now. Talk later!"],"a":[["right","ok then","anyway"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"You're from Izmir, right?\" \"",", I'm from Manisa, but I study in Izmir.\""],"a":[["actually"]],"note":""},{"t":[""," the way, the exam has moved to Tuesday."],"a":[["by"]],"note":""},{"t":["We talked about beaches, prices, and food… ",", which island should we choose?"],"a":[["anyway","so"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"It's a quiet island.\" \"Mind ",", it's busy in August.\""],"a":[["you"]],"note":""},{"t":["The island, you ",", the one near Çanakkale, has no traffic lights."],"a":[["know"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"Is it expensive?\" \"To be ",", yes, quite.\""],"a":[["honest"]],"note":""},{"t":["",", let's start planning our trip. First, dates."],"a":[["so"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-11","title":"Islands and travel — managing conversation with discourse markers","goal":"use discourse markers such as anyway, by the way, actually and as I was saying to manage a natural conversation","week":"12","minutes":18,"terms":[{"t":"discourse marker","m":"a word or phrase that organises speech (anyway, by the way)","tr":"söylem belirleyici"},{"t":"topic shift","m":"changing the subject of a conversation","tr":"konu değiştirme"},{"t":"interruption","m":"stopping someone while they are speaking","tr":"söz kesme"},{"t":"actually","m":"in fact (used to correct or surprise)","tr":"aslında"},{"t":"ferry","m":"a boat that carries people (and cars) on a regular route","tr":"feribot, vapur"}]},"en-12":{"html":"<p>When we don't know exactly what happened, we <strong>guess from the evidence</strong>. Detectives, scientists and ordinary people do this all the time: <em>\"He <strong>must have missed</strong> the bus.\"</em> <em>\"She <strong>can't have seen</strong> the message.\"</em> These are <strong>past modals of deduction</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-structure\"><h2 id=\"structure\">Structure</h2>\n<p><strong>modal + have + past participle (V3)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>How sure are you?</th><th>Modal</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>~95% sure it <strong>happened</strong></td><td><strong>must have</strong> + V3</td><td><em>The ground is wet. It <strong>must have rained</strong> last night.</em></td></tr><tr><td>about 50% — possible</td><td><strong>might have / may have / could have</strong> + V3</td><td><em>He's late. He <strong>might have missed</strong> the bus.</em></td></tr><tr><td>~95% sure it <strong>did not</strong> happen</td><td><strong>can't have / couldn't have</strong> + V3</td><td><em>She <strong>can't have stolen</strong> it — she was in Ankara.</em></td></tr><tr><td>50% it didn't happen</td><td><strong>might not have / may not have</strong> + V3</td><td><em>They <strong>might not have heard</strong> the alarm.</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The opposite of <em>must have</em> is <strong>can't have</strong> — <strong>not</strong> <em>mustn't have</em>.</li><li><em>could have</em> has two meanings: possibility (<em>He could have left already</em>) and an ability that was not used (<em>You could have called me!</em> = you didn't).</li><li>In speaking, <em>must have</em> sounds like \"must-of\" — but always <strong>write</strong> <em>have</em>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-present-vs-past\"><h2 id=\"present-vs-past\">Present vs. past</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Present guess</th><th>Past guess</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>He <strong>must be</strong> tired.</td><td>He <strong>must have been</strong> tired.</td></tr><tr><td>She <strong>might be</strong> at home.</td><td>She <strong>might have been</strong> at home.</td></tr><tr><td>It <strong>can't be</strong> true.</td><td>It <strong>can't have been</strong> true.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example-the-empty-lighthouse\"><h2 id=\"worked-example-the-empty-lighthouse\">Worked example: the empty lighthouse</h2>\n<blockquote><p>In 1900, three keepers disappeared from a lighthouse on a small Scottish island. Passing ships noticed that the lamp was not lit. When a boat finally arrived, the keepers were gone, and two of the three waterproof coats were missing.</p></blockquote>\n<ul><li>Two coats were missing, so two men <strong>must have gone</strong> outside in bad weather.</li><li>The third coat was still there, so the third man <strong>might have run</strong> out in a hurry.</li><li>The weather had been terrible for days. A big wave <strong>could have washed</strong> them into the sea.</li><li>They <strong>can't have left</strong> by boat — no boat had come to the island.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-18-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-18-items\">Practice (18 items)</h2>\n<p>Complete with <strong>must have / might have / can't have</strong> + the verb.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-nine-days-lost-in-the-sahara-280-words\"><h2 id=\"reading-nine-days-lost-in-the-sahara-280-words\">Reading: Nine days lost in the Sahara (≈280 words)</h2>\n<blockquote><p>In 1994, an Italian runner named Mauro Prosperi took part in a famous 250-kilometre race across the Sahara Desert. On the fourth day, a terrible sandstorm hit. When it finally stopped, after eight hours, the landscape had completely changed, and Prosperi had no idea where he was.</p>\n<p>He was missing for nine days before he was found by a nomadic family, hundreds of kilometres away, in Algeria. Rescuers later tried to understand how he could have survived. He <strong>must have used</strong> his race training, they said, because he walked mainly in the early morning and the evening, and rested in the shade during the hottest hours.</p>\n<p>Prosperi himself explained some of his decisions. He drank very little water at a time and made his supply last as long as possible. At one point he found a small building, a shrine, where he slept and caught small animals to eat. A plane flew over him but did not see him. The pilot <strong>can't have noticed</strong> his signal fire, because of the strong light and the distance.</p>\n<p>Doctors were surprised that he lost around 16 kilograms but had no permanent damage. Some believe his excellent physical fitness <strong>might have protected</strong> his body. Others think simple luck <strong>may have played</strong> a big part: if the nomads had not passed that way, the story <strong>could have ended</strong> very differently.</p>\n<p>Prosperi did not give up running. In fact, he returned to the same desert race several times in later years. When journalists asked why, he said that the desert had taught him not to panic.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Read the situation and write <strong>five sentences</strong> with past modals.</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>You come home. The front door is open. There is mud on the floor, an empty food bowl, a broken vase, and your dog is asleep on the sofa. Your brother's bag is not there.</em></p></blockquote>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li>Somebody <strong>must have left</strong> the door open — probably my brother when he went out.</li><li>The dog <strong>must have run</strong> outside, because there is mud on the floor.</li><li>It <strong>might have knocked</strong> over the vase while it was running around.</li><li>A burglar <strong>can't have come</strong> in — nothing is missing and the dog is calm.</li><li>My brother <strong>might have fed</strong> the dog before he left, or the dog <strong>could have eaten</strong> everything very quickly!</li></ol></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Deduction self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <em>must have</em>, <em>might/may/could have</em> and <em>can't have</em> at least once each</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Always <strong>modal + have + V3</strong> (not <em>must have went</em>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Each guess is linked to a piece of evidence</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span><em>can't have</em> (not <em>mustn't have</em>) for strong negative guesses</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>\"He must have went\"</strong> → <em>must have <strong>gone</strong></em> (use V3, not V2).</li><li><strong>\"She mustn't have seen it\"</strong> for a strong negative guess → <em>She <strong>can't have</strong> seen it.</em></li><li>Writing <strong>\"must of\"</strong> → <em>must <strong>have</strong></em>.</li><li>Using a present modal for a past situation: <em>He must be at home yesterday</em> → <em>He must <strong>have been</strong> at home yesterday</em>.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>deduction</strong></td><td>a conclusion you reach from evidence</td><td lang=\"tr\">çıkarım</td></tr><tr><td><strong>evidence</strong></td><td>facts or signs that show something is true</td><td lang=\"tr\">kanıt</td></tr><tr><td><strong>past modal</strong></td><td>modal + have + V3, for guesses about the past</td><td lang=\"tr\">geçmiş zaman kip yapısı (must have / might have)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>survive</strong></td><td>to stay alive in a dangerous situation</td><td lang=\"tr\">hayatta kalmak</td></tr><tr><td><strong>speculate</strong></td><td>to guess without being certain</td><td lang=\"tr\">tahmin yürütmek, spekülasyon yapmak</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"How long was Prosperi missing according to the text?","opts":[{"h":"forty days","why":"not stated"},{"h":"nine days","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 2"},{"h":"four days","why":"the storm happened on day four"},{"h":"eight hours","why":"that's how long the storm lasted"}],"why":"Scan for numbers — but check which event each number belongs to."},{"q":"\"He <strong>must have used</strong> his race training.\" The writer is:","opts":[{"h":"not sure at all","why":"<em>must have</em> is a strong guess"},{"h":"almost sure, based on evidence (how he walked and rested)","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"sure that it didn't happen","why":"that's <em>can't have</em>"},{"h":"talking about the future","why":"it's about the past"}],"why":"<em>must have</em> = strong deduction about the past."},{"q":"Why didn't the pilot help him?","opts":[{"h":"He didn't want to","why":"not stated"},{"h":"He probably didn't see the signal fire because of the light and distance","ok":1,"why":"\"can't have noticed\""},{"h":"The plane had no fuel","why":"not stated"},{"h":"Prosperi hid from the plane","why":"he lit a signal fire"}],"why":"Detail + modal meaning."},{"q":"Which sentence expresses the <strong>weakest</strong> certainty?","opts":[{"h":"He must have used his training.","why":"strong"},{"h":"The pilot can't have noticed the fire.","why":"strong (negative)"},{"h":"Luck may have played a big part.","ok":1,"why":"<em>may have</em> = possible"},{"h":"He lost 16 kilograms.","why":"a fact, not a guess"}],"why":"<em>may/might/could have</em> = possibility."}],[{"q":"\"His car isn't outside. He ___ to work already.\"","opts":[{"h":"can't have gone","why":"the evidence suggests he did go"},{"h":"must have gone","ok":1,"why":"strong guess from evidence"},{"h":"must have went","why":"wrong participle"},{"h":"mustn't have gone","why":"wrong form for deduction"}],"why":"Evidence → <em>must have + V3</em>."},{"q":"Which sentence means \"I'm almost sure it didn't happen\"?","opts":[{"h":"She might have forgotten.","why":"only possible"},{"h":"She must have forgotten.","why":"sure it <em>did</em> happen"},{"h":"She can't have forgotten.","ok":1,"why":"strong negative deduction"},{"h":"She could have forgotten.","why":"only possible"}],"why":"<em>can't have</em> = strong negative."},{"q":"\"You ___ me you were coming! I would have cooked.\" (criticism)","opts":[{"h":"must have told","why":"a deduction, not criticism"},{"h":"could have told","ok":1,"why":"an ability you didn't use"},{"h":"can't have told","why":"wrong meaning"},{"h":"might told","why":"wrong form"}],"why":"<em>could have + V3</em> can criticise a missed chance."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["The windows are broken and the TV is gone. Someone "," (break) in."],"a":[["must have broken in"]],"note":""},{"t":["I can't find my keys. I "," (leave) them at work — I'm not sure."],"a":[["might have left","may have left","could have left"]],"note":""},{"t":["He "," (write) this essay alone; it's far too perfect for a first-year student."],"a":[["can't have written","couldn't have written"]],"note":""},{"t":["She passed with 98%. She "," (study) very hard."],"a":[["must have studied"]],"note":""},{"t":["The lights are off. They "," (go) to bed already."],"a":[["might have gone","must have gone","may have gone"]],"note":""},{"t":["You "," (see) Ali yesterday — he's been in Germany all month!"],"a":[["can't have seen","couldn't have seen"]],"note":""},{"t":["The ground is white. It "," (snow) during the night."],"a":[["must have snowed"]],"note":""},{"t":["The explorers "," (survive) for weeks without food — maybe they found fish."],"a":[["might have survived","could have survived","may have survived"]],"note":""},{"t":["Nobody answered. They "," (hear) the bell."],"a":[["can't have heard","might not have heard","didn't hear"]],"note":""},{"t":["The ship disappeared without any radio message. A sudden storm "," (sink) it."],"a":[["might have sunk","could have sunk","may have sunk"]],"note":""},{"t":["My phone was on the table a minute ago. Someone "," (take) it!"],"a":[["must have taken"]],"note":""},{"t":["They "," (be) very hungry after walking 30 km."],"a":[["must have been"]],"note":""},{"t":["He "," (be) at the party — he was ill in bed."],"a":[["can't have been","couldn't have been"]],"note":""},{"t":["Why didn't you tell me? You "," (call) me! (ability not used)"],"a":[["could have called"]],"note":""},{"t":["The opposite of \"must have\" is \""," have\"."],"a":[["can't","cannot","couldn't"]],"note":""},{"t":["He must be tired now. (past) → He must have "," tired."],"a":[["been"]],"note":""},{"t":["I'm not sure where she went. She "," (go) home."],"a":[["might have gone","may have gone","could have gone"]],"note":""},{"t":["The note is in Japanese. Hakan "," (write) it — he doesn't know any Japanese."],"a":[["can't have written","couldn't have written"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-12","title":"Survival and mysteries — past modals of deduction","goal":"make guesses about the past with must have, might have, could have and can't have, depending on how sure you are","week":"13","minutes":22,"terms":[{"t":"deduction","m":"a conclusion you reach from evidence","tr":"çıkarım"},{"t":"evidence","m":"facts or signs that show something is true","tr":"kanıt"},{"t":"past modal","m":"modal + have + V3, for guesses about the past","tr":"geçmiş zaman kip yapısı (must have / might have)"},{"t":"survive","m":"to stay alive in a dangerous situation","tr":"hayatta kalmak"},{"t":"speculate","m":"to guess without being certain","tr":"tahmin yürütmek, spekülasyon yapmak"}]},"en-13":{"html":"<p>Good writers don't repeat the same words again and again. Compare:</p>\n<blockquote><p>✗ <em>I read a dystopian novel last month. <strong>The dystopian novel</strong> was frightening. My friend also wants to read <strong>a dystopian novel</strong>.</em> ✓ <em>I read a dystopian novel last month. <strong>It</strong> was frightening. My friend also wants to read <strong>one</strong>.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p>English has several tools for <strong>substitution</strong> (replacing words) and <strong>ellipsis</strong> (leaving words out).</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-1-pronouns-it-they-this-that-these\"><h2 id=\"1-pronouns-it-they-this-that-these\">1. Pronouns: it, they, this, that, these</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>it / they</strong> replace a noun already mentioned: <em>The city had no cars. <strong>It</strong> was silent.</em></li><li><strong>this / that</strong> can replace a whole idea: <em>The government controlled all the news. <strong>This</strong> meant nobody knew the truth.</em></li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2-one-ones-replacing-a-countable-noun\"><h2 id=\"2-one-ones-replacing-a-countable-noun\">2. one / ones — replacing a countable noun</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>one</strong> = a singular countable noun</td><td><em>I need a new phone. My old <strong>one</strong> is broken.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>ones</strong> = a plural countable noun</td><td><em>I prefer short stories to long <strong>ones</strong>.</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>the one(s) + which/that/who / with</strong></td><td><em>Which book? — <strong>The one</strong> with the red cover.</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Not with uncountable nouns: <em>I like hot coffee, not cold <strong>one</strong></em> ✗ → <em>not cold coffee / not cold</em> ✓.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-3-so-not-replacing-a-clause\"><h2 id=\"3-so-not-replacing-a-clause\">3. so / not — replacing a clause</h2>\n<p>After <em>think, hope, expect, believe, suppose, be afraid, guess</em>:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Positive</th><th>Negative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><em>\"Will robots do our jobs?\" \"I think <strong>so</strong>.\"</em></td><td><em>\"I don't think <strong>so</strong>.\"</em> / <em>\"I hope <strong>not</strong>.\"</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>\"Is the exam today?\" \"I'm afraid <strong>so</strong>.\"</em></td><td><em>\"I'm afraid <strong>not</strong>.\"</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-4-do-do-so-did-replacing-a-verb-phrase\"><h2 id=\"4-do-do-so-did-replacing-a-verb-phrase\">4. do / do so / did — replacing a verb phrase</h2>\n<ul><li><em>She said she would finish the project, and she <strong>did</strong>.</em> (= finished the project)</li><li><em>The rules told citizens to report their neighbours, but few <strong>did so</strong>.</em> (formal)</li><li><em>\"I love sci-fi.\" \"So <strong>do</strong> I.\" / \"Neither <strong>do</strong> I.\"</em> (for negatives)</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-5-the-same-such\"><h2 id=\"5-the-same-such\">5. the same / such</h2>\n<ul><li><em>I'll have a tea. — I'll have <strong>the same</strong>.</em></li><li><em>A world without books? I can't imagine <strong>such</strong> a place.</em></li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-utopia-vs-dystopia\"><h2 id=\"utopia-vs-dystopia\">Utopia vs. dystopia</h2>\n<p>A <strong>utopia</strong> is an imaginary perfect society. A <strong>dystopia</strong> is an imaginary society where life is frightening and people are controlled — often by a government, a company or technology. Dystopian stories usually <em>warn</em> readers about problems in today's world.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-18-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-18-items\">Practice (18 items)</h2>\n<p>Type the best substitute word (one, ones, it, so, not, do, did, the same, such, this…).</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-why-do-we-love-dark-futures-290-words\"><h2 id=\"reading-why-do-we-love-dark-futures-290-words\">Reading: Why do we love dark futures? (≈290 words)</h2>\n<blockquote><p>Look at the best-selling young-adult novels and series of the last twenty years and you will find many dystopias. In <strong>them</strong>, teenagers fight governments that watch every move, control every meal and choose every job. Why are such stories so popular?</p>\n<p>One reason is that they take real fears and make them bigger. Today we worry about surveillance cameras, social media algorithms and climate change. A dystopian writer imagines a world where these problems have grown out of control. Reading about <strong>one</strong> helps us think about the dangers in our own, without feeling directly threatened.</p>\n<p>A second reason is the hero. Most dystopian stories follow a young person who questions the rules. At first, the main character obeys like everyone else. Then something happens — a friend disappears, or a secret is discovered — and she decides to resist. Readers admire characters who <strong>do so</strong>, because standing up to power is difficult in real life too.</p>\n<p>Utopias, the opposite kind of story, are much less common today. Writers in the past often described perfect islands or future cities without crime or poverty. Modern readers, however, tend to find <strong>such</strong> places a little boring — or suspicious. \"If everything is perfect,\" they ask, \"who decided what perfect means?\" In fact, many dystopias start as utopias: a society that promises happiness, but only if citizens give up their freedom.</p>\n<p>Will dystopian fiction stay popular? Most publishers think <strong>so</strong>. As long as people feel worried about the future, they will want stories that explore it — and, perhaps, stories that show <strong>it</strong> can still be changed.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Rewrite this paragraph using substitution so that <strong>no noun phrase is repeated</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>In the film, the city looks like a perfect city. The citizens of the city are happy, and the citizens always smile. The government gives every citizen a new phone each year. The phones record everything. The main character starts to wonder if the government is lying. The main character thinks the government is lying. Her friend doesn't think the government is lying.</em></p></blockquote>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>In the film, the city looks like a perfect <strong>one</strong>. <strong>Its</strong> citizens are happy, and <strong>they</strong> always smile. The government gives every citizen a new phone each year, and <strong>these</strong> record everything. The main character starts to wonder if the government is lying. She thinks <strong>so</strong>, but her friend doesn't.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Substitution self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <em>one / ones</em> for countable nouns</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <em>it / they / its / these</em> clearly (the reader knows what each refers to)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <em>so / not</em> after <em>think / hope</em></span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses <em>do / does / did</em> to avoid repeating a verb phrase</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>The new paragraph is shorter and easier to read</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>\"I don't think it.\"</strong> → <em>I don't think <strong>so</strong>.</em></li><li><strong>\"I hope no.\"</strong> → <em>I hope <strong>not</strong>.</em></li><li>Using <strong>one</strong> for uncountables: <em>I want water, a cold one</em> → <em>I want cold water</em>.</li><li>Unclear pronouns: if <em>it</em> could mean two things, repeat the noun.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>substitution</strong></td><td>replacing a word or phrase with a shorter one to avoid repetition</td><td lang=\"tr\">yerine koyma (ikame)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ellipsis</strong></td><td>leaving out words that the reader can understand</td><td lang=\"tr\">eksiltme</td></tr><tr><td><strong>utopia</strong></td><td>an imaginary perfect society</td><td lang=\"tr\">ütopya</td></tr><tr><td><strong>dystopia</strong></td><td>an imaginary society where life is controlled and frightening</td><td lang=\"tr\">distopya</td></tr><tr><td><strong>surveillance</strong></td><td>watching people closely, often secretly</td><td lang=\"tr\">gözetim</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"In paragraph 2, \"Reading about <strong>one</strong>\" — <em>one</em> replaces:","opts":[{"h":"a teenager","why":"not mentioned in that sentence"},{"h":"a world where problems have grown out of control (a dystopia)","ok":1,"why":"the singular noun idea just mentioned"},{"h":"a social media algorithm","why":"no"},{"h":"the writer","why":"no"}],"why":"<em>one</em> replaces a singular countable noun from the previous sentence."},{"q":"\"Readers admire characters who <strong>do so</strong>.\" <em>do so</em> means:","opts":[{"h":"obey the rules","why":"opposite"},{"h":"resist / question the rules","ok":1,"why":"replaces \"decides to resist\""},{"h":"read books","why":"no"},{"h":"disappear","why":"no"}],"why":"<em>do so</em> replaces a verb phrase."},{"q":"Why are utopias less popular today, according to the writer?","opts":[{"h":"They are too frightening","why":"that's dystopias"},{"h":"Readers find perfect places boring or suspicious","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 4"},{"h":"They are too expensive to publish","why":"not stated"},{"h":"They are banned","why":"not stated"}],"why":"Detail question."},{"q":"\"Most publishers think <strong>so</strong>.\" <em>so</em> replaces:","opts":[{"h":"\"dystopian fiction will stay popular\"","ok":1,"why":"the clause in the question before it"},{"h":"\"dystopian fiction is boring\"","why":"no"},{"h":"\"utopias are common\"","why":"no"},{"h":"\"people are worried\"","why":"not the closest idea"}],"why":"<em>think so</em> = think that the previous statement is true."}],[{"q":"\"I like your bag. Where did you buy ___?\"","opts":[{"h":"it","ok":1,"why":"the same specific bag"},{"h":"one","why":"<em>one</em> = a different bag of the same kind"},{"h":"so","why":"<em>so</em> replaces clauses"},{"h":"ones","why":"plural"}],"why":"<em>it</em> = that exact thing; <em>one</em> = another of the same type."},{"q":"\"Is the café open on Sunday?\" \"I don't think ___.\"","opts":[{"h":"it","why":"wrong"},{"h":"not","why":"<em>I don't think not</em> is a double negative"},{"h":"so","ok":1,"why":"<em>I don't think so</em>"},{"h":"one","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>think so / don't think so</em>."},{"q":"Which sentence is correct?","opts":[{"h":"I prefer red apples to green one.","why":"plural needs <em>ones</em>"},{"h":"I prefer red apples to green ones.","ok":1,"why":"plural countable → <em>ones</em>"},{"h":"I prefer red apples to green it.","why":"wrong"},{"h":"I prefer red apples to green so.","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>ones</em> replaces a plural countable noun."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["My laptop is old. I need a new ","."],"a":[["one"]],"note":""},{"t":["These shoes are too small. Do you have bigger ","?"],"a":[["ones"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"Will it rain tomorrow?\" \"I hope ",".\" (you don't want rain)"],"a":[["not"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"Is the library open today?\" \"I think ",".\" (yes)"],"a":[["so"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"Did you pass?\" \"I'm afraid ",".\" (sadly, no)"],"a":[["not"]],"note":""},{"t":["I bought a novel and gave "," to my sister."],"a":[["it"]],"note":""},{"t":["She promised to help, and she ","."],"a":[["did"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"I don't like horror films.\" \"Neither "," I.\""],"a":[["do"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"I'll have the chicken.\" \"I'll have "," same.\""],"a":[["the"]],"note":""},{"t":["Citizens were told to stay at home, and most of them did ","."],"a":[["so"]],"note":""},{"t":["Which seat is yours? — The "," by the window."],"a":[["one"]],"note":""},{"t":["The government read everyone's messages. "," made people afraid to speak."],"a":[["this","that","it"]],"note":""},{"t":["I've never seen "," a beautiful city."],"a":[["such"]],"note":""},{"t":["I prefer old films to modern ","."],"a":[["ones"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"Is he coming?\" \"I don't think ",".\""],"a":[["so"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"I love science fiction.\" \"So "," I!\""],"a":[["do"]],"note":""},{"t":["The robots in the first film were friendly, but the "," in the second were dangerous."],"a":[["ones"]],"note":""},{"t":["\"Are we late?\" \"I'm afraid ",".\" (yes)"],"a":[["so"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-13","title":"Utopias and dystopias — pronouns and substitution","goal":"avoid repeating words by using pronouns and substitutes such as one/ones, so, not, do so and the same","week":"14","minutes":20,"terms":[{"t":"substitution","m":"replacing a word or phrase with a shorter one to avoid repetition","tr":"yerine koyma (ikame)"},{"t":"ellipsis","m":"leaving out words that the reader can understand","tr":"eksiltme"},{"t":"utopia","m":"an imaginary perfect society","tr":"ütopya"},{"t":"dystopia","m":"an imaginary society where life is controlled and frightening","tr":"distopya"},{"t":"surveillance","m":"watching people closely, often secretly","tr":"gözetim"}]},"en-14":{"html":"<p>Many things that were science fiction fifty years ago — video calls, robot vacuum cleaners, talking computers — are normal today. Others, like flying cars and time travel, are still fiction. In this lesson you compare the two, and learn how to write a strong <strong>comparison paragraph</strong>: a paragraph that shows how two things are <strong>similar</strong> and how they are <strong>different</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"sec sec-language-for-comparing\"><h2 id=\"language-for-comparing\">Language for comparing</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Similarities</th><th>Differences</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Both</strong> A <strong>and</strong> B are…</td><td>A is…, <strong>whereas / while</strong> B is…</td></tr><tr><td>A is <strong>similar to</strong> B <strong>in that</strong>…</td><td><strong>Unlike</strong> A, B is…</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Like</strong> A, B …</td><td>A is…; <strong>however</strong>, B is…</td></tr><tr><td>A …, and B <strong>also</strong> … / B does <strong>too</strong></td><td><strong>In contrast</strong>, … / <strong>On the other hand</strong>, …</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Similarly</strong>, … / <strong>Likewise</strong>, …</td><td>A is <strong>much / far / slightly</strong> more…<strong>than</strong> B</td></tr><tr><td>A is <strong>as</strong> … <strong>as</strong> B</td><td>A is <strong>not as</strong> … <strong>as</strong> B</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Comparative adjectives:</strong> <em>faster, cheaper, more realistic, less expensive, better, worse.</em> Make them stronger or weaker with <strong>much / far / a lot / slightly / a bit</strong>: <em>Real robots are <strong>far less</strong> intelligent than film robots.</em></p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-two-ways-to-organise-a-comparison-paragraph\"><h2 id=\"two-ways-to-organise-a-comparison-paragraph\">Two ways to organise a comparison paragraph</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Block method</th><th>Point-by-point method</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Topic sentence</td><td>Topic sentence</td></tr><tr><td>Everything about A (point 1, 2, 3)</td><td>Point 1: A vs. B</td></tr><tr><td>Everything about B (point 1, 2, 3)</td><td>Point 2: A vs. B</td></tr><tr><td>Concluding sentence</td><td>Point 3: A vs. B</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Concluding sentence</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>For one paragraph, <strong>point-by-point</strong> is usually clearer, because the reader sees each difference immediately.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-worked-example-point-by-point\"><h2 id=\"worked-example-point-by-point\">Worked example (point-by-point)</h2>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Topic sentence:</strong> Robots in science-fiction films and robots in real life are similar in some ways, but they are very different in intelligence and appearance. <strong>Point 1 (similarity):</strong> Both film robots and real robots do jobs that are dangerous or boring for humans, such as exploring space or building cars. <strong>Point 2 (intelligence):</strong> However, film robots can usually think, talk and feel like people, whereas real robots can only do the specific tasks they were programmed for. <strong>Point 3 (appearance):</strong> Unlike the human-shaped robots in films, most real robots look like arms, boxes or small vehicles. <strong>Conclusion:</strong> In short, real robots are far more useful than they are exciting — at least for now.</p></blockquote>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-practice-16-items\"><h2 id=\"practice-16-items\">Practice (16 items)</h2>\n<p>Type the missing word.</p>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-reading-yesterday-s-future-290-words\"><h2 id=\"reading-yesterday-s-future-290-words\">Reading: Yesterday's future (≈290 words)</h2>\n<blockquote><p>In 1966, a famous American television series showed space travellers talking to small flat devices and asking computers questions out loud. At the time, both ideas seemed as far away as travelling faster than light. Today, almost everyone carries a smartphone, and millions of people speak to digital assistants every day.</p>\n<p>It is interesting to compare how science fiction imagined the future with how it actually arrived. Some inventions appeared much faster than writers expected. <strong>Unlike</strong> the giant, room-sized computers in early novels, real computers became smaller and cheaper every year. <strong>Similarly</strong>, video calls, which looked magical in films, are now so normal that people complain about them.</p>\n<p>Other predictions, <strong>however</strong>, were far too optimistic. Writers in the 1950s were sure that by the year 2000 people would live on the Moon and drive flying cars. In reality, no human has walked on the Moon since 1972, and flying cars are still experimental. Building a working machine is one challenge; making it safe, cheap and legal is a much bigger one.</p>\n<p>There is also a difference in <strong>how</strong> technology changed life. Science fiction often showed big, visible changes — robots in every house, cities in the sky. <strong>In contrast</strong>, the real revolution has been mostly invisible: data, networks and software that we cannot see but use every minute.</p>\n<p>Perhaps the most important lesson is that science fiction is <strong>not as</strong> good at predicting gadgets <strong>as</strong> it is at predicting problems. Many stories warned about surveillance, fake news and dependence on machines. <strong>Like</strong> the inventions, these warnings have also become reality — <strong>whereas</strong> the flying cars have not.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-try-it\"><h2 id=\"try-it\">Try it</h2>\n<p>Write <strong>130–160 words</strong> comparing <strong>one piece of technology in a science-fiction film or book with the real version today</strong> (robots, AI assistants, space travel, self-driving cars, video calls…). Use the <strong>point-by-point</strong> method with one similarity and two differences.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>Artificial intelligence in science-fiction films and AI in real life are similar in one way, but they differ greatly in ability and in danger. Both film AI and real AI can understand human language and answer questions, so people can talk to them almost like to a person. However, film AI usually understands the world completely and makes its own decisions, whereas real AI tools only predict useful answers from the data they were trained on, and they still make simple mistakes. Unlike the evil computers in many films, real AI does not want to control humans; it has no wishes at all. In contrast, the real risks are less dramatic: false information, privacy problems and people trusting the answers too much. In short, real AI is far more limited than film AI, but its problems are just as important to discuss.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Comparison paragraph self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Topic sentence names both things and says they are similar in some ways and different in others</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>One similarity and at least two differences, point by point</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>At least five comparison expressions (<em>both, whereas, unlike, in contrast, far more…than</em>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Correct comparatives (<em>more realistic</em>, not <em>more realisticer</em>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Concluding sentence; 130–160 words</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-common-mistakes\"><h2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>\"more cheaper\", \"more better\"</strong> → <em>cheaper</em>, <em>better</em> (don't double the comparative).</li><li><strong>\"Unlike film robots, real robots are not like humans\"</strong> is fine, but don't forget the comma after the <em>Unlike…</em> phrase.</li><li><strong>\"Whereas\"</strong> at the start of a sentence with only one clause: <em>Whereas real robots are simple.</em> ✗ — it needs two clauses.</li><li>Writing only about A and forgetting B — every point should mention both.</li></ul>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-key-terms\"><h2 id=\"key-terms\">Key terms</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"terms\"><thead><tr><th>English term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Turkish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>comparison</strong></td><td>showing how two things are similar</td><td lang=\"tr\">karşılaştırma</td></tr><tr><td><strong>contrast</strong></td><td>showing how two things are different</td><td lang=\"tr\">zıtlık, karşıtlık</td></tr><tr><td><strong>whereas</strong></td><td>a linking word that shows a difference between two clauses</td><td lang=\"tr\">oysa, -iken</td></tr><tr><td><strong>point-by-point</strong></td><td>organising a comparison by discussing A and B together for each point</td><td lang=\"tr\">madde madde (karşılaştırma)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>prediction</strong></td><td>a statement about what will happen in the future</td><td lang=\"tr\">tahmin, öngörü</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-quick-check\"><h2 id=\"quick-check\">Quick check</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"What is the <strong>main idea</strong> of the text?","opts":[{"h":"Science fiction is always wrong","why":"some predictions came true"},{"h":"Science fiction predicted some technology well and some badly, and was better at predicting problems","ok":1,"why":"covers the whole text"},{"h":"Flying cars will arrive next year","why":"not stated"},{"h":"Computers are getting bigger","why":"opposite"}],"why":"The last paragraph gives the conclusion."},{"q":"Which prediction came true <strong>faster</strong> than expected?","opts":[{"h":"Living on the Moon","why":"didn't come true"},{"h":"Flying cars","why":"still experimental"},{"h":"Small, cheap computers and video calls","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 2"},{"h":"Cities in the sky","why":"didn't happen"}],"why":"Detail question."},{"q":"\"The real revolution has been mostly invisible.\" What does the writer mean?","opts":[{"h":"Technology does not exist","why":"no"},{"h":"The biggest changes are data, networks and software that we can't see","ok":1,"why":"paragraph 4"},{"h":"People can become invisible","why":"no"},{"h":"Robots are hidden in houses","why":"no"}],"why":"Read the sentence after \"In contrast\"."},{"q":"Which linking word in the text shows <strong>similarity</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"however","why":"difference"},{"h":"in contrast","why":"difference"},{"h":"similarly","ok":1,"why":"similarity"},{"h":"whereas","why":"difference"}],"why":"<em>Similarly</em> and <em>likewise</em> show similarity."}],[{"q":"\"Real robots are ___ intelligent than film robots.\"","opts":[{"h":"much less more","why":"two comparatives together"},{"h":"far less","ok":1,"why":"intensifier + comparative"},{"h":"less far","why":"wrong word order"},{"h":"more lesser","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>much / far / a lot</em> + comparative."},{"q":"Which sentence shows a <strong>similarity</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"Unlike films, real life is slow.","why":"difference"},{"h":"Films are exciting, whereas reality is not.","why":"difference"},{"h":"Both films and novels imagine the future.","ok":1,"why":"similarity"},{"h":"In contrast, real computers are small.","why":"difference"}],"why":"<em>Both … and</em> shows similarity."},{"q":"In a <strong>point-by-point</strong> paragraph you:","opts":[{"h":"write everything about A, then everything about B","why":"that's the block method"},{"h":"compare A and B together on each point","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"write only about differences","why":"similarities can be included"},{"h":"write only about A","why":"no comparison"}],"why":"Point-by-point = one point at a time, both items."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":[""," films and novels can tell science-fiction stories."],"a":[["both"]],"note":""},{"t":["Film robots can feel emotions, "," real robots cannot."],"a":[["whereas","while"]],"note":""},{"t":[""," film robots, real robots do not usually look like humans."],"a":[["unlike"]],"note":""},{"t":["Real phones are similar to the devices in old sci-fi films "," that they allow video calls."],"a":[["in"]],"note":""},{"t":["Flying cars are "," realistic than electric cars today."],"a":[["less"]],"note":""},{"t":["Video calls are now as common "," phone calls."],"a":[["as"]],"note":""},{"t":["Real space travel is "," more expensive than in films."],"a":[["much","far","a lot"]],"note":""},{"t":[""," Star-Trek-style computers, today's voice assistants answer spoken questions."],"a":[["like"]],"note":""},{"t":["Time travel is still fiction. "," contrast, video calls are part of daily life."],"a":[["in"]],"note":""},{"t":["Self-driving cars exist; ",", they are not yet common in Türkiye."],"a":[["however"]],"note":""},{"t":["Smart watches are good for health. ",", fitness apps help people exercise."],"a":[["similarly","likewise"]],"note":""},{"t":["Real robot dogs are "," (cheap) than they were ten years ago."],"a":[["cheaper"]],"note":""},{"t":["The film's spaceship is far "," (good) than any real one."],"a":[["better"]],"note":""},{"t":["Holograms in films look "," realistic than real holograms."],"a":[["more"]],"note":""},{"t":["A comparison paragraph shows "," and differences."],"a":[["similarities"]],"note":""},{"t":["The ","-by-point method compares A and B one point at a time."],"a":[["point"]],"note":""}]],"id":"en-14","title":"Science fiction vs. reality — writing a comparison paragraph","goal":"plan and write a clear comparison-and-contrast paragraph using comparison language and linking words","week":"15","minutes":22,"terms":[{"t":"comparison","m":"showing how two things are similar","tr":"karşılaştırma"},{"t":"contrast","m":"showing how two things are different","tr":"zıtlık, karşıtlık"},{"t":"whereas","m":"a linking word that shows a difference between two clauses","tr":"oysa, -iken"},{"t":"point-by-point","m":"organising a comparison by discussing A and B together for each point","tr":"madde madde (karşılaştırma)"},{"t":"prediction","m":"a statement about what will happen in the future","tr":"tahmin, öngörü"}]},"en-ready":{"html":"<div class=\"callout callout-note\"><div class=\"callout-h\">Important note about university exemption</div><p>This is <strong>general B2 readiness practice</strong>—it is <strong>not</strong> a copy of İGÜ's official IGUYET exam. At Istanbul Gelisim University, English exemption typically requires <strong>70/100</strong> on the university proficiency exam (or accepted scores such as <strong>YDS 60+</strong> or <strong>TOEFL iBT 72+</strong>). For current dates, rules, and official sample formats, always contact the School of Foreign Languages at <code>ydyo@gelisim.edu.tr</code>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"sec sec-part-1-grammar-15-items\"><h2 id=\"part-1-grammar-15-items\">Part 1: Grammar (15 items)</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-part-2-vocabulary-collocations-10-items\"><h2 id=\"part-2-vocabulary-collocations-10-items\">Part 2: Vocabulary &amp; Collocations (10 items)</h2>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"gaps\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-part-3-reading-comprehension-2-original-passages\"><h2 id=\"part-3-reading-comprehension-2-original-passages\">Part 3: Reading Comprehension (2 original passages)</h2>\n<h3>Passage A: Why Sleep Matters When Learning to Code</h3>\n<blockquote><p>Many first-year university students believe that staying awake until 3:00 a.m. before a coding deadline is a badge of honor. However, neuroscience research consistently shows that sleep is not wasted time—it is the exact moment when the brain organizes new skills. During deep sleep and REM sleep, the brain replays patterns practiced during the day, moving fragile short-term memories into stable long-term storage.</p>\n<p>In one well-known experiment, students learned a complex sequence of finger movements on a keyboard. Those who practiced in the morning and were tested twelve hours later improved only slightly. In contrast, students who practiced in the evening, slept eight hours, and were tested the next morning performed twenty percent faster and made far fewer errors—without any extra practice!</p>\n<p>The same principle applies to debugging C pointers or memorizing network ports. When you stare at a bug at 1:00 a.m., tired eyes miss obvious typos like <code>=</code> instead of <code>==</code>. Going to sleep after forty-five minutes of focused study often lets you spot the mistake in ten seconds the next morning. Consistent daily study paired with seven to eight hours of sleep beats last-minute all-nighters every single time.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"0\"></div>\n<h3>Passage B: The Rise of Micro-Credentials in Tech</h3>\n<blockquote><p>A decade ago, a university diploma was almost the only document employers checked when hiring junior IT staff. Today, while a degree remains essential for deep computer science foundations, companies increasingly look at <strong>micro-credentials</strong>—short, verified certificates in focused skills such as cloud administration, SQL databases, or network security.</p>\n<p>Part of this shift comes from speed. University curricula update carefully over years, whereas software tools evolve every few months. By completing a two-week lab badge or an industry networking certificate alongside their degree, students show employers two strengths at once: solid academic theory and up-to-date hands-on practice.</p>\n<p>Still, hiring managers warn against collecting dozens of certificates without real understanding. A badge only helps if you can explain the concepts in an interview or demonstrate them in a small project. The smartest approach is combining your degree coursework with two or three targeted practical skills—such as Linux basics, Git, and packet analysis—that directly match the career path you want.</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"1\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-part-4-listening-practice-read-or-have-a-partner-read-aloud\"><h2 id=\"part-4-listening-practice-read-or-have-a-partner-read-aloud\">Part 4: Listening Practice (Read or have a partner read aloud)</h2>\n<h3>Listening Transcript 1 — Campus IT Help Desk</h3>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Staff:</strong> Good morning, İGÜ IT Help Desk, how can I help? <strong>Student:</strong> Hi! I'm trying to connect my laptop to the campus Wi-Fi, <code>IGU-Student</code>, but it keeps asking for a certificate and won't load any web pages. <strong>Staff:</strong> Let's check. First, did you enter your student email address or just your student number as the username? <strong>Student:</strong> Just my student number. <strong>Staff:</strong> Ah, that's why! You need your full <code>@ogr.gelisim.edu.tr</code> email address and your portal password. Also, if your clock date is wrong, the security certificate will fail. <strong>Student:</strong> Oh! My laptop battery died yesterday and the clock reset to 2020. Let me fix the date and log in with my full email. Thanks!</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"2\"></div>\n<h3>Listening Transcript 2 — Study Group Announcement</h3>\n<blockquote><p><strong>Coordinator:</strong> Attention everyone—our weekly C and SQL peer-study session is moving from Thursday afternoon to <strong>Wednesday at 14:30</strong> in <strong>Lab 302</strong>. Please bring your own laptop if you have one, though twenty lab PCs are available. Remember, you don't have to finish all exercises before coming, because we'll solve the trickier pointer and <code>JOIN</code> problems together!</p></blockquote>\n<div class=\"widget\" data-widget=\"quiz\" data-k=\"3\"></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-part-5-writing-prompt-150-180-words\"><h2 id=\"part-5-writing-prompt-150-180-words\">Part 5: Writing Prompt (150–180 words)</h2>\n<p>Write a well-structured paragraph or short two-paragraph essay on <strong>one</strong> of these topics:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Should university programming students learn English alongside coding from Semester 1? Why or why not?</strong></li><li><strong>Describe one technology skill you have learned recently and explain how you plan to use it in the future.</strong></li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show model answer (Topic 1)</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>Learning English alongside programming from Semester 1 is essential for every Computer Programming student. First, almost all programming languages, compiler error messages, and official documentation are written in English. When a student can read error messages and technical guides directly, they solve bugs much faster without waiting for translations. In addition, international software teams communicate in English every day through code reviews, emails, and project tickets.</p>\n<p>Although studying both technical subjects and a second language takes consistent effort, small daily habits make it manageable. For example, reading one short technical article a day and writing clear, polite emails in English builds confidence steadily over the semester. Therefore, treating English as a core engineering tool—rather than an extra hurdle—prepares students for both university exams and modern tech careers.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">B2 Writing self-check</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Clear topic sentence in Paragraph 1</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Two or more concrete supporting reasons/examples</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Smooth transitions (<code>First</code>, <code>In addition</code>, <code>Although</code>, <code>For example</code>, <code>Therefore</code>)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Varied grammar (modals, conditionals/passive/present perfect where natural)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>150–180 words, formal/neutral academic tone</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>","quizzes":[[{"q":"According to Passage A, what happens to new skills while you sleep?","opts":[{"h":"The brain erases short-term memories completely","why":"it moves them to long-term storage"},{"h":"The brain replays daytime patterns and stores them in long-term memory","ok":1,"why":"improves speed and accuracy"},{"h":"You need extra midnight practice to improve","why":"the evening group improved without extra practice"},{"h":"Coding errors increase by twenty percent","why":"errors decreased"}],"why":"Sleep consolidates newly practiced patterns into long-term memory."},{"q":"Why do tired students miss simple bugs like <code>=</code> instead of <code>==</code> late at night?","opts":[{"h":"Compilers turn off warnings after midnight","why":"compilers don't care about the clock"},{"h":"Fatigue reduces focus, whereas resting lets the brain spot mistakes quickly the next morning","ok":1,"why":"45 min study + sleep beats all-nighters"},{"h":"Keyboards break at night","why":"unrelated"},{"h":"Sleep removes C syntax from memory","why":"opposite"}],"why":"Restored focus after sleep makes spotting simple typos much faster."}],[{"q":"What is the main advantage of combining a university degree with targeted micro-credentials?","opts":[{"h":"Micro-credentials replace university degrees completely","why":"degree remains essential"},{"h":"They pair strong academic foundations with up-to-date practical hands-on skills","ok":1,"why":"shows both theory and practice"},{"h":"They guarantee a job without an interview","why":"interviews still test real understanding"},{"h":"They take ten years to earn","why":"short focused certificates"}],"why":"Combining degree theory with current hands-on skills makes graduates stand out."},{"q":"What warning do hiring managers give about certificates?","opts":[{"h":"Never earn more than one certificate","why":"2–3 targeted skills are encouraged"},{"h":"Collecting badges without genuine understanding or project proof does not impress interviewers","ok":1,"why":"must be able to explain and apply them"},{"h":"Only Linux certificates are legal","why":"Linux/Git/packets are just examples"},{"h":"Degrees are useless","why":"degrees remain essential"}],"why":"Certificates must be backed by real understanding and projects."}],[{"q":"Why did the student's Wi-Fi login fail? (Choose the <strong>best</strong> complete answer)","opts":[{"h":"The campus Wi-Fi was turned off","why":"other students were connected"},{"h":"The student used only a student number instead of the full email, AND the laptop's clock had reset to 2020","ok":1,"why":"both issues blocked login and certificate check"},{"h":"The laptop had no Wi-Fi card","why":"it saw <code>IGU-Student</code>"},{"h":"The staff member forgot the password","why":"staff diagnosed the two issues"}],"why":"Both the username format (full email) and wrong system clock (2020) caused the failure."}],[{"q":"When and where will the weekly peer-study session take place?","opts":[{"h":"Thursday afternoon in Lab 202","why":"old day and wrong room"},{"h":"Wednesday at 14:30 in Lab 302","ok":1,"why":"new time and room announced"},{"h":"Friday at 14:30 online","why":"in-person in Lab 302"},{"h":"Wednesday at 10:00 in the library","why":"wrong time and place"}],"why":"Moved to Wednesday at 14:30 in Lab 302."}]],"gaps":[[{"t":["While I "," (walk) across campus yesterday, I saw my advisor."],"a":[["was walking"]],"note":"Past Continuous background"},{"t":["By the time the lecture ended, the students "," (take) three pages of notes."],"a":[["had taken"]],"note":"Past Perfect"},{"t":["Could you tell me when "," (the library / close) on Saturdays?"],"a":[["the library closes"]],"note":"indirect question word order"},{"t":["She has been learning C programming "," October."],"a":[["since"]],"note":"starting point takes <code>since</code>"},{"t":["How many SQL challenges "," (you / solve) today?"],"a":[["have you solved"]],"note":"<code>How many</code> = Present Perfect Simple"},{"t":["You "," (mustn't / don't have to) submit a paper copy; the online PDF is enough."],"a":[["don't have to","do not have to"]],"note":"optional, not forbidden"},{"t":["That new monitor looks "," (sharp) than the old classroom projector."],"a":[["sharper"]],"note":"comparative"},{"t":["He waited for "," hour outside the IT support office."],"a":[["an"]],"note":"silent <code>h</code> → vowel sound"},{"t":["That idea sounds "," (brilliant)!"],"a":[["brilliant"]],"note":"<code>sound + adjective</code>"},{"t":["If I "," (have) more free time this weekend, I would build a small game."],"a":[["had"]],"note":"Second Conditional"},{"t":["If you press <code>F5</code> in PowerPoint, the slideshow "," (start) from Slide 1."],"a":[["starts"]],"note":"Zero Conditional"},{"t":["All student passwords must "," (encrypt) before saving to the database."],"a":[["be encrypted"]],"note":"modal passive"},{"t":["The researcher "," paper won the award teaches at İGÜ."],"a":[["whose"]],"note":"possessive relative pronoun"},{"t":[""," (Although / Despite) the heavy rain, everyone arrived on time."],"a":[["Despite"]],"note":"<code>Despite + noun phrase</code>"},{"t":["I forgot my charger; ",", my laptop shut down during the quiz."],"a":[["therefore","consequently","as a result"]],"note":"result transition"}],[{"t":["Please "," attention to the subnet mask before assigning host IPs."],"a":[["pay"]],"note":"<code>pay attention</code>"},{"t":["Always "," a backup before editing <code>seed.sql</code>."],"a":[["make","take"]],"note":"<code>make/take a backup</code>"},{"t":["Engineers often study how animals "," to extreme climates."],"a":[["adapt"]],"note":"<code>adapt to</code>"},{"t":["Many old dial-up modems are now completely "," (out of date)."],"a":[["obsolete"]],"note":"synonym"},{"t":["The bug was "," (very minor)—just one missing comma."],"a":[["trivial"]],"note":"context vocabulary"},{"t":["Scanning a passage means looking quickly for a "," fact or number."],"a":[["specific"]],"note":"reading strategy"},{"t":["Using <code>BCC</code> protects recipients' "," when emailing a large group."],"a":[["privacy"]],"note":"email etiquette"},{"t":["You must "," (double-check) your work before submitting."],"a":[["verify"]],"note":"academic verb"},{"t":["Could you "," me a favor and check my slide contrast?"],"a":[["do"]],"note":"<code>do someone a favor</code>"},{"t":["Good technical writing uses clear "," words like <em>however</em> and <em>therefore</em>."],"a":[["transition","linking"]],"note":"writing term"}]],"id":"en-ready","title":"B2 English Readiness Practice Test","goal":"test your overall B2 grammar, vocabulary, reading, listening, and writing readiness for university exemption or English I mastery","week":"","minutes":60}},"bank":[{"q":"While I ___ home yesterday, it started to rain heavily.","opts":[{"h":"walked","why":"ongoing background action takes Past Continuous"},{"h":"was walking","ok":1,"why":"Past Continuous interrupted by <code>started</code>"},{"h":"had walked","why":"walking was still in progress, not finished earlier"},{"h":"am walking","why":"<code>yesterday</code> requires past tense"}],"why":"Use <code>was walking</code> for an ongoing past background action.","tag":"02"},{"q":"By the time we arrived at the station, the train ___.","opts":[{"h":"left","why":"use Past Perfect to show it happened before we arrived"},{"h":"had already left","ok":1,"why":"<code>had + V3</code> marks the earlier past event"},{"h":"was leaving","why":"<code>by the time</code> with completed departure uses Past Perfect"},{"h":"has left","why":"cannot mix Present Perfect with <code>arrived</code>"}],"why":"Past Perfect (<code>had left</code>) marks an action completed before another past event.","tag":"02"},{"q":"What ___ at 10 p.m. last night when I called you?","opts":[{"h":"did you do","why":"asks about a finished action, not what was in progress at 10 p.m."},{"h":"were you doing","ok":1,"why":"action in progress at a specific past time"},{"h":"had you done","why":"asks before 10 p.m."},{"h":"are you doing","why":"wrong tense for <code>last night</code>"}],"why":"Specific past moment in progress takes Past Continuous (<code>were you doing</code>).","tag":"02"},{"q":"She couldn't enter the lab because she ___ her student ID card at home.","opts":[{"h":"leaves","why":"present tense"},{"h":"was leaving","why":"leaving is not a continuous background here"},{"h":"had left","ok":1,"why":"happened earlier before arriving at the lab"},{"h":"has left","why":"<code>couldn't</code> is past, so use Past Perfect"}],"why":"Earlier past cause takes Past Perfect (<code>had left</code>).","tag":"02"},{"q":"Yesterday, Emre ___ his C file, compiled it, and fixed two warnings.","opts":[{"h":"opened","ok":1,"why":"sequence of completed main events in Past Simple"},{"h":"was opening","why":"sequential completed actions use Past Simple"},{"h":"had opened","why":"same timeline sequence"},{"h":"opens","why":"<code>yesterday</code> needs past"}],"why":"Sequential completed actions in a story use Past Simple.","tag":"02"},{"q":"That lemon cake smells ___!","opts":[{"h":"like delicious","why":"<code>delicious</code> is an adjective, so omit <code>like</code>"},{"h":"delicious","ok":1,"why":"<code>sense verb + adjective</code>"},{"h":"deliciously","why":"sense verbs take adjectives, not adverbs"},{"h":"as delicious","why":"<code>as</code> is wrong here"}],"why":"Use <code>smell + adjective</code> (<code>smells delicious</code>).","tag":"05"},{"q":"Your laptop fan sounds ___ a tiny helicopter!","opts":[{"h":"similar","why":"<code>sounds similar to</code> or <code>sounds like</code>"},{"h":"like","ok":1,"why":"<code>sound like + noun</code>"},{"h":"as","why":"use <code>like</code> before a noun here"},{"h":"happily","why":"adverb"}],"why":"Use <code>sense verb + like + noun</code> (<code>sounds like a helicopter</code>).","tag":"05"},{"q":"Istanbul Gelisim is ___ university in Istanbul, and my brother waited ___ hour for the shuttle.","opts":[{"h":"an / a","why":"<code>university</code> starts with <code>/j/</code> consonant; <code>hour</code> starts with vowel sound"},{"h":"a / an","ok":1,"why":"<code>a university</code> (<code>/juː/</code>), <code>an hour</code> (silent <code>h</code>)"},{"h":"a / a","why":"<code>hour</code> starts with vowel sound → <code>an</code>"},{"h":"an / an","why":"<code>university</code> starts with consonant sound → <code>a</code>"}],"why":"Choose <code>a</code> vs. <code>an</code> by <strong>sound</strong>, not spelling.","tag":"05"},{"q":"___ computers have changed how ___ students study.","opts":[{"h":"The / the","why":"general plural nouns take zero article"},{"h":"Ø / Ø","ok":1,"why":"general plural nouns (<code>Computers... students</code>) take no article"},{"h":"A / a","why":"plural nouns cannot take <code>a</code>"},{"h":"The / a","why":"plural <code>students</code> cannot take <code>a</code>"}],"why":"General plural nouns take zero article (<code>Ø</code>).","tag":"05"},{"q":"I bought a wireless mouse yesterday, and ___ mouse already needs a battery.","opts":[{"h":"a","why":"second mention of a specific item takes <code>the</code>"},{"h":"the","ok":1,"why":"second mention (<code>the mouse</code>)"},{"h":"an","why":"consonant sound and specific"},{"h":"Ø","why":"singular countable noun needs an article"}],"why":"Second mention of a known noun takes <code>the</code>.","tag":"05"},{"q":"You ___ bring your student ID to the final exam; security checks everyone at the door.","opts":[{"h":"don't have to","why":"contradicts mandatory check"},{"h":"must","ok":1,"why":"strict obligation"},{"h":"mustn't","why":"means forbidden"},{"h":"shouldn't","why":"illogical"}],"why":"Mandatory rules use <code>must</code> or <code>have to</code>.","tag":"06"},{"q":"You ___ pay for Cisco Packet Tracer—it is free for students.","opts":[{"h":"mustn't","why":"<code>mustn't</code> means using it for free is forbidden"},{"h":"don't have to","ok":1,"why":"no obligation / unnecessary"},{"h":"must","why":"contradicts \"free\""},{"h":"had to","why":"past obligation"}],"why":"Lack of necessity uses <code>don't have to</code>.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Students ___ share their portal passwords with anyone.","opts":[{"h":"don't have to","why":"implies it's optional"},{"h":"mustn't","ok":1,"why":"strictly prohibited"},{"h":"have to","why":"illogical"},{"h":"might","why":"weak possibility"}],"why":"Use <code>mustn't</code> for strict prohibition.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Yesterday evening, we ___ restart the router three times.","opts":[{"h":"must","why":"<code>must</code> has no past tense"},{"h":"had to","ok":1,"why":"past obligation uses <code>had to</code>"},{"h":"have to","why":"present tense"},{"h":"musted","why":"invalid word"}],"why":"Past obligation is always <code>had to</code>.","tag":"06"},{"q":"Which sentence is the clearest <strong>instruction</strong> in a user manual?","opts":[{"h":"Clicking Save before closing.","why":"<code>-ing</code> is not an imperative"},{"h":"First, click Save before closing the window.","ok":1,"why":"sequence linker + base imperative"},{"h":"You clicking Save now.","why":"missing verb"},{"h":"To click Save please.","why":"unnatural"}],"why":"Manuals use sequence linkers + base imperative verbs.","tag":"06"},{"q":"By 1900, she ___ in the same hospital for thirty years.","opts":[{"h":"worked","why":"past simple doesn't show duration up to a past moment"},{"h":"was working","why":"past continuous doesn't link to an earlier time"},{"h":"had been working","ok":1,"why":"past perfect continuous: duration up to 1900"},{"h":"has been working","why":"present perfect can't be used with <em>By 1900</em>"}],"why":"<em>had been + V-ing</em> shows a long action up to a past moment.","tag":"02"},{"q":"Which phrase shows complete agreement?","opts":[{"h":"I'm not so sure.","why":"doubt / disagreement"},{"h":"I couldn't agree more.","ok":1,"why":"means \"I agree 100%\""},{"h":"I see what you mean, but…","why":"partial agreement"},{"h":"To some extent.","why":"partial agreement"}],"why":"<em>I couldn't agree more</em> = full agreement.","tag":"03"},{"q":"\"Homework is a waste of time.\" Which reply is the most polite disagreement?","opts":[{"h":"That's nonsense.","why":"rude"},{"h":"You're wrong.","why":"too direct"},{"h":"I take your point, but I think it helps us practise.","ok":1,"why":"acknowledges, then disagrees with a reason"},{"h":"No way!","why":"informal and blunt"}],"why":"Acknowledge first, then disagree with a reason.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Choose the correct sentence.","opts":[{"h":"I am agree with your idea.","why":"never <em>am agree</em>"},{"h":"I agree with your idea.","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"I agreeing your idea.","why":"wrong form, missing <em>with</em>"},{"h":"I am agreed you.","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>agree</em> is a normal verb.","tag":"03"},{"q":"\"I agree <strong>up to a point</strong>\" means:","opts":[{"h":"I agree completely","why":"no"},{"h":"I agree with part of it","ok":1,"why":"partial agreement"},{"h":"I don't understand","why":"no"},{"h":"I want to change the topic","why":"no"}],"why":"<em>Up to a point</em> = partly.","tag":"03"},{"q":"Which question asks for someone's opinion?","opts":[{"h":"What's your view on this?","ok":1,"why":"asks for opinion"},{"h":"Where is the library?","why":"asks for information"},{"h":"Can I borrow your pen?","why":"a request"},{"h":"When does the class start?","why":"asks for a time"}],"why":"<em>What's your view / What do you think / How do you feel about…?</em>","tag":"03"},{"q":"Reading quickly to understand the main idea of a text is called:","opts":[{"h":"skimming","ok":1,"why":"reading for gist"},{"h":"scanning","why":"looking for one specific detail"},{"h":"translating","why":"no"},{"h":"dictating","why":"no"}],"why":"Skimming = gist; scanning = detail.","tag":"04"},{"q":"A statement says \"The museum opened in 1950.\" The text never mentions when the museum opened. The answer is:","opts":[{"h":"True","why":"not supported"},{"h":"False","why":"the text doesn't contradict it"},{"h":"Not given","ok":1,"why":"the information is not in the text"},{"h":"Partly true","why":"not an option in this task type"}],"why":"Not given = the text says nothing about it.","tag":"04"},{"q":"An animal that is in danger of disappearing completely is:","opts":[{"h":"domestic","why":"lives with people"},{"h":"endangered","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"stray","why":"has no home"},{"h":"wild","why":"lives in nature, not necessarily in danger"}],"why":"<em>Endangered species</em> = nesli tükenmekte olan tür.","tag":"04"},{"q":"Before you listen to a recording in an exam, you should first:","opts":[{"h":"read the questions and underline key words","ok":1,"why":"you know what to listen for"},{"h":"write the answers","why":"you haven't heard it yet"},{"h":"close your eyes","why":"no"},{"h":"translate every question into Turkish","why":"wastes time"}],"why":"Know what you are listening for.","tag":"04"},{"q":"The question uses the word \"pets\"; the text says \"companion animals\". This shows that exam questions often use:","opts":[{"h":"the same words as the text","why":"often they don't"},{"h":"synonyms","ok":1,"why":"different words with the same meaning"},{"h":"grammar mistakes","why":"no"},{"h":"Turkish words","why":"no"}],"why":"Look for synonyms, not only identical words.","tag":"04"},{"q":"\"This time tomorrow I ___ on the plane to London.\"","opts":[{"h":"will sit","why":"doesn't show \"in progress\" at that time"},{"h":"will be sitting","ok":1,"why":"future continuous"},{"h":"will have sat","why":"means finished"},{"h":"sit","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>This time tomorrow</em> + future continuous.","tag":"07"},{"q":"\"By 2030, I ___ university.\"","opts":[{"h":"will finish","why":"<em>by</em> + future time needs future perfect"},{"h":"will have finished","ok":1,"why":"finished before 2030"},{"h":"am finishing","why":"an arrangement, not \"finished by\""},{"h":"finish","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>by + time</em> → future perfect.","tag":"07"},{"q":"\"Look at that car! It ___ into the wall!\" (you can see it happening)","opts":[{"h":"will crash","why":"prediction without evidence"},{"h":"is going to crash","ok":1,"why":"prediction based on present evidence"},{"h":"will have crashed","why":"wrong"},{"h":"crashes","why":"wrong"}],"why":"Evidence now → <em>going to</em>.","tag":"07"},{"q":"\"I can't come on Friday. I ___ my cousin at the airport at 6.\" (it's arranged)","opts":[{"h":"will meet","why":"less natural for an arrangement"},{"h":"am meeting","ok":1,"why":"present continuous for a fixed arrangement"},{"h":"will have met","why":"wrong meaning"},{"h":"meet","why":"wrong"}],"why":"Diary arrangements → present continuous.","tag":"07"},{"q":"Which phrase introduces <strong>your own</strong> opinion?","opts":[{"h":"I'm not convinced.","why":"a response to someone else"},{"h":"As far as I'm concerned, …","ok":1,"why":"gives your view"},{"h":"Do you really think so?","why":"a question"},{"h":"I'd go along with that.","why":"agreeing with someone else"}],"why":"<em>In my opinion / As far as I'm concerned / If you ask me…</em>","tag":"07"},{"q":"Choose the correct formal sentence.","opts":[{"h":"The man with who she works is Italian.","why":"after a preposition use <em>whom</em>"},{"h":"The man with whom she works is Italian.","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"The man with that she works is Italian.","why":"no <em>that</em> after a preposition"},{"h":"The man whom she works is Italian.","why":"preposition missing"}],"why":"Preposition + <em>whom</em> (people).","tag":"08"},{"q":"\"The village ___ the film was shot is near Antalya.\"","opts":[{"h":"in that","why":"no <em>that</em> after a preposition"},{"h":"in which","ok":1,"why":"correct (= where)"},{"h":"which","why":"preposition missing (or put <em>in</em> at the end)"},{"h":"in whom","why":"<em>whom</em> is for people"}],"why":"<em>in which</em> = <em>where</em> for places.","tag":"08"},{"q":"\"The film has ten characters, most of ___ are teenagers.\"","opts":[{"h":"who","why":"after <em>of</em> use <em>whom</em>"},{"h":"whom","ok":1,"why":"<em>most of whom</em>"},{"h":"them","why":"creates two sentences without a link"},{"h":"which","why":"for things"}],"why":"Quantity + <em>of whom / of which</em>.","tag":"08"},{"q":"What usually comes at the <strong>end</strong> of a film review?","opts":[{"h":"a list of all the actors' birthdays","why":"no"},{"h":"a recommendation (who should watch it) or a star rating","ok":1,"why":"typical ending"},{"h":"the full ending of the story","why":"that would be a spoiler"},{"h":"the price of the cinema ticket","why":"no"}],"why":"Reviews end with an overall opinion and recommendation.","tag":"08"},{"q":"\"The song ___ which the film ends is beautiful.\" Choose the preposition.","opts":[{"h":"with","ok":1,"why":"the film ends <strong>with</strong> a song"},{"h":"on","why":"wrong collocation"},{"h":"at","why":"wrong"},{"h":"for","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>end with</em> → <em>the song with which the film ends</em>.","tag":"08"},{"q":"Choose the passive: \"Workers built the bridge in 1973.\"","opts":[{"h":"The bridge built in 1973.","why":"<em>be</em> is missing"},{"h":"The bridge was built in 1973.","ok":1,"why":"past simple passive"},{"h":"The bridge has built in 1973.","why":"not passive"},{"h":"The bridge was build in 1973.","why":"wrong participle"}],"why":"Past passive: <em>was/were + V3</em>.","tag":"09"},{"q":"\"Plastic bags ___ in many countries since 2015.\"","opts":[{"h":"were banned","why":"<em>since</em> needs present perfect"},{"h":"have been banned","ok":1,"why":"present perfect passive"},{"h":"are banned since","why":"wrong tense with <em>since</em>"},{"h":"banned","why":"not passive"}],"why":"<em>since</em> → present perfect (passive: <em>have been + V3</em>).","tag":"09"},{"q":"\"A new recycling system ___ at the moment.\"","opts":[{"h":"is tested","why":"doesn't show \"now\""},{"h":"is being tested","ok":1,"why":"present continuous passive"},{"h":"has tested","why":"not passive"},{"h":"tests","why":"not passive"}],"why":"<em>at the moment</em> → <em>is being + V3</em>.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which phrase starts the <strong>conclusion</strong> of a talk?","opts":[{"h":"First of all","why":"beginning"},{"h":"Let's move on to","why":"middle"},{"h":"To sum up","ok":1,"why":"conclusion"},{"h":"Today I'd like to talk about","why":"introduction"}],"why":"<em>To sum up / In conclusion</em>.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Which sentence <strong>cannot</strong> be made passive?","opts":[{"h":"They recycle paper.","why":"can: <em>Paper is recycled</em>"},{"h":"Someone stole my bike.","why":"can: <em>My bike was stolen</em>"},{"h":"The accident happened at night.","ok":1,"why":"<em>happen</em> has no object"},{"h":"People speak English here.","why":"can: <em>English is spoken here</em>"}],"why":"Only verbs with an object have a passive.","tag":"09"},{"q":"Choose the correct compound noun.","opts":[{"h":"a ten-minutes walk","why":"the first part stays singular"},{"h":"a ten-minute walk","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"a ten-minute's walk","why":"no apostrophe"},{"h":"a walk ten minutes","why":"wrong order"}],"why":"Number + singular noun before another noun.","tag":"10"},{"q":"\"This is my ___ laptop.\" (it belongs to my brother)","opts":[{"h":"brother's","ok":1,"why":"possessive 's"},{"h":"brothers","why":"plural, not possessive"},{"h":"brothers'","why":"means several brothers"},{"h":"brother","why":"needs 's for possession"}],"why":"Singular owner → <em>'s</em>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"Which is correct for things?","opts":[{"h":"the car's door","why":"we usually say <em>the car door</em>"},{"h":"the car door","ok":1,"why":"compound noun for parts of things"},{"h":"the door of car","why":"article missing"},{"h":"the door's car","why":"wrong meaning"}],"why":"For things, use a compound noun.","tag":"10"},{"q":"Which phrase introduces a <strong>disadvantage</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"One advantage is that…","why":"advantage"},{"h":"A major drawback is that…","ok":1,"why":"disadvantage"},{"h":"Another benefit is…","why":"advantage"},{"h":"Overall, …","why":"conclusion"}],"why":"<em>drawback / disadvantage / On the other hand</em>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"\"The ___ books are on the second floor.\" (books for children)","opts":[{"h":"childrens","why":"apostrophe needed"},{"h":"childrens'","why":"<em>children</em> is already plural"},{"h":"children's","ok":1,"why":"irregular plural + 's"},{"h":"child's","why":"one child only"}],"why":"<em>children's, women's, people's</em>.","tag":"10"},{"q":"\"Did you take the bus?\" \"___, I walked. It's only ten minutes.\"","opts":[{"h":"Actually","ok":1,"why":"corrects the guess"},{"h":"By the way","why":"changes topic"},{"h":"Mind you","why":"adds a limiting point"},{"h":"Speaking of","why":"needs a link word"}],"why":"<em>Actually</em> = in fact (correction).","tag":"11"},{"q":"Which marker <strong>returns</strong> to your point after an interruption?","opts":[{"h":"By the way","why":"new side topic"},{"h":"As I was saying","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"To be honest","why":"gives a true opinion"},{"h":"Speaking of","why":"links a new topic"}],"why":"<em>As I was saying</em>.","tag":"11"},{"q":"\"The hotel was great. ___, the breakfast was disappointing.\"","opts":[{"h":"Speaking of","why":"needs a linked noun"},{"h":"By the way","why":"not a new topic"},{"h":"Mind you","ok":1,"why":"adds a point that limits \"great\""},{"h":"As I was saying","why":"no interruption"}],"why":"<em>Mind you</em> = but remember…","tag":"11"},{"q":"\"Actually\" in English means:","opts":[{"h":"currently / now","why":"a common mistake (Turkish <em>aktüel</em>)"},{"h":"in fact","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"finally","why":"no"},{"h":"immediately","why":"no"}],"why":"<em>Actually</em> = aslında.","tag":"11"},{"q":"\"We missed the ferry, lost our bags, and it rained. ___, we had a great time in the end.\"","opts":[{"h":"Anyway","ok":1,"why":"returns to the main point"},{"h":"Actually","why":"not a correction"},{"h":"Mind you","why":"wrong direction"},{"h":"By the way","why":"not a side topic"}],"why":"<em>Anyway</em> moves past details to the main point.","tag":"11"},{"q":"\"The streets are wet. It ___ in the night.\"","opts":[{"h":"must have rained","ok":1,"why":"strong deduction from evidence"},{"h":"can't have rained","why":"opposite of the evidence"},{"h":"must rain","why":"present, not past"},{"h":"must have rain","why":"needs V3 <em>rained</em>"}],"why":"<em>must have + V3</em>.","tag":"12"},{"q":"\"She ___ the message — her phone was switched off all day.\"","opts":[{"h":"must have read","why":"the evidence says she couldn't"},{"h":"can't have read","ok":1,"why":"strong negative deduction"},{"h":"mustn't have read","why":"not used for deduction"},{"h":"might read","why":"not past"}],"why":"Opposite of <em>must have</em> = <em>can't have</em>.","tag":"12"},{"q":"\"I'm not sure why he's late. He ___ in traffic.\"","opts":[{"h":"must have got stuck","why":"too certain"},{"h":"might have got stuck","ok":1,"why":"possible"},{"h":"can't have got stuck","why":"too certain (negative)"},{"h":"might got stuck","why":"needs <em>have</em>"}],"why":"<em>might/may/could have</em> = possibility.","tag":"12"},{"q":"Which sentence is grammatically correct?","opts":[{"h":"He must have went home.","why":"needs V3 <em>gone</em>"},{"h":"He must of gone home.","why":"write <em>have</em>, not <em>of</em>"},{"h":"He must have gone home.","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"He must had gone home.","why":"wrong"}],"why":"modal + <strong>have</strong> + V3.","tag":"12"},{"q":"\"You ___ me! I waited for an hour.\" (you didn't tell me; criticism)","opts":[{"h":"must have told","why":"deduction, not criticism"},{"h":"could have told","ok":1,"why":"a possible action that didn't happen"},{"h":"can't have told","why":"wrong meaning"},{"h":"might tell","why":"not past"}],"why":"<em>could have + V3</em> = you had the chance but didn't.","tag":"12"},{"q":"\"My phone is broken. I need a new ___.\"","opts":[{"h":"one","ok":1,"why":"replaces \"phone\""},{"h":"it","why":"<em>it</em> = the same broken phone"},{"h":"ones","why":"plural"},{"h":"so","why":"replaces clauses"}],"why":"<em>one</em> = another of the same kind.","tag":"13"},{"q":"\"Will the exam be difficult?\" \"I hope ___.\"","opts":[{"h":"no","why":"wrong form"},{"h":"not","ok":1,"why":"<em>I hope not</em>"},{"h":"so not","why":"wrong"},{"h":"don't","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>hope so / hope not</em>.","tag":"13"},{"q":"\"She said she would call, and she ___.\"","opts":[{"h":"did","ok":1,"why":"replaces \"called\""},{"h":"was","why":"wrong auxiliary"},{"h":"so","why":"wrong"},{"h":"one","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>do/does/did</em> replaces a verb phrase.","tag":"13"},{"q":"A story about a frightening future society where people are controlled is a:","opts":[{"h":"utopia","why":"a perfect society"},{"h":"dystopia","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"biography","why":"a life story"},{"h":"comedy","why":"no"}],"why":"<em>dystopia</em> ≠ <em>utopia</em>.","tag":"13"},{"q":"\"I prefer the red shoes to the black ___.\"","opts":[{"h":"one","why":"plural noun needs <em>ones</em>"},{"h":"ones","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"it","why":"wrong"},{"h":"them ones","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>ones</em> for plural countable nouns.","tag":"13"},{"q":"\"Trains are fast, ___ buses are cheap.\"","opts":[{"h":"whereas","ok":1,"why":"shows contrast between two clauses"},{"h":"similarly","why":"shows similarity"},{"h":"both","why":"wrong position"},{"h":"like","why":"needs a noun"}],"why":"<em>whereas / while</em> + clause.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Choose the correct comparative.","opts":[{"h":"more cheaper","why":"double comparative"},{"h":"much cheaper","ok":1,"why":"intensifier + comparative"},{"h":"most cheaper","why":"wrong"},{"h":"cheaper more","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>much / far / a lot</em> + comparative.","tag":"14"},{"q":"Which linking word shows a <strong>similarity</strong>?","opts":[{"h":"However","why":"difference"},{"h":"In contrast","why":"difference"},{"h":"Likewise","ok":1,"why":"similarity"},{"h":"Unlike","why":"difference"}],"why":"<em>Similarly / Likewise / Like / Both</em>.","tag":"14"},{"q":"In the point-by-point method you:","opts":[{"h":"describe A completely, then B completely","why":"block method"},{"h":"compare A and B on each point, one point at a time","ok":1,"why":"correct"},{"h":"write only about one item","why":"no comparison"},{"h":"write a list of words","why":"no"}],"why":"Point-by-point = both items for each point.","tag":"14"},{"q":"\"Real robots are not ___ intelligent ___ film robots.\"","opts":[{"h":"as … as","ok":1,"why":"<em>not as … as</em> = less"},{"h":"so … that","why":"different structure"},{"h":"more … as","why":"wrong"},{"h":"as … than","why":"wrong"}],"why":"<em>not as + adjective + as</em>.","tag":"14"}],"exercises":[],"challenges":[],"cheat":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-1-narrative-tenses-week-2\"><h2 id=\"1-narrative-tenses-week-2\">1. Narrative tenses (week 2)</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tense</th><th>Form</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Past simple</td><td>V2 / did + V1</td><td>main events in order</td></tr><tr><td>Past continuous</td><td>was/were + V-ing</td><td>background action in progress</td></tr><tr><td>Past perfect</td><td>had + V3</td><td>an earlier past event</td></tr><tr><td>Past perfect continuous</td><td>had been + V-ing</td><td>a long action up to an earlier past moment (<em>for / since</em>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-2-conversation-weeks-3-8-12\"><h2 id=\"2-conversation-weeks-3-8-12\">2. Conversation (weeks 3, 8, 12)</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Agree</th><th>Partly agree</th><th>Disagree politely</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I couldn't agree more. · Exactly.</td><td>I agree up to a point, but… · That's true, but…</td><td>I'm not sure about that. · I see it differently.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Opinions:</strong> In my opinion… · As far as I'm concerned… · If you ask me… · I'm in two minds. · I'm not convinced. <strong>Discourse markers:</strong> <em>by the way</em> (new side topic) · <em>anyway</em> (back to main point) · <em>actually</em> (= in fact, correction — not \"now\") · <em>as I was saying</em> (after an interruption) · <em>speaking of…</em> · <em>mind you</em> (limits the last point) · <em>to be honest</em>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-3-reading-and-listening-week-4\"><h2 id=\"3-reading-and-listening-week-4\">3. Reading and listening (week 4)</h2>\n<p><strong>Gist</strong> = main idea (skim: title, first/last sentences). <strong>Detail</strong> = scan for key words, numbers, names. Questions use <strong>synonyms</strong>. <strong>False</strong> = text says the opposite; <strong>Not given</strong> = text says nothing.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-4-senses-and-articles-week-5\"><h2 id=\"4-senses-and-articles-week-5\">4. Senses and articles (week 5)</h2>\n<p><em>look / sound / feel / smell / taste</em> + <strong>adjective</strong> (<em>sounds great</em>) · + <strong>like + noun</strong> (<em>sounds like a plan</em>). <strong>a</strong> (consonant sound: <em>a university</em>) · <strong>an</strong> (vowel sound: <em>an hour</em>) · <strong>the</strong> (specific, second mention, only one) · <strong>Ø</strong> (general plurals/uncountables: <em>Ø Cats are…</em>).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-5-how-to-web-article-week-6\"><h2 id=\"5-how-to-web-article-week-6\">5. How-to web article (week 6)</h2>\n<p>Catchy title (<em>How to… / 5 ways to…</em>) → short intro → numbered tips with <strong>imperatives</strong> → conclusion. <strong>must</strong> = necessary · <strong>mustn't</strong> = forbidden · <strong>don't have to</strong> = not necessary · <strong>should</strong> = advice.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-6-future-forms-week-8\"><h2 id=\"6-future-forms-week-8\">6. Future forms (week 8)</h2>\n<p><strong>will</strong> (prediction, quick decision) · <strong>going to</strong> (plan, evidence now) · <strong>present continuous</strong> (arrangement) · <strong>will be + V-ing</strong> (in progress at a future time) · <strong>will have + V3</strong> (finished <em>by</em> a future time). After <em>when/if/as soon as</em> use the <strong>present</strong>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-7-relative-clauses-with-prepositions-week-9\"><h2 id=\"7-relative-clauses-with-prepositions-week-9\">7. Relative clauses with prepositions (week 9)</h2>\n<p>Formal: <em>the city <strong>in which</strong> she lives</em> · <em>the man <strong>with whom</strong> I work</em> · <em>ten students, <strong>most of whom</strong>…</em> Never <em>in that</em> / <em>with who</em>. <em>in which</em> = <em>where</em> (place), <em>when</em> (time).</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-8-passive-week-10\"><h2 id=\"8-passive-week-10\">8. Passive (week 10)</h2>\n<p><strong>be + V3</strong>: is made · was invented · has been banned · is being tested · will be recycled · can be reused. No passive for verbs without an object (<em>happen, arrive</em>). Talk signposts: <em>Today I'd like to talk about… · First of all… · Let's move on to… · To sum up…</em></p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-9-compound-nouns-and-possessives-week-11\"><h2 id=\"9-compound-nouns-and-possessives-week-11\">9. Compound nouns and possessives (week 11)</h2>\n<p><em>search engine, password, e-book</em> — first noun <strong>singular</strong> (<em>a shoe shop, a ten-minute video</em>). <strong>'s</strong> for people/time (<em>my sister's laptop, today's news</em>); <strong>s'</strong> for regular plurals (<em>the students' bags</em>); <em>children's, people's</em>. Paragraph: topic sentence → 2 advantages → <em>On the other hand</em> 2 disadvantages → <em>On balance…</em></p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-10-past-modals-of-deduction-week-13\"><h2 id=\"10-past-modals-of-deduction-week-13\">10. Past modals of deduction (week 13)</h2>\n<p><strong>must have + V3</strong> (almost sure it happened) · <strong>might / may / could have + V3</strong> (possible) · <strong>can't / couldn't have + V3</strong> (almost sure it didn't). Never <em>mustn't have</em> for deduction; never <em>must of</em>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-11-substitution-week-14\"><h2 id=\"11-substitution-week-14\">11. Substitution (week 14)</h2>\n<p><strong>one / ones</strong> (countable nouns) · <strong>it / they / this</strong> (things / ideas) · <strong>so / not</strong> after <em>think, hope, be afraid</em> (<em>I think so / I hope not</em>) · <strong>do / did / do so</strong> (verb phrases) · <strong>the same</strong>, <strong>such</strong>.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-12-comparison-paragraph-week-15\"><h2 id=\"12-comparison-paragraph-week-15\">12. Comparison paragraph (week 15)</h2>\n<p>Similar: <em>both…and · like · similarly · likewise · as…as</em>. Different: <em>whereas · while · unlike · however · in contrast · much/far more…than · not as…as</em>. Organise <strong>point by point</strong>: each point compares A and B.</p>\n</section>","title":"English I — Cheat Sheet"},"mocks":{"midterm":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-section-1-grammar-vocabulary-35-pts\"><h2 id=\"section-1-grammar-vocabulary-35-pts\">Section 1: Grammar &amp; Vocabulary (35 pts)</h2>\n<p>Complete each sentence with the best word or verb form:</p>\n<ol><li>While I ___ (walk) to the computer lab, I saw my roommate.</li><li>When we arrived at the classroom, the quiz ___ (already / start).</li><li>By the time the exam started, we ___ (wait) outside for forty minutes.</li><li>Write a polite reply that <strong>partly agrees</strong> with: <em>\"Students should study only at night.\"</em></li><li>She waited for ___ hour outside the library before it opened at ___ university campus.</li><li>Your idea sounds ___ (great), and it sounds ___ a real solution!</li><li>You ___ (mustn't / don't have to) print the code; PDF upload is enough.</li><li>Students ___ (mustn't / don't have to) share exam answers with anyone.</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show Section 1 answers</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li><code>was walking</code> (Past Continuous background interrupted by <code>saw</code>)</li><li><code>had already started</code> (Past Perfect — happened before <code>arrived</code>)</li><li><code>had been waiting</code> (Past Perfect Continuous — duration up to an earlier past moment)</li><li>e.g. <em>\"I agree up to a point — it's quiet at night — but we also need sleep before morning classes.\"</em></li><li><code>an</code> hour (silent <code>h</code>), <code>a</code> university (<code>/juː/</code> consonant sound)</li><li><code>great</code> (<code>sound + adj</code>), <code>like</code> (<code>sound like + noun</code>)</li><li><code>don't have to</code> (optional / not required)</li><li><code>mustn't</code> (strictly forbidden)</li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-section-2-reading-comprehension-30-pts\"><h2 id=\"section-2-reading-comprehension-30-pts\">Section 2: Reading Comprehension (30 pts)</h2>\n<p>Read this short passage (~180 words) and answer the questions below:</p>\n<blockquote><p>When first-year students start writing technical reports in English, they often try to impress instructors with long, complicated sentences. In practice, clear engineering writing works best when sentences stay short and direct. Before submitting any document, writers should check three things: structure, verb tense, and tone.</p>\n<p>First, organize every paragraph around one main idea, starting with a clear topic sentence. Next, keep tenses consistent—for example, use Past Simple when describing what you tested yesterday in the lab, and Present Simple when explaining how a system always works. Finally, avoid casual slang in university emails and reports. Following these three simple habits makes your ideas easy to follow, even when explaining complex code or network diagrams.</p></blockquote>\n<ol><li>What mistake do many first-year students make in technical reports?</li><li>Which tense should you use when describing a test you ran yesterday vs. how a system always works?</li><li>What three things should writers check before submitting?</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show Section 2 model answers</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li>They try to use overly long, complicated sentences instead of short, direct ones.</li><li>Use <strong>Past Simple</strong> for yesterday's test and <strong>Present Simple</strong> for how a system always works.</li><li>Structure, verb tense, and tone.</li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-section-3-guided-writing-35-pts\"><h2 id=\"section-3-guided-writing-35-pts\">Section 3: Guided Writing (35 pts)</h2>\n<p>Write a <strong>100–120 word</strong> how-to paragraph or short story about <strong>how to prepare for a university computer lab exam</strong>. Include <strong>narrative tenses</strong> OR <strong>sequence linkers + modals</strong> (<code>First, Next, must, don't have to, should</code>).</p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show Section 3 model answer</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>Preparing for a practical computer lab exam is easy if you follow a clear routine. First, you should review your past lab exercises two days before the test so you don't panic at the last minute. Next, practice writing small programs or SQL queries without looking at the answers. During the exam, you mustn't use unauthorized websites or copy code from neighbors. However, you don't have to memorize every rare command if you understand the core logic. Finally, save your work every ten minutes so a sudden crash never costs you points.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Midterm writing checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Clear topic sentence and logical order (100–120 words)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Uses sequence linkers (<code>First, Next, Finally</code>) and modals (<code>should, mustn't, don't have to</code>) accurately</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Minimal grammar/spelling errors</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>","title":"English I — Midterm Mock Exam (weeks 1–6)","minutes":60},"final":{"html":"<section class=\"sec sec-section-1-grammar-40-pts-4-points-each\"><h2 id=\"section-1-grammar-40-pts-4-points-each\">Section 1: Grammar (40 pts — 4 points each)</h2>\n<ol><li>Complete with the correct future form: <em>\"Don't call at 8 p.m. — I ___ (watch) the match.\"</em></li><li>Complete: <em>\"By the end of this year, I ___ (save) enough money for a laptop.\"</em></li><li>Make one formal sentence with a <strong>preposition + which/whom</strong>: <em>\"This is the lab. We work in the lab every Tuesday.\"</em></li><li>Rewrite in the <strong>passive</strong>: <em>\"Volunteers have cleaned the beach.\"</em></li><li>Rewrite in the <strong>passive</strong>: <em>\"They are building a new library.\"</em></li><li>Correct the mistake: <em>\"I bought a ten-minutes video course from a shoes shop's website.\"</em></li><li>Write the possessive: <em>the opinions of the people</em> → ___</li><li>Complete with a <strong>past modal</strong>: <em>\"His car is still outside. He ___ (not / leave) yet.\"</em> (you are almost sure)</li><li>Complete with a substitute word: <em>\"Is the library open on Sunday?\" \"I don't think ___.\"</em></li><li>Complete: <em>\"Buses are cheap, ___ taxis are expensive.\"</em> (contrast)</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show Section 1 answers</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li><code>will be watching</code> (future continuous — in progress at 8 p.m.)</li><li><code>will have saved</code> (future perfect with <em>by</em>)</li><li><em>This is the lab <strong>in which</strong> we work every Tuesday.</em> (or <em>…the lab where we work…</em>)</li><li><em>The beach <strong>has been cleaned</strong> (by volunteers).</em></li><li><em>A new library <strong>is being built</strong>.</em></li><li><em>I bought a <strong>ten-minute</strong> video course from a <strong>shoe shop's</strong> website.</em></li><li><em>the people's opinions</em></li><li><code>can't have left</code> (strong negative deduction)</li><li><code>so</code></li><li><code>whereas</code> / <code>while</code></li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-section-2-conversation-skills-15-pts\"><h2 id=\"section-2-conversation-skills-15-pts\">Section 2: Conversation skills (15 pts)</h2>\n<p>Complete the dialogue with suitable phrases (agreeing/disagreeing, discourse markers).</p>\n<blockquote><p><strong>A:</strong> I think the university should close the library at 6 p.m. to save energy. <strong>B:</strong> (1) ___ — saving energy is important, but many students study in the evenings. <strong>A:</strong> (2) ___, a lot of people only use it to charge their phones. (3) ___, did you hear that the cafeteria is closing too? <strong>B:</strong> Really? (4) ___ the cafeteria, the food there is terrible anyway! (5) ___, back to the library — maybe it could stay open but use fewer lights.</p></blockquote>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show Section 2 model answers</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li><em>I agree up to a point</em> / <em>That's true, but…</em> (partial agreement)</li><li><em>Actually</em> / <em>To be honest</em> (adding surprising information)</li><li><em>By the way</em> (new side topic)</li><li><em>Speaking of</em> (linking to a word just mentioned)</li><li><em>Anyway</em> (returning to the main topic)</li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-section-3-reading-20-pts\"><h2 id=\"section-3-reading-20-pts\">Section 3: Reading (20 pts)</h2>\n<blockquote><p>Every few years, a new technology promises to \"change education forever\". In the 1920s it was radio; in the 1960s, television; in the 2010s, free online courses. Each time, experts predicted that teachers would soon be unnecessary. Each time, they were wrong — at least partly.</p>\n<p>It is easy to see why the predictions failed. All of these technologies were good at <strong>delivering</strong> information, but learning is not only receiving information. Students need to practise, make mistakes, get feedback and stay motivated. A recorded lecture cannot notice that you look confused. A teacher can.</p>\n<p>However, it would be a mistake to say that the technologies changed nothing. Radio brought lessons to farms that had no school nearby. Online courses have allowed millions of people to learn skills that were not taught in their country. The real change was not that teachers disappeared, but that education became available in more places.</p>\n<p>Today, artificial intelligence is the newest promise. Some people believe it will replace teachers; others fear it will help students cheat. History suggests a third answer is more likely: AI will become one more tool, useful in the hands of a good teacher and a motivated student, and much less useful without them.</p></blockquote>\n<ol><li>What is the <strong>main idea</strong> of the text? (gist)</li><li>According to paragraph 2, why did the predictions fail? Give <strong>two</strong> things students need.</li><li>What positive change did radio bring?</li><li>Is this statement True, False or Not given? <em>\"Online courses are more popular than university courses.\"</em></li><li>Find a <strong>passive</strong> verb in paragraph 3.</li></ol>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show Section 3 answers</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><ol><li>New technologies have repeatedly been expected to replace teachers; they didn't, but they did make education available to more people — and AI will probably be another tool, not a replacement.</li><li>Learning needs more than information: students need practice, mistakes, feedback and motivation (any two).</li><li>It brought lessons to farms with no nearby school.</li><li><strong>Not given</strong> — the text does not compare their popularity.</li><li><em>were not taught</em> (past simple passive).</li></ol></div></details>\n</section>\n<section class=\"sec sec-section-4-writing-25-pts\"><h2 id=\"section-4-writing-25-pts\">Section 4: Writing (25 pts)</h2>\n<p>Choose <strong>one</strong> (150–180 words):</p>\n<p><strong>A.</strong> A <strong>comparison paragraph</strong>: <em>\"Learning in a classroom vs. learning online.\"</em> (point-by-point, one similarity and two differences)</p>\n<p><strong>B.</strong> A <strong>film or series review</strong> using at least two relative clauses with prepositions.</p>\n<p><strong>C.</strong> An <strong>advantages-and-disadvantages paragraph</strong>: <em>\"Using AI tools for homework.\"</em></p>\n<details class=\"reveal\"><summary>Show a model answer (A)</summary><div class=\"reveal-b\"><p>Learning in a classroom and learning online share the same goal, but they are different in flexibility and in contact with teachers. Both methods can use the same materials: slides, readings and practice exercises are often identical. However, online learning is far more flexible than classroom learning. Students can watch a lecture at midnight or pause it to take notes, whereas classroom lessons happen at a fixed time and speed. On the other hand, the classroom offers much better contact with teachers and classmates. Unlike an online video, a teacher can see that a student is confused and explain the idea again in a different way. Group discussions and lab work are also easier when everyone is in the same room. In short, online learning is more convenient, but classroom learning is usually more effective for difficult subjects, so a mix of the two is often the best choice.</p></div></details>\n<div class=\"widget rubric\" data-widget=\"rubric\"><div class=\"checklist-h\">Final exam marking checklist</div><ul class=\"checks\"><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Section 1: at least 8 of 10 grammar answers correct</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Section 2: all five gaps filled with an appropriate phrase</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Section 3: main idea stated in your own words; \"Not given\" identified</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Section 4: clear topic sentence, organised structure, linking words</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Section 4: at least three grammar points from the course used correctly (passive, relative clauses, future forms, modals, comparatives)</span></label></li><li><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Section 4: 150–180 words, appropriate formal tone</span></label></li></ul><div class=\"rubric-score\"></div></div>\n</section>","title":"English I — Final Mock Exam (all units)","minutes":75}},"extra":{}});
